# Atomic Classes and CAS

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 9 | Priority: MUST | Since: Java 5 (LongAdder Java 8, VarHandle Java 9)**

> **30-second interview answer:** Atomic classes such as `AtomicInteger`, `AtomicLong` and `AtomicReference` give lock-free, thread-safe updates of a single variable. They are built on CAS (compare-and-swap), a CPU instruction that sets a value only if it still equals the expected old value; if another thread changed it first, the operation retries. That makes `incrementAndGet` atomic without blocking. For heavily contended counters `LongAdder` is faster because it spreads updates over several cells, and `AtomicStampedReference` solves the ABA problem.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Editing a shared online spreadsheet cell with "only save if unchanged".

| Spreadsheet | CAS |
|---|---|
| You read the cell: it says 5 | Read the current value (expected = 5) |
| You compute your new value: 6 | Compute the update |
| You click save with the rule "only if it still says 5" | `compareAndSet(5, 6)` |
| Someone else saved 7 meanwhile, so your save is refused | CAS fails, returns false |
| You re-read (7), recompute (8) and save again | The retry loop |
| Nobody ever locks the cell for editing | Lock-free: no thread blocks |
| The cell changed 5 to 9 and back to 5 while you were away; your save succeeds even though things happened | The ABA problem |

### What it is
- **CAS (compare-and-swap, also compare-and-set):** an atomic hardware instruction (`LOCK CMPXCHG` on x86, LL/SC or `CAS` on ARM) taking a memory location, an expected value and a new value. It writes the new value only if the location holds the expected value, and reports whether it did.
- **Lock-free:** at least one thread always makes progress; threads never block waiting for a lock holder that might be descheduled.
- **Atomic classes** (`java.util.concurrent.atomic`): `AtomicInteger`, `AtomicLong`, `AtomicBoolean`, `AtomicReference<V>`, array versions (`AtomicIntegerArray`), field updaters, `AtomicStampedReference`, `AtomicMarkableReference`, and the striped `LongAdder`, `LongAccumulator`, `DoubleAdder`.
- **Key methods:** `get`, `set`, `incrementAndGet`, `getAndIncrement`, `addAndGet`, `compareAndSet`, `updateAndGet(fn)`, `accumulateAndGet(x, fn)` (Java 8). Values are stored in a `volatile` field, so reads and writes also have volatile visibility.
- **ABA problem:** a CAS succeeds because the value is back to what was expected, even though it changed in between, which matters when the value is a pointer into a structure that was modified.
- **`LongAdder`:** keeps a base value plus an array of cells; contending threads update different cells, and `sum()` adds them up.

### Why it exists
Locks are heavy for tiny updates: a thread that loses the race is parked by the OS and must be woken later, which costs microseconds. A counter incremented millions of times per second by many threads would spend most of its time on lock handoffs. CAS keeps every thread running and retries in nanoseconds, which is why `ConcurrentHashMap`, `AQS` (the base of `ReentrantLock`) and thread pools are all built on it.

## 2. How It Works
**`incrementAndGet` is a CAS loop** (conceptually; the JDK uses an intrinsic `getAndAdd` that maps to a single `LOCK XADD` instruction on x86):

```java
int incrementAndGet() {
    while (true) {
        int current = get();                 // volatile read
        int next = current + 1;
        if (compareAndSet(current, next)) {  // atomic: only if nobody changed it
            return next;
        }
        // another thread won: loop and retry with the fresh value
    }
}
```

```text
time   Thread A                         Thread B                      value
 t1    read 5                                                         5
 t2                                     read 5                        5
 t3    CAS(5 -> 6) succeeds                                           6
 t4                                     CAS(5 -> 6) FAILS (is 6)      6
 t5                                     read 6, CAS(6 -> 7) succeeds  7   <- nothing lost
```

**`updateAndGet` and `accumulateAndGet`** run that loop for you with your function. The function may be called several times if there is contention, so it **must be side-effect free** (no logging, no I/O, no counting).

**ABA:**

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant T1 as "Thread 1"
    participant V as "top of stack"
    participant T2 as "Thread 2"
    T1->>V: "read top = A (plans CAS A to A.next)"
    T2->>V: "pop A, pop B, push A back"
    T1->>V: "CAS(A, A.next) succeeds"
    Note over T1,V: "but A.next is now stale (B was removed), so the stack is corrupted"
```

`AtomicStampedReference` pairs the reference with an `int` stamp (a version number) and CASes both together, so A at version 0 and A at version 2 are different. In Java, garbage collection already prevents the most dangerous form (memory reuse) for plain object references, so ABA mostly matters for lock-free data structures and for values like counters or states that can legitimately cycle back.

**`LongAdder` under contention:**

```text
AtomicLong                         LongAdder
  one value, all threads CAS it      base + cells[] (one per contending thread, roughly)
  T1 T2 T3 T4 --> [ value ]          T1 --> cell[0]   T2 --> cell[1]
  many CAS failures and retries      T3 --> cell[2]   T4 --> base
                                     sum() = base + all cells (not an atomic snapshot)
```

`LongAdder` is much faster for write-heavy counters (metrics, statistics) but `sum()` is not a point-in-time snapshot and there is no `compareAndSet`. Use `AtomicLong` when you need the exact current value for decisions, such as generating ids.

**Limits of CAS:** under very high contention CAS loops spin and waste CPU; and CAS works on one variable. Invariants across several variables need a lock, or one `AtomicReference` to an immutable object holding all of them.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicStampedReference;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public class AtomicDemo {

    record Stats(int orders, long revenueCents) { }

    // A hand-written CAS loop: add credits but never exceed a cap
    static int addCapped(AtomicInteger value, int delta, int cap) {
        while (true) {
            int current = value.get();
            int next = Math.min(current + delta, cap);
            if (value.compareAndSet(current, next)) {
                return next;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();
        LongAdder adder = new LongAdder();
        AtomicInteger max = new AtomicInteger(Integer.MIN_VALUE);
        AtomicReference<Stats> stats = new AtomicReference<>(new Stats(0, 0));

        // 1. Four threads update shared state without any lock
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4)) {
            for (int t = 0; t < 4; t++) {
                final int highest = t * 1000 + 999;
                pool.submit(() -> {
                    for (int i = 0; i < 250_000; i++) {
                        counter.incrementAndGet();
                        adder.increment();
                    }
                    max.accumulateAndGet(highest, Math::max);
                    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                        // immutable snapshot replaced atomically: both fields always consistent
                        stats.updateAndGet(s -> new Stats(s.orders() + 1, s.revenueCents() + 250));
                    }
                });
            }
        }
        System.out.println("AtomicInteger: " + counter.get());
        System.out.println("LongAdder: " + adder.sum());
        System.out.println("max seen: " + max.get());
        System.out.println("stats: " + stats.get());

        // 2. compareAndSet succeeds only when the expected value matches
        AtomicInteger seats = new AtomicInteger(5);
        System.out.println("CAS 5->6: " + seats.compareAndSet(5, 6));
        System.out.println("CAS 5->7: " + seats.compareAndSet(5, 7) + ", value=" + seats.get());

        // 3. A custom CAS loop
        AtomicInteger credits = new AtomicInteger(40);
        System.out.println("capped add: " + addCapped(credits, 30, 50));

        // 4. ABA: a stamp detects the A -> B -> A change
        AtomicStampedReference<String> top = new AtomicStampedReference<>("A", 0);
        int seenStamp = top.getStamp();                 // thread 1 reads A at stamp 0
        top.compareAndSet("A", "B", 0, 1);              // thread 2: A -> B
        top.compareAndSet("B", "A", 1, 2);              // thread 2: B -> A
        boolean ok = top.compareAndSet("A", "C", seenStamp, seenStamp + 1);
        System.out.println("stamped CAS after A->B->A: " + ok
                + ", value=" + top.getReference() + ", stamp=" + top.getStamp());
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `counter.incrementAndGet()` and `adder.increment()` are each called 1,000,000 times in total across four threads; neither loses an update.
- `max.accumulateAndGet(highest, Math::max)` is a lock-free "keep the maximum". The four threads offer 999, 1999, 2999 and 3999, and the result is 3999 whatever the order.
- `stats.updateAndGet` replaces an immutable `Stats` record with a new one through a CAS loop. Because both fields live in one object, readers can never see the order count from one update and the revenue from another. The lambda is pure, as it must be, since it can run more than once.
- `compareAndSet(5, 6)` succeeds; the second `compareAndSet(5, 7)` fails because the value is now 6, and nothing changes.
- `addCapped` shows the CAS loop pattern for logic the built-in methods do not cover (here `updateAndGet(v -> Math.min(v + 30, 50))` would also work).
- The stamped reference simulates ABA in one thread for a deterministic demo: the value is back to `"A"`, but the stamp moved from 0 to 2, so thread 1's CAS with stamp 0 correctly fails.

### Output
```text
AtomicInteger: 1000000
LongAdder: 1000000
max seen: 3999
stats: Stats[orders=4000, revenueCents=1000000]
CAS 5->6: true
CAS 5->7: false, value=6
capped add: 50
stamped CAS after A->B->A: false, value=A, stamp=2
```

### Compare
| Counter implementation | Correct | Blocking | Contention behaviour |
|---|---|---|---|
| `int` with `count++` | No | No | Loses updates |
| `volatile int` with `count++` | No | No | Loses updates |
| `synchronized` increment | Yes | Yes | Threads park and wake; slow under contention |
| `AtomicInteger` | Yes | No | CAS retries; fine for moderate contention |
| `LongAdder` | Yes (sum is approximate while updating) | No | Scales best for write-heavy counters |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is CAS?**
   Compare-and-swap: an atomic CPU instruction that updates a memory location to a new value only if it currently holds an expected value, returning whether it succeeded. It lets threads update shared data without locks by retrying on failure.
2. **How does `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet` work?**
   It reads the current value, computes value plus 1 and CASes it in; if another thread changed the value first, the CAS fails and it retries with the fresh value. Modern JDKs compile it to a single atomic add instruction where the hardware supports it.
3. **Why use atomic classes instead of `synchronized`?**
   For single-variable updates they are faster and never block: a thread that loses a race retries immediately instead of being parked by the OS. They also cannot deadlock.

**Follow-up**
4. **What is the ABA problem and how do you solve it?**
   A thread reads value A, another thread changes it to B and back to A, and the first thread's CAS succeeds as if nothing happened, although the state it reasoned about changed. Fix it with a version stamp: `AtomicStampedReference` CASes the reference and an integer stamp together.
5. **When would you use `LongAdder` instead of `AtomicLong`?**
   For counters that many threads update frequently but read rarely, such as metrics and request counts. `LongAdder` spreads updates across cells to avoid CAS contention. Use `AtomicLong` when you need the exact current value for logic, such as id generation or `compareAndSet`.
6. **Why must the function passed to `updateAndGet` be side-effect free?**
   Under contention the CAS can fail and the function is re-run with the new value, so side effects (logging, sending a message, incrementing something else) could happen several times.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **Is `if (counter.get() < 100) counter.incrementAndGet();` thread-safe?**
   No. Each call is atomic, but the check and the increment are separate, so several threads can pass the check at 99 and overshoot the limit. Use a CAS loop or `updateAndGet(v -> v < 100 ? v + 1 : v)` and compare the result.
8. **We need to update a balance and a transaction count together without locks. How?**
   Put both in an immutable record and keep an `AtomicReference` to it; update with `updateAndGet(s -> new State(s.balance() + x, s.count() + 1))`. Every reader sees a consistent pair. If updates are frequent and heavily contended, a lock may actually be faster than many failed CAS retries creating garbage.
9. **Are atomic classes always faster than locks?**
   No. Under extreme contention, CAS loops spin and burn CPU, and every failed attempt wastes work; a lock that parks threads, or `LongAdder` striping, can perform better. Measure with realistic thread counts.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you understand optimistic concurrency (read, compute, CAS, retry), its pitfalls (ABA, compound checks, side effects in update functions) and when to pick `LongAdder` or a lock instead.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `get()` then `set()` on an atomic | Lost updates, like a plain field | `incrementAndGet`, `updateAndGet`, CAS loop |
| Check with `get()`, then update separately | Limits overshot | One CAS loop or `updateAndGet` containing the check |
| Side effects inside `updateAndGet` | Executed several times under contention | Keep the function pure; act on the returned value |
| Several atomics that must change together | Readers see inconsistent combinations | One `AtomicReference` to an immutable object, or a lock |
| `AtomicLong` for a hot metrics counter | CAS contention and slow throughput | `LongAdder` |
| Using `LongAdder.sum()` for exact decisions | Value can be off while updates are in flight | `AtomicLong` for decisions |

### Best practices
- Use atomics for counters, sequence numbers, flags with compare-and-set (`AtomicBoolean.compareAndSet(false, true)` for "run once"), and immutable-state holders.
- Prefer `LongAdder` for statistics; in Spring Boot, use Micrometer counters, which are built on such primitives.
- Reach for `VarHandle` (Java 9) only when writing low-level libraries; application code should use the atomic classes.
- Remember atomics give atomicity of one variable only; for multi-step invariants use a lock or immutable snapshots.

### Compare with
| Class | Holds | Best for | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| AtomicInteger, AtomicLong | A number | Counters, ids, CAS logic | `incrementAndGet`, `compareAndSet` |
| AtomicBoolean | A flag | Run-once guards | `compareAndSet(false, true)` |
| AtomicReference | An object reference | Immutable state snapshots | `updateAndGet` with records |
| AtomicStampedReference | Reference plus int stamp | ABA-safe lock-free structures | CASes both together |
| LongAdder, LongAccumulator | Striped number | Write-heavy counters, max or min | No CAS; `sum()` not a snapshot |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
OrderHub needs a `FlashSaleLimiter` that lets at most 100 buyers claim a discounted item, using no locks. Acceptance criteria:
- `boolean tryClaim()` uses a single `AtomicInteger` with a CAS loop (or `updateAndGet` plus a comparison) so the limit is never exceeded.
- 1000 claims from 16 threads result in exactly 100 successes and a final count of 100.
- An `AtomicBoolean` makes a "sold out" announcement print exactly once.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class FlashSaleLimiter {
    private final int limit;
    private final AtomicInteger claimed = new AtomicInteger();
    private final AtomicBoolean soldOutAnnounced = new AtomicBoolean();

    FlashSaleLimiter(int limit) { this.limit = limit; }

    boolean tryClaim() {
        while (true) {
            int current = claimed.get();
            if (current >= limit) {
                if (soldOutAnnounced.compareAndSet(false, true)) {
                    System.out.println("sold out");     // exactly one thread wins this CAS
                }
                return false;
            }
            if (claimed.compareAndSet(current, current + 1)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FlashSaleLimiter limiter = new FlashSaleLimiter(100);
        AtomicInteger successes = new AtomicInteger();
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16)) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                pool.submit(() -> { if (limiter.tryClaim()) successes.incrementAndGet(); });
            }
        }
        System.out.println("successes=" + successes.get() + " claimed=" + limiter.claimed.get());
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
sold out
successes=100 claimed=100
```
The check (`current >= limit`) and the increment are tied together by the CAS on `current`: if anyone changed the count in between, the CAS fails and the check runs again.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What does a CAS (compare-and-swap) operation do?
A: It atomically sets a variable to a new value only if it still equals an expected value, and reports whether it succeeded.

Q: How do atomic classes stay thread-safe without locks?
A: They use CAS in a retry loop on a volatile field: read, compute, CAS, and retry if another thread changed the value.

Q: What is the ABA problem in CAS-based code?
A: A value changes from A to B and back to A, so a CAS expecting A succeeds even though the state changed; a version stamp (`AtomicStampedReference`) detects it.

Q: When is LongAdder better than AtomicLong?
A: For counters updated by many threads and read rarely, because it spreads updates across cells and avoids CAS contention.

Q: Why must functions passed to `AtomicReference.updateAndGet` have no side effects?
A: They may be executed several times when the CAS fails under contention.

**Related:** [[volatile and Visibility]] · [[Race Conditions and synchronized]] · [[ConcurrentHashMap Internals]] · [[Designing Thread-Safe Classes]]

# ExecutorService and Thread Pools

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 9 | Priority: MUST | Since: Java 5 (AutoCloseable Java 19, virtual-thread executor Java 21)**

> **30-second interview answer:** An `ExecutorService` decouples submitting tasks from running them: you hand it `Runnable`s or `Callable`s and a pool of reusable threads executes them. The workhorse is `ThreadPoolExecutor`, configured with a core size, a maximum size, a keep-alive time, a work queue, a thread factory and a rejection policy. A new task starts a thread if fewer than core threads exist, otherwise it is queued, and only when the queue is full does the pool grow to the maximum, after which tasks are rejected. In production I avoid the unbounded `Executors` factory methods, size pools by workload (about the number of cores for CPU-bound work, more for I/O-bound), name the threads and always shut pools down.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A bank branch with tellers and a waiting area.

| Bank branch | ThreadPoolExecutor |
|---|---|
| Permanent tellers always on shift | `corePoolSize` threads |
| Chairs in the waiting area | The work queue and its capacity |
| Temporary tellers called in only when every chair is taken | Threads above core, up to `maximumPoolSize` |
| Temps go home after being idle for a while | `keepAliveTime` |
| The doorman turning customers away when tellers are busy and chairs are full | `RejectedExecutionHandler` |
| Name badges on tellers | A `ThreadFactory` that names threads |
| "We close at 5: finish the customers inside, let nobody new in" | `shutdown()` |
| "Fire alarm: everybody out now" | `shutdownNow()` |

### What it is
- **`Executor`:** one method, `execute(Runnable)`.
- **`ExecutorService`:** adds lifecycle (`shutdown`, `shutdownNow`, `awaitTermination`, `close` in Java 19) and results (`submit`, `invokeAll`, `invokeAny`).
- **`ThreadPoolExecutor` parameters:**

| Parameter | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `corePoolSize` | Threads kept alive even when idle (unless `allowCoreThreadTimeOut`) |
| `maximumPoolSize` | Upper bound on threads; extra threads are only created when the queue is full |
| `keepAliveTime` and unit | How long threads above core may idle before ending |
| `workQueue` | A `BlockingQueue<Runnable>` holding tasks waiting for a thread |
| `threadFactory` | Creates threads (names, daemon flag, uncaught-exception handler) |
| `handler` | What to do when the pool is saturated (rejection policy) |

- **Rejection policies:** `AbortPolicy` (default, throws `RejectedExecutionException`), `CallerRunsPolicy` (the submitting thread runs the task itself, which naturally slows producers: back-pressure), `DiscardPolicy` (silently drop), `DiscardOldestPolicy` (drop the oldest queued task and retry).
- **`ScheduledExecutorService`:** runs tasks after a delay or periodically (`schedule`, `scheduleAtFixedRate`, `scheduleWithFixedDelay`).
- **Virtual-thread executor (Java 21):** `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` starts a new, cheap virtual thread per task, with no pool; ideal for blocking I/O.

### Why it exists
Creating an OS thread per task is slow (tens to hundreds of microseconds and about 1 MB of stack) and unbounded: a traffic spike creates thousands of threads and the JVM dies. Pools reuse a bounded set of threads, queue the excess, give a clear policy for overload and a single place to shut everything down.

## 2. How It Works
**The `execute` decision (the most asked internal):**

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["execute(task)"] --> B{"running threads < corePoolSize?"}
    B -- "yes" --> C["start a new core thread with this task"]
    B -- "no" --> D{"queue.offer(task) succeeds?"}
    D -- "yes" --> E["task waits in the queue"]
    D -- "no, queue full" --> F{"running threads < maximumPoolSize?"}
    F -- "yes" --> G["start a non-core thread with this task"]
    F -- "no" --> H["rejection policy"]
```

Note the counter-intuitive order: **the queue fills before the pool grows past core.** With an unbounded queue the pool never grows past core, so `maximumPoolSize` is ignored.

**Worker loop:** each worker thread runs its first task, then loops on `queue.take()` (or `poll(keepAlive)` for non-core threads, which end when the poll times out). Exceptions from `execute` tasks kill the worker, which is replaced; `submit` tasks capture exceptions in their `Future`.

**The `Executors` factory traps:**

| Factory | Real configuration | Production risk |
|---|---|---|
| `newFixedThreadPool(n)` | core = max = n, unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` | Queue grows without limit: memory exhaustion, unbounded latency |
| `newSingleThreadExecutor()` | 1 thread, unbounded queue | Same as above |
| `newCachedThreadPool()` | core 0, max `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, `SynchronousQueue`, 60 s keep-alive | Unbounded threads under load |
| `newScheduledThreadPool(n)` | n core threads, unbounded delay queue | Unbounded queue |
| `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` | A virtual thread per task | Unbounded concurrency: limit access to scarce resources with a `Semaphore` |

For services, build a `ThreadPoolExecutor` explicitly (or Spring's `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor`) with a bounded queue and a deliberate rejection policy.

**Sizing rule of thumb (Brian Goetz):**

```text
CPU-bound work:  threads ≈ number of cores (+1)
I/O-bound work:  threads ≈ cores x (1 + wait time / compute time)
                 e.g. 8 cores, tasks wait 90 ms on a DB and compute 10 ms -> 8 x (1 + 9) = 80
Then measure: queue length, active threads, task latency, rejections.
```

Also bound pools by downstream capacity: 200 threads calling a database with a pool of 20 connections just means 180 threads waiting for connections.

**Shutdown:**

```text
shutdown()          no new tasks; queued and running tasks finish
awaitTermination(t) block until finished or timeout
shutdownNow()       interrupt running tasks, return the queued ones as a List<Runnable>
close() (Java 19)   shutdown + awaitTermination in a loop; works with try-with-resources
```

The idiom: `shutdown()`, then `awaitTermination(30, SECONDS)`, then `shutdownNow()` if it timed out. Forgetting to shut down a pool of non-daemon threads keeps the JVM from exiting.

**Spring Boot:** auto-configures a `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` named `applicationTaskExecutor` (core 8, unbounded queue by default, configurable with `spring.task.execution.pool.*`), used by `@Async` and MVC async requests. With `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` (Boot 3.2+) it uses virtual threads instead.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.concurrent.RejectedExecutionException;
import java.util.concurrent.ScheduledExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.ScheduledFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadFactory;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class ThreadPoolDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1. Watch the core -> queue -> max -> reject sequence
        CountDownLatch release = new CountDownLatch(1);
        AtomicInteger threadNumber = new AtomicInteger();
        ThreadFactory named = r -> new Thread(r, "orderhub-worker-" + threadNumber.incrementAndGet());

        ThreadPoolExecutor pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
                2, 4,                                   // core 2, max 4
                30, TimeUnit.SECONDS,                   // idle non-core threads end after 30 s
                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2),            // bounded queue of 2
                named,
                new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy());  // throw when saturated

        Runnable blockingTask = () -> {
            try {
                release.await();                        // hold the thread until we release it
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        };

        for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
            try {
                pool.execute(blockingTask);
                System.out.println("task " + i + ": threads=" + pool.getPoolSize()
                        + " queued=" + pool.getQueue().size());
            } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
                System.out.println("task " + i + ": REJECTED (4 threads busy, queue full)");
            }
        }
        release.countDown();                            // let all tasks finish
        pool.shutdown();                                // no new tasks
        pool.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        System.out.println("completed=" + pool.getCompletedTaskCount() + " terminated=" + pool.isTerminated());

        // 2. invokeAll returns futures in submission order
        try (ExecutorService fixed = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3)) {
            List<Callable<String>> calls = List.of(() -> "price", () -> "stock", () -> "reviews");
            List<String> results = new ArrayList<>();
            for (Future<String> f : fixed.invokeAll(calls)) {
                results.add(f.get());
            }
            System.out.println("invokeAll: " + results);
        }

        // 3. Delayed task
        try (ScheduledExecutorService scheduler = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor()) {
            ScheduledFuture<String> reminder =
                    scheduler.schedule(() -> "payment reminder sent", 100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            System.out.println("scheduled: " + reminder.get());
        }

        // 4. Java 21: one virtual thread per task, 10,000 blocking tasks
        AtomicInteger done = new AtomicInteger();
        try (ExecutorService virtual = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
                virtual.submit(() -> {
                    Thread.sleep(10);                   // blocking call: the virtual thread unmounts
                    return done.incrementAndGet();
                });
            }
        }
        System.out.println("virtual threads finished " + done.get() + " tasks");
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- Every task blocks on the latch, so threads stay busy and we can watch the pool fill up deterministically.
- Tasks 1 and 2 each start a core thread. Tasks 3 and 4 go into the queue (capacity 2) because core threads exist. Task 5 finds the queue full and the pool below max, so a third thread starts; task 6 starts the fourth. Task 7 finds 4 threads and a full queue and is rejected by `AbortPolicy`.
- After `release.countDown()`, the six accepted tasks finish, `shutdown` lets the queue drain, and `awaitTermination` waits for it. The completed count is 6.
- `invokeAll` waits for all tasks and returns futures in the order of the input list, so results are in a predictable order no matter which finishes first.
- `schedule` returns a `ScheduledFuture`; `get()` waits for the 100 ms delay and the result.
- `close()` (Java 19+) on the virtual-thread executor waits for all 10,000 tasks. Ten thousand platform threads would be expensive; ten thousand virtual threads sleeping 10 ms each finish in a fraction of a second.

### Output
```text
task 1: threads=1 queued=0
task 2: threads=2 queued=0
task 3: threads=2 queued=1
task 4: threads=2 queued=2
task 5: threads=3 queued=2
task 6: threads=4 queued=2
task 7: REJECTED (4 threads busy, queue full)
completed=6 terminated=true
invokeAll: [price, stock, reviews]
scheduled: payment reminder sent
virtual threads finished 10000 tasks
```

### Compare
| Old or risky | Better | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(task).start()` per request | Bounded `ThreadPoolExecutor` | Reuse and bounded resources |
| `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)` in a service | Explicit `ThreadPoolExecutor` with `ArrayBlockingQueue` | Unbounded queue hides overload until OOM |
| `newCachedThreadPool()` for request work | Bounded max plus `CallerRunsPolicy` | Unbounded threads under spikes |
| Never shutting down | `try (var pool = ...)` or `shutdown` plus `awaitTermination` | Lingering threads, JVM cannot exit |
| Huge platform pool for blocking I/O | `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` (Java 21) | Cheap threads, simpler code |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **Why use a thread pool instead of creating threads?**
   Thread creation is expensive and unbounded creation can crash the JVM. A pool reuses a bounded set of threads, queues excess work, gives an overload policy and central lifecycle management.
2. **What are the parameters of `ThreadPoolExecutor`?**
   Core pool size, maximum pool size, keep-alive time and unit, the work queue, a thread factory and a rejected-execution handler.
3. **What is the difference between `shutdown` and `shutdownNow`?**
   `shutdown` stops accepting new tasks but lets queued and running tasks finish. `shutdownNow` also interrupts running tasks and returns the tasks that never started. Neither waits; call `awaitTermination` for that.

**Follow-up**
4. **In what order does `ThreadPoolExecutor` use core threads, the queue and max threads?**
   First it starts threads up to core size; then it queues; only when the queue is full does it start threads up to the maximum; then it rejects. So with an unbounded queue the pool never grows beyond core.
5. **Why is `Executors.newFixedThreadPool` considered dangerous in production?**
   It uses an unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue`. If tasks arrive faster than they finish, the queue grows without limit, latency climbs and eventually memory runs out, with no rejection to signal overload. Build the executor with a bounded queue and a rejection policy.
6. **What does `CallerRunsPolicy` do and why is it useful?**
   When the pool is saturated, the submitting thread runs the task itself. That slows the producer down to the pool's pace, a simple form of back-pressure, and nothing is dropped.
7. **How do you size a thread pool?**
   For CPU-bound work about the number of cores; for I/O-bound work cores x (1 + wait time / compute time), then tune with metrics. Also respect downstream limits such as the database connection pool size.

**Tricky / scenario**
8. **We configured core 10, max 100 and a `LinkedBlockingQueue` with no capacity, yet only 10 threads ever run while latency grows. Why?**
   The pool only creates threads beyond core when the queue rejects an offer, and an unbounded queue never does. Use a bounded queue (or a `SynchronousQueue`) so the pool can grow to max.
9. **A Spring `@Async` method is called 50,000 times during a batch import and the service runs out of memory. What happened?**
   The default `applicationTaskExecutor` has an unbounded queue, so 50,000 tasks (with their captured data) queued up. Configure `spring.task.execution.pool.queue-capacity` and `max-size`, a rejection policy, or process the batch in chunks.
10. **When would you use virtual threads instead of a pool?**
    For many concurrent tasks that mostly block on I/O (HTTP calls, JDBC), with Java 21: each task gets a cheap virtual thread and you write simple blocking code. For CPU-bound work they give no benefit, and you still need limits (a `Semaphore` or connection pool) to protect scarce resources.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you know the task-admission algorithm, the hidden unbounded queues behind convenient factories, and can size, monitor and shut down pools like someone who has run them in production.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Unbounded queues (`newFixedThreadPool`) | Memory grows, latency explodes silently | Bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue` and a rejection policy |
| Expecting max threads with an unbounded queue | Pool never grows beyond core | Bounded queue or `SynchronousQueue` |
| Not shutting down pools | Thread leaks, JVM will not exit | try-with-resources or `shutdown` and `awaitTermination` |
| One shared pool for everything | A slow dependency starves all other work | Separate pools per dependency (bulkheads) |
| Tasks that block waiting for other tasks in the same pool | Starvation deadlock | Separate pools or non-blocking composition |
| Unnamed threads | Unreadable thread dumps | Custom `ThreadFactory` or Spring `threadNamePrefix` |
| Ignoring the `Future` of submitted tasks | Silent failures | Check results or log inside tasks |

### Best practices
- Create executors explicitly with bounded queues, named threads and a chosen rejection policy; expose them as Spring beans so they are shut down with the context.
- Monitor pool metrics (active threads, queue size, completed and rejected counts); Micrometer can bind `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` metrics automatically.
- Use separate pools for different workloads (bulkhead pattern) so one slow downstream system cannot consume every thread.
- Prefer virtual threads for blocking I/O on Java 21, and keep a bounded pool for CPU-heavy work.
- Propagate context (MDC, security, tracing) with a `TaskDecorator` in Spring.

### Compare with
| Executor | Threads | Queue | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Custom ThreadPoolExecutor | Bounded core and max | Bounded | Production services |
| `newFixedThreadPool` | Fixed | Unbounded | Small tools, tests |
| `newCachedThreadPool` | Unbounded | None (handoff) | Short bursts of short tasks |
| `ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor` | Fixed core | Delay queue | Timers, periodic jobs |
| ForkJoinPool | Work-stealing | Per-thread deques | Recursive CPU tasks, parallel streams |
| Virtual-thread-per-task | One virtual thread per task | None | High-concurrency blocking I/O (Java 21) |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
Configure OrderHub's email-notification executor as a Spring bean: a `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` with core 4, max 8, queue capacity 100, thread name prefix `notify-`, `CallerRunsPolicy`, and graceful shutdown that waits up to 30 seconds. Then write an `@Async` method that sends an order confirmation on that executor. Acceptance criteria:
- `@EnableAsync` is present and the executor bean is named `notificationExecutor`.
- `@Async("notificationExecutor")` is used on a public method of a separate bean (not self-invoked).
- Application properties keep Spring's default `applicationTaskExecutor` bounded too.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableAsync;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class NotificationExecutorConfig {

    @Bean(name = "notificationExecutor")
    public ThreadPoolTaskExecutor notificationExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(4);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(8);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);                       // bounded: the pool can grow to 8
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("notify-");
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        executor.setWaitForTasksToCompleteOnShutdown(true);
        executor.setAwaitTerminationSeconds(30);
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderNotificationService {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderNotificationService.class);

    @Async("notificationExecutor")
    public void sendConfirmation(long orderId, String email) {
        try {
            // call the mail provider here
            log.info("Sent confirmation for order {} to {} on {}", orderId, email, Thread.currentThread().getName());
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            log.error("Confirmation for order {} failed", orderId, e);   // void @Async: log, nobody calls get()
        }
    }
}
```

```yaml
spring:
  task:
    execution:
      pool:
        core-size: 8
        max-size: 16
        queue-capacity: 500
      thread-name-prefix: app-async-
```
Log line when `OrderService` calls `notificationService.sendConfirmation(42, "ana@example.com")` after saving an order:
```text
INFO  c.o.n.OrderNotificationService : Sent confirmation for order 42 to ana@example.com on notify-1
```
`@Async` works through a Spring proxy, so the method must be public and called from another bean; a call from inside the same class bypasses the proxy and runs synchronously.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: In what order does ThreadPoolExecutor handle a new task?
A: Start a thread if below core size, otherwise queue it, otherwise start a thread if below max size, otherwise apply the rejection policy.

Q: Why is `Executors.newFixedThreadPool` risky in production services?
A: It uses an unbounded queue, so overload turns into growing memory and latency instead of rejections.

Q: What does ThreadPoolExecutor's CallerRunsPolicy do?
A: It runs a rejected task on the submitting thread, slowing producers down as back-pressure.

Q: What is the rule of thumb for sizing an I/O-bound thread pool?
A: Cores x (1 + wait time / compute time), then tune with metrics and downstream limits.

Q: What is the difference between ExecutorService `shutdown()` and `shutdownNow()`?
A: `shutdown()` finishes queued and running tasks but accepts no new ones; `shutdownNow()` interrupts running tasks and returns the unstarted ones.

**Related:** [[Runnable vs Callable]] · [[Future and CompletableFuture]] · [[Virtual Threads]] · [[ForkJoinPool and Work Stealing]]

# Future and CompletableFuture

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 9 | Priority: MUST | Since: Java 5 (Future), Java 8 (CompletableFuture), Java 9 (timeouts)**

> **30-second interview answer:** A `Future` is a handle to a result that will exist later, but the only way to use it is to block on `get()`. `CompletableFuture` (Java 8) is a `Future` you can compose: you attach callbacks such as `thenApply` to transform, `thenCompose` to chain another async call, `thenCombine` or `allOf` to join parallel calls, and `exceptionally` or `handle` to recover from errors, all without blocking. I always pass my own executor to the `...Async` methods, because the default common `ForkJoinPool` is small and shared, and I add timeouts with `orTimeout` or `completeOnTimeout`.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Ordering at a food court.

| Food court | Java |
|---|---|
| A paper ticket: you stand at the counter until your number is called | `Future.get()`: block until ready |
| A buzzer that vibrates when your food is ready, so you can sit down | `CompletableFuture` callbacks |
| "When the burger is ready, add ketchup" | `thenApply` (transform the result) |
| "When the burger is ready, order a dessert that uses its receipt" | `thenCompose` (start another async step) |
| Burger and drink ordered at two counters, eat when both arrive | `thenCombine`, `allOf` |
| "If the burger is sold out, give me fries instead" | `exceptionally`, `handle` |
| "If it is not ready in 10 minutes, I take a sandwich" | `completeOnTimeout` |
| Which staff cook your order | The `Executor` you pass in |

### What it is
- **`Future<V>`** (Java 5): `get()`, `get(timeout, unit)`, `isDone()`, `cancel(boolean)`. No callbacks, no composition, no way to complete it yourself.
- **`CompletableFuture<V>`** (Java 8): implements `Future` and `CompletionStage`. It can be completed by a task (`supplyAsync`, `runAsync`) or manually (`complete(v)`, `completeExceptionally(ex)`).
- **Stage methods (each returns a new `CompletableFuture`):**

| Method | Input to your function | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `thenApply(fn)` | The result | Transform synchronously (like `map`) |
| `thenAccept(consumer)` | The result | Consume, return nothing |
| `thenRun(runnable)` | Nothing | Run after completion |
| `thenCompose(fn)` | The result; fn returns a `CompletableFuture` | Chain a dependent async call (like `flatMap`) |
| `thenCombine(other, bifn)` | Both results | Join two independent futures |
| `allOf(cfs...)` | Nothing (returns `CompletableFuture<Void>`) | Wait for all |
| `anyOf(cfs...)` | The first result as `Object` | First to finish |
| `exceptionally(fn)` | The exception | Recover with a fallback value |
| `handle(bifn)` | Result or exception | Map both outcomes |
| `whenComplete(biconsumer)` | Result or exception | Side effect (logging), keeps the outcome |
| `orTimeout`, `completeOnTimeout` (Java 9) | | Fail or default after a time limit |

- **`...Async` variants** (`thenApplyAsync`, etc.) run the callback on an executor instead of on whichever thread completed the previous stage.
- **`join()` vs `get()`:** both block; `join` throws unchecked `CompletionException`, `get` throws checked `ExecutionException` and `InterruptedException`.

### Why it exists
Real services call several slow systems: price, stock, reviews, shipping. With plain `Future`s you either call them one after another or block a thread per call and then juggle `get()`s with nested try-catch. `CompletableFuture` lets you describe the whole flow (fan out, combine, recover, time out) declaratively, so threads are not wasted waiting and the total latency is the slowest call, not the sum.

## 2. How It Works
**Sequential vs parallel calls:**

```text
Sequential with Future.get():          Parallel with CompletableFuture:
price   |=====| 200 ms                 price   |=====|
stock          |=====| 200 ms          stock   |=====|
reviews               |===| 150 ms     reviews |===|
total   550 ms                         total   200 ms (slowest call) then combine
```

**Inside:** a `CompletableFuture` holds a `volatile Object result` (the value, or an `AltResult` wrapping an exception or null) and a stack of dependent actions. Calling `thenApply` on an incomplete future pushes a dependent node; when the future completes (via CAS on `result`), it pops and fires the dependents. If the future is already complete, `thenApply` runs the function immediately on the calling thread.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["supplyAsync(fetchProduct, io)"] --> B["thenCompose(p -> fetchReviewsAsync(p))"]
    A --> C["thenApply(p -> p.name())"]
    D["supplyAsync(fetchPrice, io)"] --> E["thenCombine(stock, (p, s) -> summary)"]
    F["supplyAsync(fetchStock, io)"] --> E
    E --> G["exceptionally(ex -> fallback)"]
    G --> H["orTimeout(2, SECONDS)"]
    H --> I["join() at the edge, or return to the framework"]
```

**Which thread runs a callback?**
- Non-async `thenApply`: the thread that completes the previous stage, or the caller if it is already complete. Keep these callbacks cheap.
- `thenApplyAsync(fn)` without an executor: the common `ForkJoinPool` (size = cores minus 1). Blocking I/O there starves parallel streams and every other user of the pool.
- `thenApplyAsync(fn, executor)`: your executor. For I/O, always provide one.

**Exceptions travel down the chain:** if a stage throws, dependents are skipped and complete exceptionally with a `CompletionException` wrapping the cause, until a stage like `exceptionally` or `handle` recovers. Because of that wrapping, a handler should unwrap: `Throwable cause = ex instanceof CompletionException ? ex.getCause() : ex;`.

**Cancellation does not interrupt:** `cancel(true)` on a `CompletableFuture` completes it with `CancellationException` but does not interrupt the thread running the task. Likewise, `orTimeout` completes the future but the underlying work keeps running.

Phase 16's [[CompletableFuture Composition and Error Handling]] goes deeper into composition patterns, error strategies and context propagation.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletionException;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class CompletableFutureDemo {

    record Product(long id, String name) { }

    // Simulates a remote call
    static <T> T slow(long millis, T value) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(millis);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            throw new IllegalStateException(e);
        }
        return value;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        try (ExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8)) {
            // 1. Plain Future: the only option is to block
            Future<String> legacy = io.submit(() -> slow(100, "legacy result"));
            System.out.println("Future.isDone right away: " + legacy.isDone());
            System.out.println("Future.get: " + legacy.get());

            // 2. Two independent calls in parallel, combined
            CompletableFuture<Integer> price = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> slow(200, 1999), io);
            CompletableFuture<Integer> stock = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> slow(200, 7), io);
            String summary = price.thenCombine(stock, (p, s) -> "price=" + p + " stock=" + s).join();
            System.out.println("combined: " + summary);

            // 3. thenApply transforms; thenCompose chains another async call
            CompletableFuture<Product> product = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> new Product(42, "Keyboard"), io);
            CompletableFuture<String> label = product.thenApply(p -> p.name().toUpperCase());
            CompletableFuture<String> reviews = product.thenCompose(
                    p -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> p.name() + " has 12 reviews", io));
            System.out.println("thenApply: " + label.join());
            System.out.println("thenCompose: " + reviews.join());

            // 4. Fan out to many services, wait for all
            List<CompletableFuture<String>> calls = List.of("inventory", "pricing", "shipping").stream()
                    .map(name -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> slow(50, name + ":ok"), io))
                    .toList();
            CompletableFuture.allOf(calls.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).join();
            System.out.println("allOf: " + calls.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList());

            // 5. Recovering from failure
            CompletableFuture<Integer> failing = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
                throw new IllegalStateException("pricing service down");
            }, io);
            int fallback = failing.exceptionally(ex -> {
                Throwable cause = ex instanceof CompletionException ? ex.getCause() : ex;
                System.out.println("recovered from: " + cause.getMessage());
                return 0;
            }).join();
            System.out.println("fallback price: " + fallback);
            String handled = failing.handle((v, ex) -> ex == null ? "ok " + v : "failed, using cache").join();
            System.out.println("handle: " + handled);

            // 6. Time limit with a default value (Java 9)
            String estimate = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> slow(1000, "carrier estimate"), io)
                    .completeOnTimeout("default shipping estimate", 100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)
                    .join();
            System.out.println("completeOnTimeout: " + estimate);

            // 7. join throws an unchecked CompletionException
            try {
                failing.join();
            } catch (CompletionException e) {
                System.out.println("join throws CompletionException, cause: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- Part 1: `isDone()` is false because the task sleeps 100 ms; `get()` then blocks the main thread until it finishes. That blocking is all a plain `Future` offers.
- Part 2: price and stock run at the same time on the `io` pool, so the combined result arrives after about 200 ms, not 400. `thenCombine` runs when both are done.
- Part 3: `thenApply` maps `Product` to `String`. `thenCompose` is needed when the function itself returns a `CompletableFuture`; with `thenApply` you would get a nested `CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<String>>`.
- Part 4: `allOf` returns `CompletableFuture<Void>`; after joining it, every individual future is done, so `join()` on each returns instantly. Results keep the list order.
- Part 5: the supplier throws, so `failing` completes exceptionally. `exceptionally` receives a `CompletionException` wrapping the real exception, which we unwrap, and supplies 0. `handle` sees both outcomes and maps either to a string.
- Part 6: `completeOnTimeout` completes the future with a default if the real value has not arrived in 100 ms. The slow task keeps running in the background (and the pool's `close()` waits for it at the end).
- Part 7: `join()` on a failed future throws `CompletionException` (unchecked), while `get()` would throw `ExecutionException`.
- All printing happens on the main thread after a `join()`, except the "recovered from" line, which runs inside the `exceptionally` callback before `join()` returns; so the output order is fixed.

### Output
```text
Future.isDone right away: false
Future.get: legacy result
combined: price=1999 stock=7
thenApply: KEYBOARD
thenCompose: Keyboard has 12 reviews
allOf: [inventory:ok, pricing:ok, shipping:ok]
recovered from: pricing service down
fallback price: 0
handle: failed, using cache
completeOnTimeout: default shipping estimate
join throws CompletionException, cause: pricing service down
```

### Compare
| Future | CompletableFuture |
|---|---|
| Only `get()` (blocking) | Callbacks: `thenApply`, `thenAccept`, `thenCompose` |
| Cannot combine results without blocking | `thenCombine`, `allOf`, `anyOf` |
| Exceptions only through `get()` | `exceptionally`, `handle`, `whenComplete` |
| Cannot be completed manually | `complete`, `completeExceptionally` |
| Timeout only on `get(timeout)` | `orTimeout`, `completeOnTimeout` (Java 9) |
| `cancel(true)` interrupts the task | `cancel` does not interrupt the running task |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is the difference between `Future` and `CompletableFuture`?**
   `Future` represents a pending result that you can only wait for with a blocking `get()`. `CompletableFuture` adds non-blocking composition (transform, chain, combine), error handling, manual completion and timeouts.
2. **What is the difference between `thenApply` and `thenCompose`?**
   `thenApply` takes a function returning a plain value and is like `map`. `thenCompose` takes a function returning another `CompletableFuture` and flattens it, like `flatMap`; use it to chain dependent async calls.
3. **How do you run several calls in parallel and wait for all of them?**
   Start each with `supplyAsync(supplier, executor)`, then `CompletableFuture.allOf(...)` and read each result with `join()` once it completes. For two results, `thenCombine` is neater.

**Follow-up**
4. **Which thread runs `supplyAsync` if you do not pass an executor? Why does that matter?**
   The common `ForkJoinPool`, which has about cores minus one threads and is shared with parallel streams and other libraries. Blocking I/O there can exhaust it and stall unrelated work, so pass a dedicated executor for I/O.
5. **How do exceptions propagate through a chain?**
   A failure skips the remaining normal stages; each dependent completes exceptionally with a `CompletionException` wrapping the original, until `exceptionally`, `handle` or `whenComplete` deals with it. At the end, `join()` throws `CompletionException` and `get()` throws `ExecutionException`.
6. **`exceptionally` vs `handle` vs `whenComplete`?**
   `exceptionally` runs only on failure and returns a replacement value. `handle` runs on both outcomes and maps them to a new value. `whenComplete` runs on both for side effects such as logging and passes the original outcome through unchanged.
7. **How do you add a timeout?**
   Java 9 added `orTimeout(t, unit)`, which completes exceptionally with `TimeoutException`, and `completeOnTimeout(value, t, unit)`, which completes with a default. On Java 8 you would use `get(timeout)` or a scheduler that completes the future.

**Tricky / scenario**
8. **A REST endpoint fans out 3 calls with `supplyAsync` and no executor. Under load, the endpoint and the app's parallel streams both slow to a crawl. Why?**
   All the blocking calls run on the small common `ForkJoinPool`, so it is saturated and every user of it waits. Give the calls their own bounded executor (or virtual threads), and add timeouts so one slow dependency cannot hold threads indefinitely.
9. **Does `orTimeout` stop the slow task?**
   No. It completes the future exceptionally, but the task keeps running and holding its thread. If the work supports it, cancel it separately (for example through an HTTP client timeout) and always set timeouts on the underlying I/O.
10. **Why might `cf.thenApply(...)` run on the main thread?**
    If `cf` is already complete when `thenApply` is called, the function runs immediately on the calling thread. Otherwise it runs on the thread that completes `cf`. Use `thenApplyAsync(fn, executor)` when you need control.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you can design a parallel, failure-tolerant call flow, know which threads execute each stage, and avoid the common-pool and missing-timeout traps.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Blocking I/O in `supplyAsync` without an executor | Common pool starved, app-wide slowdown | Pass a dedicated executor |
| Calling `join()` right after each `supplyAsync` | Calls become sequential | Start all futures first, then join |
| No timeouts | Threads and requests hang on slow services | `orTimeout`, `completeOnTimeout`, client timeouts |
| Using `thenApply` with a function returning a future | Nested `CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<T>>` | `thenCompose` |
| Forgetting `CompletionException` wrapping | Wrong exception type checks | Unwrap `getCause()` |
| Swallowing failures in `exceptionally` without logging | Silent degraded behaviour | Log, emit a metric, then fall back |

### Best practices
- Fan out first, join last; join only at the edge (or return the `CompletableFuture` from a Spring MVC controller, which supports async return values).
- Always use your own bounded executor (or a virtual-thread executor on Java 21) for I/O stages.
- Put a timeout on every remote call and a fallback where the business allows a degraded answer.
- Use `whenComplete` for logging and metrics without altering results.
- Keep callbacks short and non-blocking; heavy work goes in `...Async` stages on a suitable executor.

### Compare with
| Approach | Blocking | Composition | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sequential calls | Yes | Not needed | Simple, dependent steps |
| `ExecutorService` plus `Future.get()` | Yes, at `get` | Manual | A few parallel tasks |
| `invokeAll` | Yes | Collect all results | Batch of similar tasks |
| CompletableFuture | No until `join` | Rich | Parallel service calls with fallbacks |
| Virtual threads with simple blocking code (Java 21) | Yes, but cheap | Plain code | Readable concurrency for I/O |
| Reactor (`Mono`, `Flux`) | No | Very rich, streams, back-pressure | WebFlux, streaming |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
OrderHub's product page needs product details, price and stock. Write a plain-Java `ProductPageService.load(long id)` that fetches price and stock in parallel after the product is loaded, with a 300 ms timeout on stock that falls back to "unknown", and a failing price service that falls back to the catalogue price. Acceptance criteria:
- Uses a dedicated executor, `thenCompose` (or chaining after the product) and `thenCombine`.
- The stock call takes 1000 ms, so the fallback is used; the price call throws.
- `load(42)` returns `ProductPage[name=Keyboard, price=49.99 (catalogue), stock=unknown]`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class ProductPageService implements AutoCloseable {

    record Product(long id, String name, String cataloguePrice) { }
    record ProductPage(String name, String price, String stock) { }

    private final ExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);

    CompletableFuture<Product> fetchProduct(long id) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> new Product(id, "Keyboard", "49.99"), io);
    }

    CompletableFuture<String> fetchLivePrice(Product p) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            throw new IllegalStateException("pricing down");
        }, io);
    }

    CompletableFuture<String> fetchStock(Product p) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
            return "12";
        }, io);
    }

    ProductPage load(long id) {
        return fetchProduct(id).thenCompose(product -> {
            CompletableFuture<String> price = fetchLivePrice(product)
                    .exceptionally(ex -> product.cataloguePrice() + " (catalogue)");
            CompletableFuture<String> stock = fetchStock(product)
                    .completeOnTimeout("unknown", 300, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            return price.thenCombine(stock, (pr, st) -> new ProductPage(product.name(), pr, st));
        }).join();
    }

    @Override
    public void close() { io.shutdownNow(); }   // interrupts the still-sleeping stock call

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (ProductPageService service = new ProductPageService()) {
            System.out.println(service.load(42));
        }
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
ProductPage[name=Keyboard, price=49.99 (catalogue), stock=unknown]
```
Price and stock start at the same moment once the product is known, so the page is ready after about 300 ms (the stock timeout) instead of waiting for the slow call.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What can CompletableFuture do that a plain Future cannot?
A: Compose stages with callbacks (`thenApply`, `thenCompose`, `thenCombine`), handle errors (`exceptionally`, `handle`), be completed manually and apply timeouts, all without blocking.

Q: What is the difference between CompletableFuture `thenApply` and `thenCompose`?
A: `thenApply` maps the result to a plain value; `thenCompose` maps it to another `CompletableFuture` and flattens it, for chaining dependent async calls.

Q: Which pool runs `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(supplier)` when no executor is given?
A: The common `ForkJoinPool`, which is small and shared, so blocking I/O should use a dedicated executor.

Q: What does `CompletableFuture.join()` throw when the future failed?
A: An unchecked `CompletionException` wrapping the original exception.

Q: Does `orTimeout` stop the underlying task of a CompletableFuture?
A: No, it only completes the future exceptionally; the task keeps running.

**Related:** [[Runnable vs Callable]] · [[ExecutorService and Thread Pools]] · [[CompletableFuture Composition and Error Handling]] · [[RestClient, WebClient and HTTP Interfaces]]

# Concurrent Collections and BlockingQueue

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 9 | Priority: MUST | Since: Java 5 (ConcurrentSkipListMap Java 6, LinkedTransferQueue Java 7)**

> **30-second interview answer:** `java.util.concurrent` provides collections that are safe for many threads without external locking: `ConcurrentHashMap` for maps, `CopyOnWriteArrayList` for read-mostly lists, `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` for non-blocking queues and `ConcurrentSkipListMap` for sorted maps. `BlockingQueue` adds methods that wait: `put` blocks while the queue is full and `take` blocks while it is empty, which makes it the standard building block for producer-consumer designs and the work queue inside every thread pool. They scale far better than `Collections.synchronizedXxx` wrappers because they use lock striping, CAS or copy-on-write instead of one big lock.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A warehouse loading dock.

| Loading dock | Java |
|---|---|
| A conveyor belt with room for 10 boxes | `ArrayBlockingQueue` with capacity 10 |
| The loader stops when the belt is full | `put` blocks while full |
| The packer waits when the belt is empty | `take` blocks while empty |
| "Try to place the box; if full, put it aside" | `offer` returns false |
| "Wait up to 5 seconds for a box, then give up" | `poll(5, SECONDS)` returns null |
| A box marked "end of shift" | A poison pill that tells consumers to stop |
| A handover with no belt at all: loader waits until a packer takes the box from their hands | `SynchronousQueue` |
| Express boxes jump the line | `PriorityBlockingQueue` |
| A noticeboard that is reprinted whenever someone edits it; readers keep their own printed copy | `CopyOnWriteArrayList` |

### What it is
- **Thread-safe collection:** every method is atomic and visible across threads without the caller locking.
- **Main families:**

| Class | Structure | Concurrency technique | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ConcurrentHashMap` | Hash table | CAS plus per-bin locks | Shared maps, caches, counters |
| `ConcurrentSkipListMap`, `ConcurrentSkipListSet` | Skip list (sorted) | Lock-free CAS | Sorted shared data, leaderboards |
| `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, `CopyOnWriteArraySet` | Array copied on every write | Copy-on-write | Listeners, config lists: many reads, rare writes |
| `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`, `ConcurrentLinkedDeque` | Linked nodes | Lock-free CAS (Michael-Scott queue) | Non-blocking unbounded queues |
| `ArrayBlockingQueue` | Fixed array | One `ReentrantLock`, two conditions | Bounded producer-consumer |
| `LinkedBlockingQueue` | Linked nodes, optionally bounded | Separate put and take locks | Thread-pool queues, higher throughput |
| `PriorityBlockingQueue` | Binary heap, unbounded | One lock | Priority work queues |
| `DelayQueue` | Heap of `Delayed` elements | One lock | Retry after delay, expiring items |
| `SynchronousQueue` | No storage | Direct handoff | `newCachedThreadPool`, handoffs |
| `LinkedTransferQueue` | Linked nodes, unbounded | Lock-free, `transfer` waits for a consumer | High-throughput handoffs |

- **The four method groups of `BlockingQueue`:**

| Operation | Throws exception | Returns special value | Blocks | Times out |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Insert | `add(e)` | `offer(e)` returns false | `put(e)` | `offer(e, time, unit)` |
| Remove | `remove()` | `poll()` returns null | `take()` | `poll(time, unit)` |
| Examine | `element()` | `peek()` returns null | Not applicable | Not applicable |

- **Producer-consumer:** producers put work on a queue, consumers take it off, and the queue decouples their speeds. A bounded queue gives back-pressure: fast producers are slowed down instead of exhausting memory.
- **Poison pill:** a special element that tells a consumer to stop; with N consumers, put N pills.
- Concurrent collections reject `null` elements (`null` is used as the "nothing there" signal by `poll` and `get`).

### Why it exists
`Collections.synchronizedList` and `Hashtable` serialize every access through one lock, so they do not scale, and compound actions and iteration still need manual locking. Hand-written `wait` and `notify` queues are error-prone. Doug Lea's `java.util.concurrent` (JSR-166, Java 5) gave correct, fast building blocks so application code rarely needs explicit locks.

## 2. How It Works
**`ArrayBlockingQueue` internals** (essentially the bounded buffer from [[wait, notify and notifyAll]], done properly):

```text
items: Object[capacity]   takeIndex -> [ e1 ][ e2 ][ e3 ][    ][    ] <- putIndex (circular)
count = 3
lock = ReentrantLock
notEmpty = lock.newCondition()   // consumers wait here
notFull  = lock.newCondition()   // producers wait here

put(e):  lock; while (count == items.length) notFull.await(); enqueue; notEmpty.signal(); unlock
take():  lock; while (count == 0) notEmpty.await(); dequeue; notFull.signal(); unlock
```

Two `Condition`s mean a producer only wakes consumers and vice versa: no lost wakeups and no wasted `notifyAll`. `LinkedBlockingQueue` goes further with separate `putLock` and `takeLock`, so a producer and a consumer can work at the same time.

**Producer-consumer flow:**

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant P as "Producer"
    participant Q as "BlockingQueue (capacity 2)"
    participant C as "Consumer"
    P->>Q: "put(order 1)"
    P->>Q: "put(order 2)"
    P->>Q: "put(order 3): full, producer blocks"
    C->>Q: "take(): order 1"
    Q-->>P: "space freed, put(order 3) completes"
    C->>Q: "take(): order 2"
    C->>Q: "take(): order 3"
    P->>Q: "put(POISON)"
    C->>Q: "take(): POISON, consumer stops"
```

**Copy-on-write:** every `add`, `set` or `remove` on `CopyOnWriteArrayList` copies the whole array under a lock and swaps a volatile reference to the new array. Readers and iterators use whichever array was current when they started: no locks, no `ConcurrentModificationException`, but O(n) per write and possibly stale reads. Great for 10 listeners read a million times; terrible for a list with frequent writes.

**Skip list:** a sorted linked list with extra "express lane" levels added randomly, giving expected O(log n) search. It is easier to make lock-free than a red-black tree, which is why the concurrent sorted map is a skip list, not a `TreeMap`.

**Weakly consistent iterators:** concurrent collections' iterators never throw `ConcurrentModificationException` and may or may not reflect changes made during iteration. `size()` on `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` is O(n) and only an estimate under concurrency.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentSkipListMap;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.PriorityBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.SynchronousQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class ConcurrentCollectionsDemo {

    record OrderEvent(long orderId, long amountCents) { }

    private static final OrderEvent POISON = new OrderEvent(-1, 0);

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1. The four method groups on a full and an empty queue
        BlockingQueue<String> q = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);
        q.put("a");
        q.put("b");
        System.out.println("offer on full: " + q.offer("c"));
        System.out.println("offer with 50 ms timeout: " + q.offer("c", 50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS));
        try {
            q.add("c");
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("add on full: IllegalStateException (" + e.getMessage() + ")");
        }
        q.clear();
        System.out.println("poll on empty: " + q.poll());
        System.out.println("poll with 50 ms timeout: " + q.poll(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS));

        // 2. One producer, three consumers, bounded queue, poison pills
        BlockingQueue<OrderEvent> events = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100);
        AtomicLong revenue = new AtomicLong();
        AtomicLong processed = new AtomicLong();
        int consumers = 3;
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(consumers + 1)) {
            for (int c = 0; c < consumers; c++) {
                pool.submit(() -> {
                    while (true) {
                        OrderEvent e = events.take();          // blocks while empty
                        if (e == POISON) return null;          // stop signal
                        revenue.addAndGet(e.amountCents());
                        processed.incrementAndGet();
                    }
                });
            }
            pool.submit(() -> {
                for (long id = 1; id <= 1000; id++) {
                    events.put(new OrderEvent(id, 500));       // blocks while 100 are waiting
                }
                for (int c = 0; c < consumers; c++) {
                    events.put(POISON);                        // one pill per consumer
                }
                return null;
            });
        }
        System.out.println("processed=" + processed.get() + " revenueCents=" + revenue.get());

        // 3. CopyOnWriteArrayList: modify while iterating, iteration sees a snapshot
        List<String> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>(List.of("email", "sms"));
        for (String l : listeners) {
            if (l.equals("email")) listeners.add("push");
        }
        System.out.println("listeners: " + listeners);

        // 4. ConcurrentSkipListMap: a sorted map updated by several threads
        ConcurrentSkipListMap<Integer, String> leaderboard = new ConcurrentSkipListMap<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4)) {
            pool.submit(() -> leaderboard.put(90, "ana"));
            pool.submit(() -> leaderboard.put(75, "ben"));
            pool.submit(() -> leaderboard.put(99, "cy"));
            pool.submit(() -> leaderboard.put(60, "dee"));
        }
        System.out.println("top: " + leaderboard.firstEntry() + ", ranking: " + leaderboard);

        // 5. PriorityBlockingQueue: highest amount first
        PriorityBlockingQueue<OrderEvent> urgent =
                new PriorityBlockingQueue<>(11, Comparator.comparingLong(OrderEvent::amountCents).reversed());
        urgent.add(new OrderEvent(1, 100));
        urgent.add(new OrderEvent(2, 9000));
        urgent.add(new OrderEvent(3, 500));
        System.out.println("highest value first: order " + urgent.take().orderId());

        // 6. SynchronousQueue: no capacity, a direct handoff
        SynchronousQueue<String> handoff = new SynchronousQueue<>();
        System.out.println("offer with no waiting consumer: " + handoff.offer("x"));
        Thread taker = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                System.out.println("taken: " + handoff.take());
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        taker.start();
        handoff.put("parcel");      // waits until the taker receives it
        taker.join();
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- Part 1 shows each method group: `offer` returns false at once, timed `offer` gives up after 50 ms, `add` throws `IllegalStateException("Queue full")`, and on an empty queue `poll` returns null at once or after the timeout.
- Part 2 is the producer-consumer pattern. The queue is bounded at 100, so the producer blocks whenever consumers fall behind. Each consumer stops on the poison pill; with three consumers the producer sends three pills. The pool's `close()` waits for all of them, and the totals are exact: 1000 events x 500 cents.
- The lambdas use `return null` so they are `Callable`s, which may throw the checked `InterruptedException` from `take` and `put`.
- Part 3: adding during iteration is allowed. The loop iterates the snapshot taken when it started, so `push` is added once and not visited.
- Part 4: four threads insert concurrently, but the skip list keeps keys sorted (descending, by the comparator), so the result does not depend on timing.
- Part 5: `take()` returns the element with the highest priority according to the comparator, not the oldest.
- Part 6: `SynchronousQueue` has no capacity: `offer` fails when nobody is waiting, and `put` blocks until a consumer takes the element from its hands.

### Output
```text
offer on full: false
offer with 50 ms timeout: false
add on full: IllegalStateException (Queue full)
poll on empty: null
poll with 50 ms timeout: null
processed=1000 revenueCents=500000
listeners: [email, sms, push]
top: 99=cy, ranking: {99=cy, 90=ana, 75=ben, 60=dee}
highest value first: order 2
offer with no waiting consumer: false
taken: parcel
```

### Compare
| Synchronized wrapper or legacy | Concurrent collection | Difference |
|---|---|---|
| `Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>())`, `Hashtable` | `ConcurrentHashMap` | Per-bin locking, lock-free reads, atomic `merge` and `compute` |
| `Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>())` | `CopyOnWriteArrayList` (read-mostly) | No lock to iterate, snapshot iteration |
| `Collections.synchronizedSortedMap(new TreeMap<>())` | `ConcurrentSkipListMap` | Lock-free, concurrent navigation |
| Hand-written `wait` and `notify` buffer | `ArrayBlockingQueue` or `LinkedBlockingQueue` | Correct, timeouts, two conditions |
| `synchronizedList` used as a queue | `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` or `BlockingQueue` | Non-blocking or blocking queue semantics |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is a `BlockingQueue`?**
   A thread-safe queue with operations that wait: `put` blocks while the queue is full and `take` blocks while it is empty, plus timed `offer` and `poll`. It is the standard tool for producer-consumer designs and the work queue of `ThreadPoolExecutor`.
2. **What is the difference between `offer`, `add` and `put`?**
   On a full queue, `offer` returns false, `add` throws `IllegalStateException`, and `put` waits until there is space. `offer(e, timeout, unit)` waits up to the timeout. The removal side mirrors this: `poll`, `remove`, `take`, `poll(timeout, unit)`.
3. **When do you use `CopyOnWriteArrayList`?**
   When reads and iteration vastly outnumber writes, such as listener or observer lists and small configuration lists. Each write copies the array, so frequent writes or large lists make it slow and memory-hungry.

**Follow-up**
4. **`ArrayBlockingQueue` vs `LinkedBlockingQueue`?**
   `ArrayBlockingQueue` is always bounded, backed by an array, and uses one lock for both ends. `LinkedBlockingQueue` is optionally bounded (unbounded by default, which is risky), allocates a node per element, and uses separate put and take locks, so it often has higher throughput.
5. **Why are concurrent collections better than `Collections.synchronizedList` or `synchronizedMap`?**
   The wrappers use one lock for everything, so threads serialize, and you must still lock manually around iteration and compound actions. Concurrent collections use finer-grained techniques (CAS, striping, copy-on-write), provide atomic compound methods and iterate without `ConcurrentModificationException`.
6. **How do you shut down consumers of a `BlockingQueue` cleanly?**
   Send one poison pill per consumer after the last real item, or interrupt the consumer threads (`take` throws `InterruptedException`), for example through `ExecutorService.shutdownNow`. Poison pills guarantee all queued work is processed first.
7. **What is a `SynchronousQueue` and where is it used?**
   A queue with no capacity: each `put` waits for a matching `take`. `Executors.newCachedThreadPool` uses it, so a task is handed directly to an idle thread or a new thread is created.

**Tricky / scenario**
8. **Our order-import pipeline uses `new LinkedBlockingQueue<>()` between a fast reader and slow database writers, and the service runs out of memory during big imports. Why?**
   The default `LinkedBlockingQueue` is unbounded, so the reader keeps adding and nothing pushes back. Give it a capacity (for example 1000) so `put` blocks when writers fall behind, which bounds memory and throttles the reader.
9. **Is `if (!queue.isEmpty()) item = queue.poll();` safe with several consumers?**
   No, it is check-then-act: another consumer can take the last item between the check and the `poll`, so `poll` may return null. Just call `poll()` (or `take()`) and handle null; the queue's methods are atomic on their own.
10. **Would you use `CopyOnWriteArrayList` to collect results from 16 threads adding 100,000 items each?**
    No. Each add copies the whole array, so that is quadratic work. Use `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`, a per-thread list merged at the end, or a stream collector.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you can pick the right concurrent structure for a workload, explain blocking versus non-blocking semantics and back-pressure, and avoid the unbounded-queue trap.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` between fast and slow stages | Memory exhaustion | Give it a capacity |
| `CopyOnWriteArrayList` with frequent writes | O(n) copy per write, GC pressure | `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` or locking |
| Check-then-act on a concurrent collection | Races despite thread-safe methods | Use the atomic method (`poll`, `putIfAbsent`, `merge`) |
| Inserting `null` | `NullPointerException` | Use a sentinel object or `Optional` |
| Relying on `size()` of concurrent queues | Estimate only, O(n) for linked queues | Track counts separately if needed |
| One poison pill for several consumers | Remaining consumers block forever | One pill per consumer |
| Ignoring `InterruptedException` from `take` | Consumers cannot be shut down | Exit the loop and restore the flag |

### Best practices
- Bound every queue between stages; decide what happens when it is full (block, drop, reject).
- Use `poll(timeout)` in consumers that also need to check a shutdown flag or do periodic work.
- Choose by access pattern: read-mostly lists use copy-on-write, shared maps use `ConcurrentHashMap`, sorted data uses skip lists, pipelines use `BlockingQueue`.
- In Spring Boot, prefer a message broker (Kafka, RabbitMQ) when the queue must survive restarts or span several instances; in-memory queues lose data on crash.

### Compare with
| Queue | Bounded | Blocking | Ordering | Typical use |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ArrayBlockingQueue | Always | Yes | FIFO | Bounded pipelines, thread pools |
| LinkedBlockingQueue | Optional | Yes | FIFO | Pools (bounded), pipelines |
| PriorityBlockingQueue | No | `take` blocks | By priority | Priority jobs |
| DelayQueue | No | `take` waits for expiry | By delay | Scheduled retries |
| SynchronousQueue | Zero capacity | Yes | Handoff | Cached thread pools |
| ConcurrentLinkedQueue | No | No | FIFO | Non-blocking, high-throughput queues |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
Build OrderHub's in-memory invoice pipeline: a reader stage parses order lines, a pricing stage multiplies quantity by unit price, and a writer stage sums totals. Acceptance criteria:
- Two bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue`s (capacity 10) connect the three stages, each stage on its own thread.
- Each stage forwards a poison pill to the next and stops.
- Lines `"A,2,500"`, `"B,1,1250"`, `"C,4,99"` give a printed total of `2646` cents and `3 lines`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class InvoicePipeline {

    record Line(String sku, int qty, long unitCents) { }
    record Priced(String sku, long totalCents) { }

    private static final Line LINE_END = new Line("", 0, 0);
    private static final Priced PRICED_END = new Priced("", 0);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> input = List.of("A,2,500", "B,1,1250", "C,4,99");
        BlockingQueue<Line> parsed = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10);
        BlockingQueue<Priced> priced = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10);
        long[] result = new long[2];   // written by the writer, read by main after close()

        try (ExecutorService stages = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3)) {
            stages.submit(() -> {                                    // reader
                for (String raw : input) {
                    String[] f = raw.split(",");
                    parsed.put(new Line(f[0], Integer.parseInt(f[1]), Long.parseLong(f[2])));
                }
                parsed.put(LINE_END);
                return null;
            });
            stages.submit(() -> {                                    // pricing
                while (true) {
                    Line l = parsed.take();
                    if (l == LINE_END) { priced.put(PRICED_END); return null; }
                    priced.put(new Priced(l.sku(), l.qty() * l.unitCents()));
                }
            });
            stages.submit(() -> {                                    // writer
                long total = 0;
                int lines = 0;
                while (true) {
                    Priced p = priced.take();
                    if (p == PRICED_END) break;
                    total += p.totalCents();
                    lines++;
                }
                result[0] = total;
                result[1] = lines;
                return null;
            });
        }   // close() waits for all stages; that also makes result[] visible to main
        System.out.println("total=" + result[0] + " cents, " + result[1] + " lines");
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
total=2646 cents, 3 lines
```
2 x 500 + 1 x 1250 + 4 x 99 = 1000 + 1250 + 396 = 2646. Bounded queues mean a slow writer automatically slows the earlier stages instead of letting memory grow.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What do `put` and `take` do on a BlockingQueue?
A: `put` waits while the queue is full; `take` waits while it is empty.

Q: On a full BlockingQueue, how do `add`, `offer` and `put` differ?
A: `add` throws `IllegalStateException`, `offer` returns false, and `put` blocks until space is available.

Q: When is CopyOnWriteArrayList a good choice?
A: For small, read-mostly lists such as listeners, because reads and iteration are lock-free but every write copies the array.

Q: Why is an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue risky between pipeline stages?
A: Producers are never slowed down, so the queue can grow until memory runs out; a bounded queue provides back-pressure.

Q: How do you stop N consumers of a BlockingQueue after all work is done?
A: Put N poison-pill elements after the last real item, one per consumer.

Q: Which concurrent collection provides a thread-safe sorted map?
A: `ConcurrentSkipListMap`.

**Related:** [[ConcurrentHashMap Internals]] · [[wait, notify and notifyAll]] · [[ExecutorService and Thread Pools]] · [[ConcurrentModificationException]]

# Coding drill: Concurrency problems

Ten classic concurrency tasks from Java interviews. For each, explain aloud which shared state exists, which threads touch it and how you guarantee correctness, before you write code. Every solution prints deterministic output so you can check yourself.

## 1. Print odd and even numbers with two threads
Two threads print 1 to 10 in order: one prints odd numbers, the other even numbers.

Hint: a shared counter guarded by one lock, `wait` in a `while` loop until it is your turn, then `notifyAll`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
public class OddEvenPrinter {
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    private final int max;
    private int next = 1;                               // guarded by lock
    private final StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();

    OddEvenPrinter(int max) { this.max = max; }

    void run(boolean odd, String name) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            while (true) {
                while (next <= max && (next % 2 == 1) != odd) {
                    try {
                        lock.wait();
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                        return;
                    }
                }
                if (next > max) {
                    lock.notifyAll();                   // release the other thread too
                    return;
                }
                out.append(name).append(':').append(next).append(' ');
                next++;
                lock.notifyAll();
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        OddEvenPrinter p = new OddEvenPrinter(10);
        Thread even = new Thread(() -> p.run(false, "E"));
        Thread odd = new Thread(() -> p.run(true, "O"));
        even.start();
        odd.start();
        even.join();
        odd.join();
        System.out.println(p.out.toString().trim());
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
O:1 E:2 O:3 E:4 O:5 E:6 O:7 E:8 O:9 E:10
```
The counter decides whose turn it is, so start order does not matter. The final `notifyAll` stops the other thread from waiting forever once the counter passes `max`.
</details>

## 2. Thread-safe singleton, three ways
Write a lazily created, thread-safe singleton using the holder idiom, an enum, and double-checked locking. Prove from 8 threads that only one instance exists.

Hint: double-checked locking needs `volatile`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.IdentityHashMap;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class Singletons {

    // 1. Holder idiom: lazy, thread-safe through class initialization, no locking on reads
    static final class Config {
        private Config() { }
        private static final class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }
        static Config get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
    }

    // 2. Enum: thread-safe, serialization-safe and reflection-safe (Effective Java's favourite)
    enum Registry { INSTANCE }

    // 3. Double-checked locking
    static final class Connection {
        private static volatile Connection instance;      // volatile is essential
        private Connection() { }
        static Connection get() {
            Connection local = instance;
            if (local == null) {
                synchronized (Connection.class) {
                    local = instance;
                    if (local == null) instance = local = new Connection();
                }
            }
            return local;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<Object> seen = Collections.synchronizedSet(Collections.newSetFromMap(new IdentityHashMap<>()));
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8)) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
                pool.submit(() -> {
                    seen.add(Config.get());
                    seen.add(Registry.INSTANCE);
                    seen.add(Connection.get());
                });
            }
        }
        System.out.println("distinct instances across 8 threads: " + seen.size());
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
distinct instances across 8 threads: 3
```
Three classes, one instance each. In an interview, recommend the holder idiom or enum; explain DCL to show you understand why `volatile` is needed. In Spring you would simply use a singleton-scoped bean.
</details>

## 3. Run initialization exactly once
This "init once" code sometimes runs `init` twice when many threads call `ensureInit` together. Find the bug and fix it without `synchronized`.

```java
private volatile boolean initialized;
void ensureInit() {
    if (!initialized) {
        init();
        initialized = true;
    }
}
```

Hint: `volatile` gives visibility, not atomicity of check-then-act.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Two threads can both read `false` before either sets `true`. Make the check and the claim one atomic step with `compareAndSet`:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class InitOnce {
    private final AtomicBoolean started = new AtomicBoolean();
    private final AtomicInteger initRuns = new AtomicInteger();

    void ensureInit() {
        if (started.compareAndSet(false, true)) {    // only one thread wins
            init();
        }
    }

    private void init() { initRuns.incrementAndGet(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        InitOnce service = new InitOnce();
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16)) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) pool.submit(service::ensureInit);
        }
        System.out.println("init ran " + service.initRuns.get() + " time(s)");
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
init ran 1 time(s)
```
Caveat to mention: losers return immediately, possibly before `init()` has finished. If callers must wait for initialization to complete, use the holder idiom, a `CountDownLatch`, or a `CompletableFuture` that every caller joins.
</details>

## 4. Build a one-shot latch with wait and notifyAll
Write `SimpleLatch(int count)` with `countDown()` and `await()`, behaving like `CountDownLatch`. Three workers count down; `main` waits for all of them.

Hint: one counter, one monitor, `while (count > 0) wait();`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
public class SimpleLatch {
    private int count;                        // guarded by this

    public SimpleLatch(int count) {
        if (count < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("count < 0");
        this.count = count;
    }

    public synchronized void countDown() {
        if (count > 0 && --count == 0) {
            notifyAll();                      // open the gate for every waiter
        }
    }

    public synchronized void await() throws InterruptedException {
        while (count > 0) {
            wait();
        }
    }

    public synchronized int getCount() { return count; }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SimpleLatch ready = new SimpleLatch(3);
        for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
            new Thread(ready::countDown, "worker-" + i).start();
        }
        ready.await();
        System.out.println("all 3 workers ready, count=" + ready.getCount());
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
all 3 workers ready, count=0
```
This is the essence of `CountDownLatch`, which is built on `AbstractQueuedSynchronizer` instead of a monitor.
</details>

## 5. Find and fix the deadlock
Two threads call `transfer(a, b, 1)` and `transfer(b, a, 1)` 1000 times each. Explain the deadlock and fix it, assuming accounts have no id field.

```java
static void transfer(Account from, Account to, int amount) {
    synchronized (from) {
        synchronized (to) {
            from.balance -= amount;
            to.balance += amount;
        }
    }
}
```

Hint: impose a global order; without ids, use `System.identityHashCode` plus a tie-breaker lock.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Thread 1 locks `a` then waits for `b`; thread 2 locks `b` then waits for `a`: circular wait. Always lock in the same global order:

```java
public class DeadlockFix {

    static final class Account { int balance; Account(int b) { balance = b; } }

    private static final Object TIE_LOCK = new Object();

    static void transfer(Account from, Account to, int amount) {
        int h1 = System.identityHashCode(from);
        int h2 = System.identityHashCode(to);
        if (h1 < h2) {
            synchronized (from) { synchronized (to) { move(from, to, amount); } }
        } else if (h1 > h2) {
            synchronized (to) { synchronized (from) { move(from, to, amount); } }
        } else {
            synchronized (TIE_LOCK) {         // rare hash tie: serialize through a third lock
                synchronized (from) { synchronized (to) { move(from, to, amount); } }
            }
        }
    }

    private static void move(Account from, Account to, int amount) {
        from.balance -= amount;
        to.balance += amount;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Account a = new Account(100);
        Account b = new Account(100);
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) transfer(a, b, 1); });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) transfer(b, a, 1); });
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
        System.out.println("done: a=" + a.balance + " b=" + b.balance);
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
done: a=100 b=100
```
This is the textbook solution from "Java Concurrency in Practice". With entity ids, simply order by id.
</details>

## 6. Parallel sum with an ExecutorService
Sum the numbers 1 to 1,000,000 by splitting the range into 4 chunks processed in parallel.

Hint: one `Callable<Long>` per chunk, `invokeAll`, then add the results.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class ParallelSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        long n = 1_000_000;
        int chunks = 4;
        List<Callable<Long>> tasks = new ArrayList<>();
        long size = n / chunks;
        for (int c = 0; c < chunks; c++) {
            long from = c * size + 1;
            long to = (c == chunks - 1) ? n : from + size - 1;
            tasks.add(() -> {
                long sum = 0;
                for (long i = from; i <= to; i++) sum += i;
                return sum;
            });
        }
        long total = 0;
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(chunks)) {
            for (Future<Long> f : pool.invokeAll(tasks)) total += f.get();
        }
        System.out.println("sum=" + total + " formula=" + n * (n + 1) / 2);
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
sum=500000500000 formula=500000500000
```
Each task works on its own range and local variable, so no shared mutable state exists until the results are combined on the main thread. Mention that `LongStream.rangeClosed(1, n).parallel().sum()` does the same with the common `ForkJoinPool`.
</details>

## 7. Cheapest price from several suppliers with a timeout
Ask three suppliers in parallel for a price. Supplier A answers 1999 after 100 ms, B answers 1899 after 50 ms, C takes 2 seconds. Use a 300 ms limit per supplier and return the cheapest of those that answered.

Hint: `completeOnTimeout` with an empty `Optional`, then `allOf`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class CheapestPrice {

    static CompletableFuture<Optional<Integer>> quote(ExecutorService io, long delayMs, int price) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
                    try { Thread.sleep(delayMs); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
                    return Optional.of(price);
                }, io)
                .completeOnTimeout(Optional.empty(), 300, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)
                .exceptionally(ex -> Optional.empty());           // a failing supplier is ignored too
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
        List<CompletableFuture<Optional<Integer>>> quotes = List.of(
                quote(io, 100, 1999), quote(io, 50, 1899), quote(io, 2000, 1500));
        CompletableFuture.allOf(quotes.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).join();
        List<Integer> answered = quotes.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).flatMap(Optional::stream).toList();
        int cheapest = answered.stream().min(Integer::compare).orElseThrow();
        System.out.println("cheapest=" + cheapest + " from " + answered.size() + " responding suppliers");
        io.shutdownNow();                                          // interrupt supplier C
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
cheapest=1899 from 2 responding suppliers
```
Supplier C would have been cheapest but missed the deadline; that is the business trade-off timeouts encode. `shutdownNow` interrupts its sleeping thread so the program exits promptly.
</details>

## 8. Three threads print 1 to 9 in turn
Thread T1 prints 1, 4, 7; T2 prints 2, 5, 8; T3 prints 3, 6, 9, so the output is 1 to 9 in order.

Hint: generalise task 1: thread k may print when `(next - 1) % 3 == k`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
public class RoundRobinPrinter {
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    private final int threads;
    private final int max;
    private int next = 1;                                   // guarded by lock
    private final StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();

    RoundRobinPrinter(int threads, int max) { this.threads = threads; this.max = max; }

    void run(int k) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            while (true) {
                while (next <= max && (next - 1) % threads != k) {
                    try { lock.wait(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); return; }
                }
                if (next > max) { lock.notifyAll(); return; }
                out.append("T").append(k + 1).append('=').append(next).append(' ');
                next++;
                lock.notifyAll();                           // notify alone could wake the wrong thread
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        RoundRobinPrinter p = new RoundRobinPrinter(3, 9);
        Thread[] ts = new Thread[3];
        for (int k = 2; k >= 0; k--) {                      // start in reverse order on purpose
            final int id = k;
            ts[k] = new Thread(() -> p.run(id));
            ts[k].start();
        }
        for (Thread t : ts) t.join();
        System.out.println(p.out.toString().trim());
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
T1=1 T2=2 T3=3 T1=4 T2=5 T3=6 T1=7 T2=8 T3=9
```
With three waiters on one monitor, `notify` could wake a thread whose turn it is not, and the signal would be lost; `notifyAll` lets every thread re-check.
</details>

## 9. Time out and cancel a slow task
Submit a task that would take 5 seconds, wait at most 200 ms for it, then cancel it and prove the task saw the interruption.

Hint: `future.get(timeout, unit)`, `future.cancel(true)`, and a latch counted down in the task's `catch (InterruptedException)`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

public class TimeoutCancel {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        CountDownLatch sawInterrupt = new CountDownLatch(1);
        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor()) {
            Future<String> report = pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(5_000);                    // slow report generation
                    return "report";
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    sawInterrupt.countDown();
                    throw e;
                }
            });
            try {
                report.get(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            } catch (TimeoutException e) {
                boolean cancelled = report.cancel(true);    // true = interrupt the running thread
                System.out.println("timed out, cancel returned " + cancelled + ", isCancelled=" + report.isCancelled());
            }
            System.out.println("task observed interrupt: " + sawInterrupt.await(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS));
        }
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
timed out, cancel returned true, isCancelled=true
task observed interrupt: true
```
`get` with a timeout only stops waiting; `cancel(true)` is what interrupts the worker, and it only helps because `sleep` responds to interruption. Code stuck in non-interruptible I/O would keep running.
</details>

## 10. A memoizer that computes each key once
Many threads request product details by id; the slow loader must run at most once per id, and concurrent callers for the same id must share the result.

Hint: cache `CompletableFuture`s, not values, in a `ConcurrentHashMap`, so the bin is held only for an instant.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.Executor;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.function.Function;

public class Memoizer<K, V> {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, CompletableFuture<V>> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final Function<K, V> loader;
    private final Executor executor;

    public Memoizer(Function<K, V> loader, Executor executor) {
        this.loader = loader;
        this.executor = executor;
    }

    public CompletableFuture<V> get(K key) {
        CompletableFuture<V> future = cache.computeIfAbsent(key,
                k -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> loader.apply(k), executor));
        // Do not cache failures forever: remove so a later call can retry
        future.whenComplete((v, ex) -> { if (ex != null) cache.remove(key, future); });
        return future;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AtomicInteger loads = new AtomicInteger();
        try (ExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
             ExecutorService callers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16)) {
            Memoizer<String, String> details = new Memoizer<>(id -> {
                loads.incrementAndGet();
                try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
                return "details of " + id;
            }, io);

            CompletableFuture<?>[] results = new CompletableFuture<?>[50];
            for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
                results[i] = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> details.get("p1").join(), callers);
            }
            CompletableFuture.allOf(results).join();
            System.out.println(details.get("p1").join() + ", loader ran " + loads.get() + " time(s)");
        }
    }
}
```
Output:
```text
details of p1, loader ran 1 time(s)
```
`computeIfAbsent` only creates the future (fast), so the map bin is locked for microseconds; the slow load happens on the `io` executor and all 50 callers wait on the same future. This is the "Memoizer" from "Java Concurrency in Practice", modernised with `CompletableFuture`. Note that try-with-resources closes `callers` first and `io` second (reverse order of declaration).
</details>

# IoC and Dependency Injection

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: Spring 1.0 (2004); annotation config Spring 2.5; Java config Spring 3.0**

> **30-second interview answer:** Inversion of Control means my objects do not create or look up their own collaborators; something outside (the Spring container) creates them and wires them together. Dependency injection is the way Spring implements IoC: dependencies are passed in through the constructor (preferred), a setter or a field. The result is loosely coupled classes that depend on interfaces, are easy to unit test with fakes, and can be reconfigured without code changes.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A film production versus a one-person home video.

| Film production | Spring |
|---|---|
| The actor does not hire the camera crew or buy props | A class does not call `new` on its collaborators |
| The production office books crew and props for each scene | The IoC container creates and wires beans |
| The actor's contract lists what they need ("a stunt double") | The constructor parameters declare dependencies |
| Swap the stunt double without rewriting the script | Swap an implementation without changing the class |
| Rehearsal with stand-ins | Unit tests with mocks or fakes |

### What it is
- **Dependency:** an object another object needs to do its job (`OrderService` needs a `PaymentGateway`).
- **Inversion of Control (IoC):** the general principle that the framework, not your code, controls object creation and flow ("don't call us, we'll call you").
- **Dependency Injection (DI):** a concrete form of IoC where dependencies are *given* to an object instead of being created or looked up by it.
- **Service locator:** the alternative form of IoC where an object asks a registry for its dependencies (`context.getBean(...)`). It still hides dependencies inside the class, so DI is preferred.
- **IoC container:** the Spring component (`ApplicationContext`) that holds bean definitions, creates beans and injects them.

### Why it exists
Without DI, `OrderService` writes `new StripePaymentGateway(new HttpClient(...))`. Now the service is welded to Stripe, to a real HTTP client and to its configuration. You cannot test it without hitting Stripe, and switching providers means editing every class that calls `new`. DI moves the "who uses which implementation" decision into one place (configuration), so business classes only state what they need.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Boot as "SpringApplication.run"
    participant Ctx as "ApplicationContext"
    participant Def as "Bean definitions"
    participant OS as "OrderService"
    participant PG as "StripePaymentGateway"
    Boot->>Ctx: create and refresh
    Ctx->>Def: scan @Component classes and @Bean methods
    Ctx->>PG: instantiate (no dependencies)
    Ctx->>OS: instantiate with constructor(PaymentGateway)
    Note over Ctx,OS: Spring resolves the parameter type PaymentGateway and passes the StripePaymentGateway bean
    Ctx-->>Boot: context ready, beans in singleton cache
```

Step by step:
1. **Read configuration.** Component scanning and `@Configuration` classes produce `BeanDefinition` objects (class, scope, dependencies, init and destroy methods). No objects exist yet.
2. **Build the dependency graph.** For each singleton, Spring looks at the constructor parameters and finds beans that match by type (then by qualifier or name).
3. **Create in dependency order.** Dependencies are created first, then the dependent bean is constructed with them.
4. **Post-process.** `BeanPostProcessor`s may wrap the bean in a proxy (for `@Transactional`, `@Async`, AOP). The proxy, not the raw object, is what gets injected elsewhere.
5. **Cache.** Singletons are stored in the singleton cache; every later injection gets the same instance.

```text
Without DI                         With DI
OrderService ---new---> Stripe      OrderService ---needs---> PaymentGateway (interface)
     |                                                          ^
     +---new---> HttpClient          Container decides: StripePaymentGateway or FakePaymentGateway
```

## 3. Code Example
### Code
A plain-Java version first, so you can see that DI is just "pass collaborators in", with no framework needed.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class DiDemo {

    interface PaymentGateway {
        String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount);
    }

    static class StripePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
        public String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount) {
            return "stripe-ok:" + customer + ":" + amount;
        }
    }

    // Test double: records calls instead of charging real money
    static class FakePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
        final List<String> calls = new ArrayList<>();
        public String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount) {
            calls.add(customer + "=" + amount);
            return "fake-ok";
        }
    }

    // Tightly coupled: creates its own dependency
    static class CoupledOrderService {
        private final PaymentGateway gateway = new StripePaymentGateway();
        String place(String customer, BigDecimal amount) { return gateway.charge(customer, amount); }
    }

    // Dependency injection: the dependency comes in through the constructor
    static class OrderService {
        private final PaymentGateway gateway;
        OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) { this.gateway = gateway; }
        String place(String customer, BigDecimal amount) { return gateway.charge(customer, amount); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new CoupledOrderService().place("alice", new BigDecimal("20.00")));

        // "Production wiring": the composition root chooses the implementation
        OrderService prod = new OrderService(new StripePaymentGateway());
        System.out.println(prod.place("bob", new BigDecimal("15.50")));

        // "Test wiring": same class, different collaborator
        FakePaymentGateway fake = new FakePaymentGateway();
        OrderService test = new OrderService(fake);
        System.out.println(test.place("carol", new BigDecimal("9.99")));
        System.out.println("fake recorded: " + fake.calls);
    }
}
```

Now the same wiring done by Spring Boot in OrderHub:

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/payment/PaymentGateway.java
package com.orderhub.payment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface PaymentGateway {
    String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount);
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/payment/StripePaymentGateway.java
package com.orderhub.payment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component                       // Spring registers a bean definition for this class
public class StripePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    @Override
    public String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount) {
        return "stripe-ok:" + customer + ":" + amount;
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import com.orderhub.payment.PaymentGateway;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;

    // Single constructor: Spring uses it automatically, no @Autowired needed (since Spring 4.3)
    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    public String place(String customer, BigDecimal amount) {
        return gateway.charge(customer, amount);
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/OrderHubApplication.java
package com.orderhub;

import com.orderhub.order.OrderService;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;

@SpringBootApplication
public class OrderHubApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(OrderHubApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    CommandLineRunner demo(OrderService orders) {      // dependency injected into a @Bean method
        return args -> System.out.println(orders.place("dave", new BigDecimal("42.00")));
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `CoupledOrderService` creates `StripePaymentGateway` itself, so a test cannot avoid Stripe and switching providers means editing this class.
- `OrderService` only declares "I need a `PaymentGateway`". In `main`, the code that builds objects (called the **composition root**) decides which implementation to pass. In production it passes Stripe, in the test it passes a fake that records calls.
- In the Spring version, `@Component` and `@Service` let component scanning find the classes. The container sees that `OrderService`'s only constructor needs a `PaymentGateway`, finds exactly one bean of that type, and passes it in. Spring is just an automatic composition root.
- The `CommandLineRunner` bean runs after the context starts, so you see the call happen.

### Output
Plain Java:
```text
stripe-ok:alice:20.00
stripe-ok:bob:15.50
fake-ok
fake recorded: [carol=9.99]
```
Spring Boot (after the startup banner and logs):
```text
stripe-ok:dave:42.00
```

### Compare
| Creating dependencies yourself | Dependency injection |
|---|---|
| `new StripePaymentGateway()` inside the service | Constructor parameter of type `PaymentGateway` |
| Hidden dependencies; you read the body to find them | Dependencies visible in the constructor signature |
| Tests need the real implementation | Tests pass a fake or a Mockito mock |
| Changing provider edits many classes | Changing provider edits one configuration |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is Inversion of Control?**
   A design principle where control over object creation and program flow moves from your code to a framework. Instead of a class building its collaborators, the container builds them and calls your code. Spring's container is an IoC container.
2. **What is dependency injection and how is it related to IoC?**
   DI is one way to achieve IoC: an object receives its dependencies from outside, through a constructor, setter or field. The other way is a service locator, where the object asks a registry. DI is preferred because dependencies stay visible and the class has no framework lookup code.
3. **What are the benefits of DI?**
   Loose coupling (code depends on interfaces), easy unit testing with fakes, central configuration of which implementation is used, and lifecycle management (singletons, proxies, cleanup) handled by the container.

**Follow-up**
4. **Is DI only possible with Spring?**
   No. DI is a pattern; passing collaborators into a constructor by hand is DI. Spring, Guice, Dagger, Micronaut and Quarkus automate the wiring. Interviewers like hearing that you could write the composition root yourself.
5. **How does Spring know what to inject into a constructor?**
   It resolves each parameter by type against the registered bean definitions. If exactly one bean matches it is used; if several match, Spring narrows down by `@Qualifier`, then `@Primary`, then by matching the parameter name to a bean name. If none or still several remain, startup fails with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` or `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`.
6. **Why is `context.getBean(...)` inside business code considered a smell?**
   It is the service-locator style: the class hides its real dependencies, depends on the Spring API, and is harder to test. Inject the dependency instead; use `ObjectProvider` only when you genuinely need lazy or optional lookup.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **A colleague says "DI makes code slower because of reflection". Answer them.**
   Reflection is used once, at startup, to create and wire beans. After that, method calls between beans are ordinary Java calls (or a proxy call when AOP is involved). Runtime cost is negligible; startup cost is the real trade-off, which Spring AOT and native images reduce.
8. **You have a class with eight constructor parameters. Is DI the problem?**
   No, DI exposed the problem. A long constructor means the class has too many responsibilities. Split it into smaller services or group related collaborators. Field injection would only hide the smell.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** that you understand DI as a design idea (dependencies passed in, interfaces, testability), not just "I put `@Autowired` on things".

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Calling `new` on a Spring-managed class | The object is not a bean: no injection, no `@Transactional` proxy | Inject it instead of creating it |
| Depending on concrete classes everywhere | Cannot swap implementations or mock easily | Depend on interfaces at boundaries (payments, email, storage) |
| `applicationContext.getBean(...)` in services | Hidden dependencies, framework coupling | Constructor injection; `ObjectProvider` for lazy cases |
| Creating an interface for every class "for DI" | Noise with no second implementation | Introduce interfaces where variation or test seams are real |

### Best practices
- Use constructor injection with `final` fields; one constructor means no annotation is needed.
- Keep business classes free of Spring APIs where practical; the container should be an implementation detail.
- Put interfaces at architectural boundaries (external systems), not between every pair of classes.
- If a constructor grows past four or five parameters, treat it as a design warning.

### Compare with
| Approach | How dependencies arrive | Visibility | Testability |
|---|---|---|---|
| `new` inside the class | Created internally | Hidden | Poor |
| Service locator | Looked up from a registry | Hidden | Medium |
| Dependency injection | Passed in from outside | Explicit | Excellent |
| Static factories or singletons | Global access | Hidden | Poor |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Phase 5 left `ProductService` creating its repository with `new InMemoryProductRepository()`. Refactor:
1. Extract a `ProductRepository` interface (`findAll`, `findById`, `save`, `deleteById`), make `InMemoryProductRepository` implement it and annotate it with `@Repository`.
2. Make `ProductService` take `ProductRepository` through its constructor.
3. Write a plain JUnit test (no Spring) that builds `ProductService` with a hand-written fake repository.

Acceptance criteria: no `new InMemoryProductRepository()` remains in production code; the app starts; the unit test runs without a Spring context.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductRepository.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public interface ProductRepository {
    List<Product> findAll();
    Optional<Product> findById(long id);
    Product save(Product product);
    void deleteById(long id);
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/InMemoryProductRepository.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

@Repository
public class InMemoryProductRepository implements ProductRepository {
    private final Map<Long, Product> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final AtomicLong ids = new AtomicLong();

    public List<Product> findAll() { return new ArrayList<>(store.values()); }
    public Optional<Product> findById(long id) { return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id)); }
    public Product save(Product p) {
        Product saved = p.id() == null ? p.withId(ids.incrementAndGet()) : p;
        store.put(saved.id(), saved);
        return saved;
    }
    public void deleteById(long id) { store.remove(id); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductService.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository repository;

    public ProductService(ProductRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public List<Product> findAll() { return repository.findAll(); }

    public Product get(long id) {
        return repository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(id));
    }

    public Product create(Product product) { return repository.save(product); }
}

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductServiceTest.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class ProductServiceTest {

    static class FakeRepo implements ProductRepository {
        final Map<Long, Product> data = new HashMap<>();
        public List<Product> findAll() { return new ArrayList<>(data.values()); }
        public Optional<Product> findById(long id) { return Optional.ofNullable(data.get(id)); }
        public Product save(Product p) { Product s = p.withId(1L); data.put(1L, s); return s; }
        public void deleteById(long id) { data.remove(id); }
    }

    @Test
    void createsAndFindsProduct() {
        ProductService service = new ProductService(new FakeRepo());   // no Spring needed
        Product saved = service.create(new Product(null, "Keyboard", new BigDecimal("49.90"), 10));
        assertEquals("Keyboard", service.get(saved.id()).name());
    }

    @Test
    void missingProductThrows() {
        ProductService service = new ProductService(new FakeRepo());
        assertThrows(ProductNotFoundException.class, () -> service.get(99));
    }
}
```
`Product` is the Phase 5 record `Product(Long id, String name, BigDecimal price, int stock)` with a `withId` helper that returns a copy with the new id. The test proves the service no longer depends on a concrete repository or on Spring.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What does Inversion of Control mean in Spring?
A: Object creation and wiring move from your classes to the Spring container, which builds beans and passes them their collaborators.

Q: How is dependency injection related to IoC?
A: DI is one implementation of IoC in which dependencies are passed in from outside (constructor, setter or field) instead of created or looked up.

Q: How does Spring resolve a constructor dependency?
A: By type first, then by `@Qualifier`, then `@Primary`, then parameter name; zero or multiple remaining candidates fail startup.

Q: Why is calling `context.getBean()` from business code a smell in DI?
A: It is the service-locator pattern: it hides dependencies, couples code to Spring and makes testing harder.

Q: Does dependency injection slow down method calls at runtime?
A: No; reflection is used at startup for wiring, and later calls are plain Java calls or proxy calls.

**Related:** [[ApplicationContext and Bean Definitions]] · [[Constructor vs Setter vs Field Injection]] · [[@Qualifier, @Primary and Multiple Beans]]

# ApplicationContext and Bean Definitions

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: Spring 1.0 (BeanFactory and ApplicationContext); AnnotationConfigApplicationContext Spring 3.0**

> **30-second interview answer:** The `ApplicationContext` is Spring's IoC container. It first collects bean definitions, which are recipes describing each bean (class, scope, dependencies, lazy flag, init and destroy methods), from component scanning, `@Configuration` classes and auto-configuration. Then, during `refresh()`, it runs factory post-processors that can change those recipes, registers bean post-processors, and eagerly creates all non-lazy singletons. It extends the basic `BeanFactory` with events, internationalisation, resource loading and environment support.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
An architect's blueprints versus the finished houses.

| Construction | Spring |
|---|---|
| Blueprint for each house type | `BeanDefinition` |
| Planning office that can edit blueprints before building | `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` |
| Inspectors who check and decorate each house after it is built | `BeanPostProcessor` |
| The construction company that builds from blueprints | `BeanFactory` |
| The whole estate office: building plus utilities, mail, notices | `ApplicationContext` |
| Show homes built on day one | Eager singletons created at startup |

### What it is
- **BeanFactory:** the core container interface. It can register definitions and create beans lazily on `getBean`.
- **ApplicationContext:** a sub-interface of `BeanFactory` that adds eager singleton creation, automatic registration of post-processors, events (`ApplicationEventPublisher`), message sources (i18n), resource loading and the `Environment` (profiles and properties). In practice you always use an `ApplicationContext`.
- **BeanDefinition:** metadata for one bean: bean class or factory method, scope, constructor arguments, property values, `lazyInit`, `primary`, `dependsOn`, init and destroy method names.
- **Bean name:** the unique id in the container. Defaults: class name with a lower-case first letter for `@Component` (`orderService`), method name for `@Bean`.
- **Common implementations:** `AnnotationConfigApplicationContext` (plain Java config), `AnnotationConfigServletWebServerApplicationContext` (what Spring Boot creates for a servlet web app), `ReactiveWebServerApplicationContext` (WebFlux).

### Why it exists
Separating "what to build" (definitions) from "building it" (instantiation) lets Spring inspect and modify the whole plan before any object exists. That is how placeholders like `${orderhub.currency}` get resolved, how `@Configuration` classes get parsed, how auto-configuration adds beans conditionally, and how Spring detects missing dependencies early at startup instead of at the first request.

## 2. How It Works
The heart of the container is `AbstractApplicationContext.refresh()`:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["1. prepareRefresh: environment, property sources"] --> B["2. obtainFreshBeanFactory"]
    B --> C["3. invokeBeanFactoryPostProcessors"]
    C --> C1["ConfigurationClassPostProcessor: scan packages, parse @Configuration, @Bean, @Import, auto-config"]
    C --> C2["PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer: resolve placeholders"]
    C1 --> D["4. registerBeanPostProcessors"]
    D --> E["5. initMessageSource, initApplicationEventMulticaster"]
    E --> F["6. onRefresh: Boot starts the embedded web server here"]
    F --> G["7. registerListeners"]
    G --> H["8. finishBeanFactoryInitialization: create all non-lazy singletons"]
    H --> I["9. finishRefresh: publish ContextRefreshedEvent"]
```

Key points:
- **Phase A, definitions only (steps 1 to 3).** `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor` is itself a `BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor`. It is what processes `@ComponentScan`, `@Configuration`, `@Bean`, `@Import` and Boot's `@EnableAutoConfiguration`. At this point the registry holds only definitions.
- **Phase B, objects (step 8).** For each non-lazy singleton: instantiate, inject dependencies, run `Aware` callbacks, run `BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization`, init callbacks, then `postProcessAfterInitialization` (where AOP proxies are created).
- **Fail fast.** Because singletons are created eagerly, a missing bean or a circular constructor dependency fails at startup, not at 3 a.m. under traffic.
- **Hierarchy.** Contexts can have a parent; a child sees parent beans but not the reverse (used by classic Spring MVC with a root and a servlet context, and by Spring Cloud).

```text
BeanDefinitionRegistry (map: name -> BeanDefinition)
  "orderService"  -> class=OrderService, scope=singleton, ctorArgs=[ref paymentGateway]
  "stripePaymentGateway" -> class=StripePaymentGateway, scope=singleton
  "clock" -> factoryBean=appConfig, factoryMethod=clock, scope=singleton
               |
               v  finishBeanFactoryInitialization
Singleton cache (map: name -> object)
  "orderService" -> OrderService@3f2a (or its proxy)
```

## 3. Code Example
### Code
Bootstrapping a context by hand (no Boot) shows the moving parts that Boot normally hides.

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/demo/ContextDemo.java
package com.orderhub.demo;

import java.time.Clock;
import java.util.Arrays;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.BeanDefinition;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.BeanFactoryPostProcessor;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.AnnotationConfigApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

public class ContextDemo {

    @Component
    static class InventoryService {
        InventoryService() { System.out.println("InventoryService created"); }
    }

    @Configuration
    static class AppConfig {
        @Bean
        Clock clock() {
            System.out.println("clock() called");
            return Clock.systemUTC();
        }

        // static: must run before other beans are created
        @Bean
        static BeanFactoryPostProcessor makeInventoryLazy() {
            return beanFactory -> {
                BeanDefinition bd = beanFactory.getBeanDefinition("contextDemo.InventoryService");
                System.out.println("BFPP sees definition, lazy before = " + bd.isLazyInit());
                bd.setLazyInit(true);           // change the recipe before any object exists
            };
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext();
        ctx.register(AppConfig.class, InventoryService.class);
        System.out.println("definitions before refresh: " + ctx.getBeanDefinitionCount() + " registered");
        ctx.refresh();                          // runs the 9 phases
        System.out.println("refresh done");

        System.out.println("app beans: " + Arrays.stream(ctx.getBeanDefinitionNames())
                .filter(n -> !n.startsWith("org.springframework"))
                .toList());

        ctx.getBean(InventoryService.class);    // lazy: created only now
        System.out.println("same clock twice: " + (ctx.getBean(Clock.class) == ctx.getBean(Clock.class)));
        ctx.close();
    }
}
```

In Spring Boot you can inspect the same data at runtime:

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/demo/BeanListing.java
package com.orderhub.demo;

import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationRunner;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class BeanListing {
    @Bean
    ApplicationRunner listOrderHubBeans(ApplicationContext ctx) {
        return args -> {
            System.out.println("context type: " + ctx.getClass().getSimpleName());
            for (String name : ctx.getBeanDefinitionNames()) {
                Object bean = ctx.getBean(name);
                if (bean.getClass().getName().startsWith("com.orderhub")) {
                    System.out.println(name + " -> " + bean.getClass().getSimpleName());
                }
            }
        };
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `register(...)` only adds bean definitions. The count printed before refresh includes Spring's own internal processors (registered by the annotation context) plus our two classes.
- `refresh()` runs `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor`, which parses `AppConfig` and finds two `@Bean` methods. Our `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` then edits the `InventoryService` definition to lazy before any bean is built. It is declared `static` so Spring can call it without first creating `AppConfig` (otherwise you get a warning and lose `@Configuration` processing benefits for that class).
- The nested class bean name is `contextDemo.InventoryService` because `AnnotationBeanNameGenerator` uses the short class name including the outer class.
- During `finishBeanFactoryInitialization`, `clock()` is called (eager singleton), but `InventoryService` is skipped because it is now lazy. It is created only on `getBean`.
- `getBean(Clock.class)` twice returns the same instance: singleton scope.
- In Boot, the context is `AnnotationConfigServletWebServerApplicationContext` for a Spring MVC app.

### Output
```text
definitions before refresh: 7 registered
BFPP sees definition, lazy before = false
clock() called
refresh done
app beans: [contextDemo.AppConfig, contextDemo.InventoryService, clock, makeInventoryLazy]
InventoryService created
same clock twice: true
```
Boot runner output (OrderHub, abbreviated to app beans):
```text
context type: AnnotationConfigServletWebServerApplicationContext
orderHubApplication -> OrderHubApplication$$SpringCGLIB$$0
productController -> ProductController
productService -> ProductService
inMemoryProductRepository -> InMemoryProductRepository
```

### Compare
| BeanFactory | ApplicationContext |
|---|---|
| Lazy: creates beans on first `getBean` | Eagerly creates non-lazy singletons at refresh |
| You register post-processors manually | Detects and registers post-processors automatically |
| No events, no i18n, no environment abstraction | Events, `MessageSource`, `Environment`, resource loading |
| Used inside frameworks or very constrained setups | What every real application uses |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is the ApplicationContext?**
   Spring's main IoC container interface. It holds bean definitions, creates and wires beans, and adds enterprise features such as events, internationalisation, the environment with profiles and properties, and resource loading.
2. **BeanFactory vs ApplicationContext?**
   `BeanFactory` is the minimal container with lazy creation. `ApplicationContext` extends it with eager singleton creation, automatic post-processor registration, events, message sources and environment support. Use `ApplicationContext` unless you are writing framework code.
3. **What is a bean definition?**
   A recipe for a bean: its class or factory method, scope, constructor arguments, dependencies, lazy and primary flags, and init and destroy methods. Spring builds all definitions first and creates objects from them later.

**Follow-up**
4. **What happens during `refresh()`?**
   Spring prepares the environment, loads definitions, runs `BeanFactoryPostProcessor`s (including the one that parses `@Configuration` and component scans), registers `BeanPostProcessor`s, initialises the event multicaster, lets Boot start the web server in `onRefresh`, creates all non-lazy singletons and publishes `ContextRefreshedEvent`.
5. **BeanFactoryPostProcessor vs BeanPostProcessor?**
   A `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` works on definitions before any regular bean exists (example: placeholder resolution, `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor`). A `BeanPostProcessor` works on bean instances after creation (example: `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor`, AOP proxy creators).
6. **How are default bean names chosen?**
   For `@Component` classes, the simple class name with the first letter lower-cased (`OrderService` becomes `orderService`; if the first two letters are upper-case, like `URLParser`, the name stays `URLParser`). For `@Bean` methods, the method name. Both can be overridden.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **Why should a `@Bean` method returning a `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` be static?**
   The post-processor must run before normal beans are created. If the method is an instance method, Spring has to instantiate the `@Configuration` class early, before its own processing is complete, which can break `@Autowired` and `@Value` inside that configuration. A static method lets Spring call it without the configuration instance.
8. **The app fails at startup with `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`. Why is that a good thing?**
   Eager singleton creation surfaces wiring errors during deployment instead of on the first request. Making beans lazy hides those problems until runtime, so global lazy initialisation is a trade-off between startup time and fail-fast safety.
9. **Can two beans have the same name?**
   Not in one context. Since Spring Boot 2.1, bean definition overriding is disabled by default, so a duplicate name fails startup with `BeanDefinitionOverrideException`. You can enable `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding`, but that usually hides a configuration mistake.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you can describe the two-phase model (definitions, then instances) and know where extension points plug in.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Non-static `@Bean` method for a `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` | Config class created too early, injection inside it misbehaves | Declare it `static` |
| Turning on global lazy init to "speed up startup" in production | Wiring errors appear on first request, first requests are slow | Use lazy init selectively, or only in dev |
| Injecting `ApplicationContext` everywhere to call `getBean` | Service-locator style, hidden dependencies | Inject the actual dependency |
| Relying on bean definition overriding | The last definition silently wins | Keep names unique; use `@Primary` or conditions |

### Best practices
- Think of startup in two phases; extension points that change *what* exists act on definitions, those that change *how a bean behaves* act on instances.
- Use the Actuator `beans` endpoint or the conditions report (`--debug`) to see what is actually in the context.
- Keep the context fail-fast: eager singletons, no overriding, no `getBean` in business code.

### Compare with
| Container type | Typical use |
|---|---|
| `AnnotationConfigApplicationContext` | Plain Spring with Java config, small tools, tests |
| `AnnotationConfigServletWebServerApplicationContext` | Spring Boot servlet (Spring MVC) apps |
| `ReactiveWebServerApplicationContext` | Spring Boot WebFlux apps |
| `GenericApplicationContext` | Programmatic registration, functional bean registration |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Add a startup report to OrderHub:
1. Create `StartupReport` in `com.orderhub.config`, a bean that implements `ApplicationListener<ContextRefreshedEvent>`.
2. When the context is refreshed, print how many bean definitions exist in total and list the names of beans whose class is in the `com.orderhub` package, sorted.
3. Mark `InMemoryProductRepository` with `@Lazy` and show that it still appears in the list (it is a definition) but is created only when `ProductService` needs it.

Acceptance criteria: the report prints once at startup; the list is sorted; you can explain why `@Lazy` on the repository changes nothing if `ProductService` is an eager singleton that depends on it.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/config/StartupReport.java
package com.orderhub.config;

import java.util.Arrays;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.ConfigurableListableBeanFactory;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationListener;
import org.springframework.context.ConfigurableApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.event.ContextRefreshedEvent;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class StartupReport implements ApplicationListener<ContextRefreshedEvent> {

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ContextRefreshedEvent event) {
        ConfigurableListableBeanFactory factory =
                ((ConfigurableApplicationContext) event.getApplicationContext()).getBeanFactory();
        System.out.println("total bean definitions: " + factory.getBeanDefinitionCount());
        Arrays.stream(factory.getBeanDefinitionNames())
                .filter(name -> {
                    String cls = factory.getBeanDefinition(name).getBeanClassName();
                    return cls != null && cls.startsWith("com.orderhub");
                })
                .sorted()
                .forEach(name -> System.out.println("  " + name));
    }
}
```
Sample output:
```text
total bean definitions: 312
  inMemoryProductRepository
  orderHubApplication
  productController
  productService
  startupReport
```
`@Lazy` on `InMemoryProductRepository` only delays creation until someone asks for it. `ProductService` is an eager singleton that needs the repository in its constructor, so the repository is created right away anyway. To truly defer it you would also put `@Lazy` on the constructor parameter, which injects a lazy-resolution proxy. `@Bean` methods have no bean class name in the definition (they use a factory method), which is why the filter checks for null.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What is a Spring bean definition?
A: Metadata describing how to create a bean: class or factory method, scope, dependencies, lazy and primary flags, init and destroy methods.

Q: What does `ApplicationContext` add on top of `BeanFactory`?
A: Eager singleton creation, automatic post-processor registration, events, message sources, the `Environment` and resource loading.

Q: What is the difference between a BeanFactoryPostProcessor and a BeanPostProcessor in Spring?
A: The first modifies bean definitions before beans are created; the second modifies or wraps bean instances after creation.

Q: In which refresh step are non-lazy singletons created in Spring?
A: `finishBeanFactoryInitialization`, after post-processors are registered and before `ContextRefreshedEvent` is published.

Q: What happens in Spring Boot when two bean definitions share a name?
A: Startup fails with `BeanDefinitionOverrideException`, because overriding is disabled by default since Boot 2.1.

**Related:** [[IoC and Dependency Injection]] · [[Bean Lifecycle Callbacks]] · [[@Configuration and @Bean]] · [[Conditional Beans]]

# Constructor vs Setter vs Field Injection

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: Spring 1.0 (constructor and setter); @Autowired Spring 2.5; implicit single-constructor injection Spring 4.3**

> **30-second interview answer:** Spring can inject through a constructor, a setter or directly into a field. I use constructor injection for required dependencies: fields can be `final`, the object is never half-built, dependencies are visible, and I can unit test with plain `new`. Setter injection fits optional or reconfigurable dependencies. Field injection is short but hides dependencies, prevents `final`, needs reflection for tests and is discouraged by the Spring team.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Three ways to equip a new delivery driver.

| Equipping the driver | Injection style |
|---|---|
| The van, keys and route sheet are handed over at hiring; no van, no job | Constructor injection: required, complete from the start |
| A phone charger they can be given later if available | Setter injection: optional, can change later |
| Someone secretly slips tools into the driver's pockets at night | Field injection: works, but nobody can see what the driver depends on |

### What it is
- **Constructor injection:** dependencies are constructor parameters. Spring calls the constructor with resolved beans. With a single constructor no annotation is needed (since Spring 4.3).
- **Setter injection:** Spring calls `@Autowired` setter methods after construction.
- **Field injection:** `@Autowired` on a field; Spring sets it with reflection after construction, even if it is private.
- **Required vs optional:** constructor parameters are required by default; for optional ones use `Optional<T>`, `ObjectProvider<T>` or `@Autowired(required = false)` on a setter.

### Why it exists
Each style solves a different need. Constructor injection guarantees a fully formed, immutable object. Setter injection allows optional dependencies and breaking circular dependencies (a design smell, but sometimes legacy reality). Field injection exists for brevity and was popular in early annotation-driven Spring, but its costs became clear as codebases grew.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as "Container"
    participant AAB as "AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor"
    participant B as "OrderService instance"
    C->>C: choose constructor (single, or the @Autowired one)
    C->>B: new OrderService(gateway, repo)  [constructor injection]
    C->>AAB: postProcessProperties(bean)
    AAB->>B: field.set(bean, auditLog)  [field injection, reflection]
    AAB->>B: setNotifier(notifier)  [setter injection]
    C->>B: init callbacks (@PostConstruct)
```

Details that matter in interviews:
- **Constructor choice.** One constructor: used automatically. Several: Spring uses the one marked `@Autowired`; if none is marked it falls back to a no-arg constructor or fails.
- **Field and setter injection happen after construction**, in `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor.postProcessProperties`. So inside the constructor, injected fields are still `null`.
- **Circular dependencies.** A needs B and B needs A. With constructor injection, Spring cannot create either first, and startup fails with `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`. With field or setter injection, Spring used to resolve it using an early reference from the "third-level" singleton cache. Since Spring Boot 2.6, circular references are **prohibited by default** (`spring.main.allow-circular-references=false`), so all styles fail unless you opt in.

```text
Constructor injection timeline     Field injection timeline
new OrderService(gw)  -> ready     new OrderService()  -> gw == null (half-built)
                                   reflection sets gw  -> ready
```

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/InjectionStyles.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import com.orderhub.payment.PaymentGateway;
import java.util.Optional;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

interface AuditLog { void record(String msg); }
interface SmsNotifier { void send(String msg); }

@Component
class ConsoleAuditLog implements AuditLog {
    public void record(String msg) { System.out.println("AUDIT " + msg); }
}

// 1. Field injection: short, but hidden and mutable
@Service
class FieldInjectedOrderService {
    @Autowired
    private PaymentGateway gateway;          // cannot be final; null inside the constructor

    FieldInjectedOrderService() {
        System.out.println("field style: gateway in constructor = " + gateway);
    }
}

// 2. Setter injection: good for optional collaborators
@Service
class SetterInjectedOrderService {
    private SmsNotifier sms;                 // optional; no SmsNotifier bean exists

    @Autowired(required = false)
    void setSms(SmsNotifier sms) { this.sms = sms; }

    void notifyCustomer(String msg) {
        if (sms != null) sms.send(msg);
        else System.out.println("setter style: no SMS notifier, skipping");
    }
}

// 3. Constructor injection: required deps final, optional ones explicit
@Service
class ConstructorInjectedOrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    private final AuditLog audit;
    private final Optional<SmsNotifier> sms;           // optional dependency
    private final ObjectProvider<SmsNotifier> smsLater; // lazy and optional lookup

    ConstructorInjectedOrderService(PaymentGateway gateway, AuditLog audit,
                                    Optional<SmsNotifier> sms,
                                    ObjectProvider<SmsNotifier> smsLater) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
        this.audit = audit;
        this.sms = sms;
        this.smsLater = smsLater;
        System.out.println("constructor style: gateway in constructor = "
                + gateway.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }

    void place(String customer) {
        audit.record("order for " + customer);
        sms.ifPresentOrElse(s -> s.send("thanks"), () -> System.out.println("no SMS (Optional)"));
        smsLater.ifAvailable(s -> s.send("thanks again"));
    }
}

// Runner to show the behaviour at startup
@Component
class InjectionRunner implements org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner {
    private final SetterInjectedOrderService setterStyle;
    private final ConstructorInjectedOrderService ctorStyle;

    InjectionRunner(SetterInjectedOrderService setterStyle, ConstructorInjectedOrderService ctorStyle) {
        this.setterStyle = setterStyle;
        this.ctorStyle = ctorStyle;
    }

    public void run(String... args) {
        setterStyle.notifyCustomer("shipped");
        ctorStyle.place("alice");
    }
}
```

The unit test shows the practical difference:

```java
// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/order/ConstructorInjectionTest.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

import com.orderhub.payment.PaymentGateway;
import java.util.Optional;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;

class ConstructorInjectionTest {
    @Test
    void placesOrderWithoutSpring() {
        AuditLog audit = mock(AuditLog.class);
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        ObjectProvider<SmsNotifier> none = mock(ObjectProvider.class);
        var service = new ConstructorInjectedOrderService(
                mock(PaymentGateway.class), audit, Optional.empty(), none);   // plain new

        service.place("bob");

        verify(audit).record("order for bob");
    }
    // A field-injected class would need ReflectionTestUtils.setField or @InjectMocks magic.
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `FieldInjectedOrderService` prints `null` in its constructor because field injection happens after construction. The field cannot be `final`, so any code could reassign it.
- `SetterInjectedOrderService` declares the SMS notifier optional with `required = false`. No `SmsNotifier` bean exists, so Spring simply never calls the setter.
- `ConstructorInjectedOrderService` gets everything at construction time. Required dependencies are `final`. Optional ones are explicit: `Optional<SmsNotifier>` is empty when no bean exists, and `ObjectProvider` defers the lookup until `ifAvailable` is called.
- The test builds the constructor-injected service with `new` and mocks. No Spring context, no reflection tricks.

### Output
```text
field style: gateway in constructor = null
constructor style: gateway in constructor = StripePaymentGateway
setter style: no SMS notifier, skipping
AUDIT order for alice
no SMS (Optional)
```
(Bean creation order between the two services can differ, so the first two lines may swap.)

### Compare
| Aspect | Constructor | Setter | Field |
|---|---|---|---|
| Immutability (`final`) | Yes | No | No |
| Object complete after `new` | Yes | No | No |
| Dependencies visible in API | Yes | Partly | No |
| Unit test without Spring | Easy | Easy | Needs reflection |
| Optional dependencies | `Optional` or `ObjectProvider` | Natural fit | `required = false` |
| Circular dependency | Fails fast | Possible only if allowed | Possible only if allowed |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What are the three types of dependency injection in Spring?**
   Constructor injection (dependencies as constructor parameters), setter injection (`@Autowired` setter methods called after construction) and field injection (`@Autowired` directly on fields, set by reflection).
2. **Which one do you prefer and why?**
   Constructor injection for required dependencies: fields can be final, the object is always valid, dependencies are visible, and tests can use plain `new`. The Spring team recommends it.
3. **Do I need `@Autowired` on a constructor?**
   Not if the class has a single constructor, since Spring 4.3. With multiple constructors, mark the one Spring should use.

**Follow-up**
4. **When does setter injection make sense?**
   For optional dependencies that have a sensible default, or values that may be reconfigured after construction (for example via JMX). It is also how some framework base classes accept collaborators.
5. **Why is field injection discouraged?**
   It hides dependencies (you must read the fields), prevents `final`, makes the object usable in an invalid half-built state, ties the class to the container (you cannot construct it properly without reflection) and makes it easy to keep adding dependencies unnoticed.
6. **How do you inject an optional dependency with constructor injection?**
   Use `Optional<T>`, `ObjectProvider<T>` (lazy and optional, also handles multiple candidates) or a `@Nullable` parameter. `ObjectProvider` is the most flexible.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **What does this print, and why?**
   ```java
   @Service
   class Report {
       @Autowired private Clock clock;
       Report() { System.out.println(clock.instant()); }
   }
   ```
   Startup fails with a `BeanCreationException` caused by a `NullPointerException`: the constructor runs before field injection, so `clock` is null. Move the logic to `@PostConstruct` or, better, inject the `Clock` through the constructor.
8. **Two services need each other through constructor injection and startup fails. What do you do?**
   First, fix the design: extract the shared logic into a third service, or use events so one side does not call the other directly. As a stopgap you can put `@Lazy` on one constructor parameter, which injects a proxy resolved on first use. Re-enabling `allow-circular-references` is a last resort.
9. **Lombok `@RequiredArgsConstructor` on a service: what kind of injection is that?**
   Constructor injection. Lombok generates a constructor for all `final` fields, and with a single constructor Spring uses it automatically.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you know the trade-offs and the timing (fields are set after construction), not just "constructor is best".

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Using an injected field in the constructor | `NullPointerException` at startup | Constructor injection or `@PostConstruct` |
| Field injection plus `new` in tests | Dependencies stay null | Constructor injection with mocks |
| Fixing a circular dependency with field injection | Fails anyway since Boot 2.6; hides a design problem | Refactor, events, or `@Lazy` parameter |
| `@Autowired(required = false)` on a required dependency | NPE later instead of startup failure | Keep required dependencies required |

### Best practices
- Constructor injection with `private final` fields; one constructor; no `@Autowired`.
- Lombok `@RequiredArgsConstructor` is fine if your team uses Lombok.
- Optional dependencies: `ObjectProvider<T>` or `Optional<T>` in the constructor.
- Field injection is acceptable in test classes (`@Autowired` in a `@SpringBootTest`) where the test framework manages the lifecycle.

### Compare with
| Style | Spring team stance | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| Constructor | Recommended for mandatory dependencies | Services, controllers, repositories |
| Setter | Recommended for optional dependencies | Optional collaborators, framework base classes |
| Field | Discouraged in production code | Test classes, quick demos |
| Method injection (`@Lookup`) | Niche | Getting a fresh prototype from a singleton |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Audit and refactor OrderHub's injection style:
1. Convert every `@Autowired` field in `ProductController`, `ProductService` and any helpers to constructor injection with `final` fields.
2. Add an optional `ProductEventsPublisher` dependency to `ProductService`. No implementation exists yet, so the service must work without it and must call it if it exists.
3. Set `spring.main.allow-circular-references=false` explicitly in `application.yml` to document the rule.

Acceptance criteria: `grep -r "@Autowired" src/main` returns nothing; the app starts with no publisher bean; a unit test verifies that the publisher is called when present.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductEventsPublisher.java
package com.orderhub.product;

public interface ProductEventsPublisher {
    void productCreated(Product product);
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductService.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository repository;
    private final ObjectProvider<ProductEventsPublisher> publisher;

    public ProductService(ProductRepository repository,
                          ObjectProvider<ProductEventsPublisher> publisher) {
        this.repository = repository;
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }

    public List<Product> findAll() { return repository.findAll(); }

    public Product create(Product product) {
        Product saved = repository.save(product);
        publisher.ifAvailable(p -> p.productCreated(saved));   // no-op when no bean exists
        return saved;
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductController.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {
    private final ProductService service;

    public ProductController(ProductService service) { this.service = service; }

    @GetMapping
    public List<Product> all() { return service.findAll(); }

    @PostMapping
    public Product create(@RequestBody Product product) { return service.create(product); }
}

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductServicePublisherTest.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import static org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.any;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.Consumer;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;

class ProductServicePublisherTest {
    @Test
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    void publishesWhenPublisherExists() {
        ProductRepository repo = mock(ProductRepository.class);
        ProductEventsPublisher events = mock(ProductEventsPublisher.class);
        ObjectProvider<ProductEventsPublisher> provider = mock(ObjectProvider.class);
        doAnswer(inv -> { ((Consumer<ProductEventsPublisher>) inv.getArgument(0)).accept(events); return null; })
                .when(provider).ifAvailable(any());
        Product p = new Product(1L, "Mouse", new BigDecimal("19.90"), 5);
        when(repo.save(any())).thenReturn(p);

        new ProductService(repo, provider).create(p);

        verify(events).productCreated(p);
    }
}
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml
spring:
  main:
    allow-circular-references: false   # already the default since Boot 2.6, stated for clarity
```
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What are the three injection styles in Spring?
A: Constructor injection, setter injection and field injection.

Q: Why is constructor injection preferred in Spring?
A: Dependencies can be final, the object is complete after construction, dependencies are visible, and it is testable with plain `new`.

Q: When are `@Autowired` fields populated relative to the constructor?
A: After the constructor runs, by `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor`, so they are null inside the constructor.

Q: How do you declare an optional dependency with constructor injection in Spring?
A: Use `Optional<T>`, `ObjectProvider<T>` or a `@Nullable` parameter.

Q: Are circular dependencies allowed by default in Spring Boot 3?
A: No; since Boot 2.6 `spring.main.allow-circular-references` defaults to false, so cycles fail at startup.

**Related:** [[IoC and Dependency Injection]] · [[@Qualifier, @Primary and Multiple Beans]] · [[Bean Lifecycle Callbacks]]

# @Qualifier, @Primary and Multiple Beans

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: @Qualifier Spring 2.5; @Primary Spring 3.0; ordered List injection Spring 4.0**

> **30-second interview answer:** When two beans implement the same interface, Spring cannot choose and fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`. `@Primary` marks the default candidate, and `@Qualifier("name")` at the injection point picks a specific one; a qualifier wins over primary. If I need all implementations, I inject a `List<PaymentGateway>` (ordered by `@Order`) or a `Map<String, PaymentGateway>` keyed by bean name, which is how I build strategy patterns in Spring.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Calling a taxi company that has several drivers.

| Taxi company | Spring |
|---|---|
| "Send me a driver" with three on shift: dispatcher cannot choose | `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` |
| The default driver for normal bookings | `@Primary` |
| "I specifically want Maria" | `@Qualifier("maria")` |
| The VIP booking label that any VIP driver matches | Custom qualifier annotation (`@Express`) |
| The dispatcher's full roster, in shift order | `List<Driver>` ordered by `@Order` |
| Roster as a name-to-driver lookup | `Map<String, Driver>` |

### What it is
- **Candidate beans:** all beans assignable to the injection point type.
- **`@Primary`:** on a bean (class or `@Bean` method); makes it the preferred candidate when several match.
- **`@Qualifier`:** on the bean to give it a qualifier value, and on the injection point to require that value. Without an explicit qualifier on the bean, the bean name acts as a fallback qualifier.
- **Custom qualifier annotation:** your own annotation meta-annotated with `@Qualifier`, giving type-safe selection without string names.
- **Collection injection:** `List<T>`, `Set<T>`, `T[]` or `Map<String, T>` receive all candidates.
- **`@Order` or `Ordered`:** controls ordering in injected lists (lower value first). It does not affect single-bean selection.
- **`@Fallback`** (Spring 6.2): the opposite of `@Primary`; the bean is used only if no other candidate exists.

### Why it exists
Real applications have several implementations of one abstraction: multiple payment providers, several notification channels, a read and a write `DataSource`. Spring needs explicit rules to choose, and you need ways to inject either one specific implementation or all of them.

## 2. How It Works
Resolution for a single-valued injection point (inside `DefaultListableBeanFactory.doResolveDependency`):

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Find all beans assignable to the type"] --> B{"How many?"}
    B -- "0" --> X["Required: NoSuchBeanDefinitionException; optional: null or empty"]
    B -- "1" --> OK["Inject it"]
    B -- "more than 1" --> Q{"@Qualifier at injection point?"}
    Q -- "yes" --> Q2["Keep only matching qualifier or bean name"]
    Q -- "no" --> P{"Exactly one @Primary?"}
    Q2 --> P
    P -- "yes" --> OK
    P -- "no" --> F{"Non-@Fallback candidates = 1?"}
    F -- "yes" --> OK
    F -- "no" --> N{"Parameter name equals a bean name?"}
    N -- "yes" --> OK
    N -- "no" --> E["NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException"]
```

Notes:
- The qualifier filter is applied first; `@Primary` then applies among the remaining candidates.
- Parameter-name matching needs the `-parameters` compiler flag, which Spring Boot's Maven and Gradle plugins enable. Spring 6.1 removed the fallback that read names from debug info, so without `-parameters` this step silently does nothing.
- Two `@Primary` beans of the same type fail with "more than one primary bean found".
- For `List<T>` injection, Spring collects every candidate and sorts by `@Order`, `Ordered` or `@Priority`; unordered beans go last.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/payment/PaymentConfig.java
package com.orderhub.payment;

import java.lang.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

interface PaymentGateway {
    String code();
    String charge(BigDecimal amount);
}

// Custom, type-safe qualifier
@Target({ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Qualifier
@interface Sandbox { }

@Component("stripe")
@Primary                    // default when nothing else is specified
@Order(1)
class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public String code() { return "STRIPE"; }
    public String charge(BigDecimal a) { return "stripe charged " + a; }
}

@Component("paypal")
@Order(2)
class PaypalGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public String code() { return "PAYPAL"; }
    public String charge(BigDecimal a) { return "paypal charged " + a; }
}

@Component
@Sandbox
@Order(3)
class SandboxGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public String code() { return "SANDBOX"; }
    public String charge(BigDecimal a) { return "sandbox pretended to charge " + a; }
}

@Service
class CheckoutService {
    private final PaymentGateway defaultGateway;
    private final PaymentGateway paypal;
    private final PaymentGateway sandbox;

    CheckoutService(PaymentGateway defaultGateway,                  // @Primary wins
                    @Qualifier("paypal") PaymentGateway paypal,     // by qualifier or bean name
                    @Sandbox PaymentGateway sandbox) {              // by custom qualifier
        this.defaultGateway = defaultGateway;
        this.paypal = paypal;
        this.sandbox = sandbox;
    }

    void demo() {
        System.out.println(defaultGateway.charge(new BigDecimal("10")));
        System.out.println(paypal.charge(new BigDecimal("20")));
        System.out.println(sandbox.charge(new BigDecimal("30")));
    }
}

// Strategy pattern: pick the gateway the customer chose at runtime
@Service
class PaymentRouter {
    private final Map<String, PaymentGateway> byCode;

    PaymentRouter(List<PaymentGateway> gateways) {   // all three, ordered by @Order
        System.out.println("order of list: " + gateways.stream().map(PaymentGateway::code).toList());
        this.byCode = gateways.stream()
                .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toMap(PaymentGateway::code, g -> g));
    }

    String pay(String code, BigDecimal amount) {
        PaymentGateway g = byCode.get(code);
        if (g == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown payment method " + code);
        return g.charge(amount);
    }
}

@Configuration
class PaymentDemoConfig {
    @Bean
    CommandLineRunner paymentDemo(CheckoutService checkout, PaymentRouter router,
                                  Map<String, PaymentGateway> byBeanName) {
        return args -> {
            checkout.demo();
            System.out.println(router.pay("PAYPAL", new BigDecimal("55")));
            System.out.println("map keys: " + byBeanName.keySet());
        };
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- Three beans implement `PaymentGateway`. Without any hints, an unqualified injection would fail with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: expected single matching bean but found 3: stripe,paypal,sandboxGateway`.
- `StripeGateway` is `@Primary`, so the unqualified `defaultGateway` parameter gets Stripe.
- `@Qualifier("paypal")` matches the bean named `paypal` (the name is the fallback qualifier).
- `@Sandbox` is a custom qualifier: compile-time checked, refactor-friendly, no strings.
- `PaymentRouter` takes a `List<PaymentGateway>`, which contains all three in `@Order` order, then builds its own map keyed by a business code. This is the standard Spring strategy pattern.
- Injecting `Map<String, PaymentGateway>` gives bean name to bean, in registration order.

### Output
```text
order of list: [STRIPE, PAYPAL, SANDBOX]
stripe charged 10
paypal charged 20
sandbox pretended to charge 30
paypal charged 55
map keys: [stripe, paypal, sandboxGateway]
```

### Compare
| Need | Tool |
|---|---|
| One default implementation | `@Primary` on the bean |
| A specific implementation at one injection point | `@Qualifier` at the injection point |
| Type-safe, reusable selection | Custom annotation meta-annotated with `@Qualifier` |
| Pick at runtime by user input | Inject `List<T>` or `Map<String, T>` and route |
| A default that steps aside when anyone defines another | `@Fallback` (Spring 6.2) or `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What happens if two beans implement the same interface and you inject the interface?**
   Startup fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`, unless Spring can narrow it down with `@Qualifier`, `@Primary`, `@Fallback` or by matching the parameter name to a bean name.
2. **Difference between `@Primary` and `@Qualifier`?**
   `@Primary` is declared on the bean and sets a global default. `@Qualifier` is declared at the injection point and picks a specific bean for that one place. When both apply, the qualifier wins because it filters candidates before primary is considered.
3. **How do you inject all implementations of an interface?**
   Inject `List<T>`, `Set<T>`, an array or `Map<String, T>`. The list is sorted by `@Order` or `Ordered`; the map key is the bean name.

**Follow-up**
4. **Does `@Order` decide which single bean gets injected?**
   No. `@Order` only sorts collection injection and some ordered callbacks (filters, advice). For single-bean selection use `@Primary` or `@Qualifier`.
5. **Why use a custom qualifier annotation instead of `@Qualifier("sandbox")`?**
   Strings are not checked by the compiler and break silently on renames. A custom annotation is type-safe, self-documenting and can carry attributes.
6. **Can a parameter name select a bean?**
   Yes, as the last fallback: if the parameter is named `paypal` and a bean named `paypal` exists, it is chosen. It requires compiling with `-parameters` (Boot's build plugins do this). It is fragile, so prefer explicit qualifiers.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **A library auto-configures an `ObjectMapper`. You define your own, and now some code gets yours and some fails. Why?**
   Most Boot auto-configurations use `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, so defining your own replaces theirs entirely. Failures come when something else defines a second one, or when you meant to customise rather than replace. Use a `Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer` to customise, or mark yours `@Primary` if two must coexist.
8. **You have two `DataSource` beans (primary and reporting). How do you wire JPA to the right one?**
   Mark the main one `@Primary` so auto-configured JPA and `JdbcTemplate` use it, and inject the reporting one with `@Qualifier("reportingDataSource")` into a dedicated `JdbcTemplate` or `JdbcClient`.
9. **What is the output when two beans are both marked `@Primary`?**
   Startup fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` saying more than one primary bean was found among the candidates.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you know the resolution order and can design a strategy pattern with Spring instead of an if-else chain.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `@Qualifier("stripeGateway")` after renaming the class | Startup failure or wrong bean | Give beans explicit names or use custom qualifiers |
| Expecting `@Order` to choose a single bean | Still `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` | Use `@Primary` or `@Qualifier` |
| Two `@Primary` beans of the same type | Startup failure | Exactly one primary per type |
| if-else on payment type inside a service | Every new provider edits the service | Inject `List<T>` and route by key |

### Best practices
- Put `@Primary` on the implementation most code should use; use qualifiers for exceptions.
- Prefer custom qualifier annotations over string qualifiers in larger codebases.
- Use `List<T>` or `Map<String, T>` injection for strategy, plugin and chain-of-responsibility patterns.
- Fail clearly when a runtime key has no implementation (throw with a helpful message).

### Compare with
| Mechanism | Declared on | Scope of effect | Wins over |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@Qualifier` | Bean and injection point | One injection point | `@Primary` |
| `@Primary` | Bean | All unqualified injection points | `@Fallback`, name matching |
| `@Fallback` | Bean | Used only when no other candidate | Nothing |
| Parameter name | Injection point | One injection point | Last resort |
| `@Order` | Bean | Ordering of collections only | Not a selection tool |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** OrderHub must support two payment providers.
1. Create `PaymentGateway` with `PaymentMethod method()` and `PaymentResult charge(long orderId, BigDecimal amount)`; `PaymentMethod` is an enum `CARD, PAYPAL`.
2. Implement `CardPaymentGateway` (mark it `@Primary`) and `PaypalPaymentGateway`.
3. Create `PaymentService` that receives `List<PaymentGateway>`, builds an `EnumMap<PaymentMethod, PaymentGateway>` and exposes `pay(orderId, method, amount)`.
4. Startup must fail with a clear message if two gateways claim the same method.

Acceptance criteria: `pay(1, PAYPAL, 10)` uses PayPal; any class injecting a plain `PaymentGateway` gets the card gateway; a duplicate method breaks startup with `IllegalStateException`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/payment/PaymentService.java
package com.orderhub.payment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

enum PaymentMethod { CARD, PAYPAL }

record PaymentResult(long orderId, PaymentMethod method, String reference) { }

interface PaymentGateway {
    PaymentMethod method();
    PaymentResult charge(long orderId, BigDecimal amount);
}

@Component
@Primary
class CardPaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public PaymentMethod method() { return PaymentMethod.CARD; }
    public PaymentResult charge(long orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return new PaymentResult(orderId, method(), "card-" + orderId);
    }
}

@Component
class PaypalPaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public PaymentMethod method() { return PaymentMethod.PAYPAL; }
    public PaymentResult charge(long orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return new PaymentResult(orderId, method(), "pp-" + orderId);
    }
}

@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final Map<PaymentMethod, PaymentGateway> gateways = new EnumMap<>(PaymentMethod.class);

    public PaymentService(List<PaymentGateway> all) {
        for (PaymentGateway g : all) {
            PaymentGateway previous = gateways.putIfAbsent(g.method(), g);
            if (previous != null) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Two gateways for " + g.method() + ": "
                        + previous.getClass().getSimpleName() + " and " + g.getClass().getSimpleName());
            }
        }
    }

    public PaymentResult pay(long orderId, PaymentMethod method, BigDecimal amount) {
        PaymentGateway g = gateways.get(method);
        if (g == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("No gateway for " + method);
        return g.charge(orderId, amount);
    }
}
```
`pay(1, PAYPAL, BigDecimal.TEN)` returns `PaymentResult[orderId=1, method=PAYPAL, reference=pp-1]`. Adding a third provider means adding one class; `PaymentService` never changes (open/closed principle).
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: Which exception does Spring throw when several beans match one injection point?
A: `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`.

Q: When both `@Primary` and `@Qualifier` apply in Spring, which wins?
A: `@Qualifier`, because it filters candidates before `@Primary` is considered.

Q: How do you inject every implementation of an interface in Spring?
A: Inject `List<T>` (sorted by `@Order`), `Set<T>`, an array, or `Map<String, T>` keyed by bean name.

Q: Does `@Order` choose which single bean Spring injects?
A: No; it only orders collection injection and ordered callbacks.

Q: What does `@Fallback` do in Spring 6.2?
A: Marks a bean to be used only when no other candidate of that type exists, the opposite of `@Primary`.

**Related:** [[Constructor vs Setter vs Field Injection]] · [[Stereotype Annotations]] · [[Conditional Beans]] · [[IoC and Dependency Injection]]

# Stereotype Annotations

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: @Repository Spring 2.0; @Component, @Service, @Controller Spring 2.5; @RestController Spring 4.0**

> **30-second interview answer:** `@Component` marks a class as a Spring-managed bean found by component scanning. `@Service`, `@Repository` and `@Controller` are specialisations of `@Component` that document the layer. Functionally, `@Service` adds nothing extra, `@Repository` enables translation of persistence exceptions into Spring's `DataAccessException` hierarchy, and `@Controller` makes the class a Spring MVC handler; `@RestController` is `@Controller` plus `@ResponseBody`. Scanning starts at the package of the `@SpringBootApplication` class.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Name badges in a company.

| Company badge | Spring |
|---|---|
| "Employee" badge: you work here | `@Component` |
| "Front desk" badge: talks to visitors | `@Controller` and `@RestController` |
| "Operations" badge: does the core work | `@Service` |
| "Archive" badge: handles the records, and translates archive jargon into company language | `@Repository` with exception translation |
| HR walks the building floor by floor collecting badged people | Component scanning from a base package |
| People in another building are never found | Classes outside the scanned packages |

### What it is
- **Component scanning:** at startup Spring walks the classpath under base packages, reads class metadata with ASM (without loading the classes) and registers a bean definition for each class annotated (directly or via meta-annotation) with `@Component`.
- **`@Component`:** generic stereotype.
- **`@Service`:** business logic layer; semantically meaningful, no extra behaviour.
- **`@Repository`:** data access layer; `PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor` wraps it in a proxy that converts exceptions like JPA's `PersistenceException` or JDBC `SQLException` into `DataAccessException` subclasses.
- **`@Controller`:** web layer; detected by `RequestMappingHandlerMapping` as a handler with `@RequestMapping` methods returning views.
- **`@RestController`:** `@Controller` plus `@ResponseBody`: return values are written to the HTTP body (JSON) instead of resolved as view names.
- **Meta-annotation:** an annotation placed on another annotation. `@Service` is itself annotated with `@Component`, which is why scanning finds it.

### Why it exists
Before annotations, every bean was declared in XML. Stereotypes let the class itself say "I am a bean, and I belong to this layer". The specialised stereotypes also give tools and aspects a hook: you can target "all repositories" with exception translation or "all controllers" with `@ControllerAdvice`.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["@SpringBootApplication in com.orderhub"] --> B["@ComponentScan(basePackages = com.orderhub)"]
    B --> C["ClassPathBeanDefinitionScanner reads .class files with ASM"]
    C --> D{"Annotated with @Component or a meta-annotated stereotype?"}
    D -- "yes" --> E["Register ScannedGenericBeanDefinition, name = decapitalised class name"]
    D -- "no" --> F["Ignored"]
    E --> G["Later: post-processors add stereotype behaviour"]
    G --> H["@Repository: exception translation proxy"]
    G --> I["@Controller: handler methods registered by RequestMappingHandlerMapping"]
```

What `@Service` looks like inside Spring:

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Documented
@Component                    // this meta-annotation is what makes scanning find it
public @interface Service {
    @AliasFor(annotation = Component.class)
    String value() default "";
}
```

Scanning rules worth knowing:
- `@SpringBootApplication` includes `@ComponentScan` with no base package, meaning "the package of this class and everything below it".
- A class in `com.other` is not found if the main class is in `com.orderhub`. Fix with `scanBasePackages` or, better, move the main class to the root package.
- Filters: `includeFilters` and `excludeFilters` on `@ComponentScan` (Boot already excludes auto-configuration classes from scanning).
- For faster startup with huge classpaths, Spring used to offer a compile-time index (`spring-context-indexer`); it is deprecated since 6.1 in favour of AOT.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/customer/Customer.java
package com.orderhub.customer;

public record Customer(Long id, String email, String name) { }

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/customer/CustomerRepository.java
package com.orderhub.customer;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import org.springframework.dao.DuplicateKeyException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

@Repository                                   // data layer + exception translation
public class CustomerRepository {
    private final Map<String, Customer> byEmail = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public Customer save(Customer c) {
        if (byEmail.putIfAbsent(c.email(), c) != null) {
            // Throw a Spring DataAccessException subtype, like a real DB unique violation would become
            throw new DuplicateKeyException("email already used: " + c.email());
        }
        return c;
    }

    public Optional<Customer> findByEmail(String email) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(byEmail.get(email));
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/customer/CustomerService.java
package com.orderhub.customer;

import org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service                                      // business layer
public class CustomerService {
    private final CustomerRepository repository;

    public CustomerService(CustomerRepository repository) { this.repository = repository; }

    public Customer register(String email, String name) {
        try {
            return repository.save(new Customer(null, email, name));
        } catch (DataAccessException e) {     // technology-neutral exception type
            throw new EmailTakenException(email);
        }
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/customer/EmailTakenException.java
package com.orderhub.customer;

public class EmailTakenException extends RuntimeException {
    public EmailTakenException(String email) { super("Email already registered: " + email); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/customer/CustomerController.java
package com.orderhub.customer;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController                               // @Controller + @ResponseBody
@RequestMapping("/api/customers")
public class CustomerController {
    private final CustomerService service;

    public CustomerController(CustomerService service) { this.service = service; }

    record RegisterRequest(String email, String name) { }

    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public Customer register(@RequestBody RegisterRequest req) {
        return service.register(req.email(), req.name());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(EmailTakenException.class)
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
    public String taken(EmailTakenException e) { return e.getMessage(); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/common/UseCase.java
package com.orderhub.common;

import java.lang.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.AliasFor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

// Your own stereotype: meta-annotated with @Service (and therefore @Component)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Documented
@Service
public @interface UseCase {
    @AliasFor(annotation = Service.class)
    String value() default "";
}
```

### Walkthrough
- All four classes live under `com.orderhub`, the package of `OrderHubApplication`, so scanning finds them without configuration.
- `CustomerRepository` throws `DuplicateKeyException`, a `DataAccessException`. With a real JPA repository the `@Repository` proxy would translate a `ConstraintViolationException` or `PersistenceException` into `DataIntegrityViolationException` for you, so the service only ever catches Spring's neutral hierarchy.
- `CustomerService` is a `@Service`: no extra behaviour, but it tells readers and tools "business logic lives here".
- `CustomerController` is a `@RestController`: the returned `Customer` record is serialised to JSON by Jackson.
- `@UseCase` shows you can build your own stereotype; classes annotated with it are discovered because of the meta-annotation chain `@UseCase` to `@Service` to `@Component`.

### Output
```http
POST /api/customers
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"ana@example.com","name":"Ana"}
```
```http
HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Content-Type: application/json

{"id":null,"email":"ana@example.com","name":"Ana"}
```
Second identical request:
```http
HTTP/1.1 409 Conflict
Content-Type: text/plain;charset=UTF-8

Email already registered: ana@example.com
```

### Compare
| Annotation | Layer | Extra behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| `@Component` | Any | None |
| `@Service` | Business | None (semantic only) |
| `@Repository` | Data access | Persistence exception translation |
| `@Controller` | Web (views) | Handler detection; return value is a view name |
| `@RestController` | Web (APIs) | `@Controller` plus `@ResponseBody` on every method |
| `@Configuration` | Configuration | CGLIB proxy so `@Bean` methods return singletons |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is the difference between `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository` and `@Controller`?**
   All are detected by component scanning because they are meta-annotated with `@Component`. `@Service` is purely semantic. `@Repository` adds exception translation into `DataAccessException`. `@Controller` registers the class as a Spring MVC handler.
2. **What is the difference between `@Controller` and `@RestController`?**
   `@RestController` is `@Controller` plus `@ResponseBody`. Return values are written to the response body through message converters (JSON with Jackson) instead of being treated as view names.
3. **Where does Spring Boot start component scanning?**
   From the package of the class annotated with `@SpringBootApplication`, including all sub-packages.

**Follow-up**
4. **Can I use `@Component` instead of `@Service` on a service class?**
   Yes, it works identically today. Use `@Service` for clarity and so aspects or architecture tests (ArchUnit) can target the layer.
5. **What exactly does exception translation do?**
   `PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor` wraps `@Repository` beans in a proxy. When a vendor-specific exception escapes (JPA, Hibernate, JDBC), the proxy asks registered `PersistenceExceptionTranslator`s to convert it into Spring's unchecked `DataAccessException` hierarchy, for example `DataIntegrityViolationException`. Services stay independent of the persistence technology.
6. **Do Spring Data JPA repository interfaces need `@Repository`?**
   No. Spring Data creates the implementation and applies exception translation itself. Adding `@Repository` to the interface is harmless but redundant.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **You added a `@Service` in package `com.orderhub2.billing` and get `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`. Why?**
   It is outside the scanned base package `com.orderhub` (`com.orderhub2` is a different package, not a sub-package). Move it under `com.orderhub` or add `scanBasePackages`.
8. **Is a class annotated with `@Component` but with two constructors and no `@Autowired` valid?**
   Spring uses the no-arg constructor if one exists; otherwise startup fails with "No default constructor found". Mark the intended constructor with `@Autowired` or keep a single constructor.
9. **Can you put stereotype annotations on interfaces?**
   Scanning only registers concrete classes; an annotated interface is skipped. Spring Data repositories are an exception because Spring Data registers them through its own scanning.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** that you know stereotypes are mostly semantic, which one has real extra behaviour, and how scanning finds classes.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Main class in a sub-package (`com.orderhub.app`) | Beans in `com.orderhub.product` are not found | Main class in the root package |
| `@Controller` on a JSON API without `@ResponseBody` | Spring looks for a view named after the return value, 404 or 500 | Use `@RestController` |
| Stereotype on a class that also has a `@Bean` method creating it | Two beans of the same type, `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` | Declare it once |
| Catching `SQLException` in services | Persistence details leak upward | Catch `DataAccessException` or translate at the repository |

### Best practices
- Package by feature (`product`, `order`, `customer`) with the main class at the root.
- Use the most specific stereotype; it documents intent and enables layer-targeted rules.
- Use `@Bean` instead of stereotypes for third-party classes you cannot annotate.
- Enforce layering with ArchUnit tests (controllers do not call repositories directly).

### Compare with
| Way to register a bean | When |
|---|---|
| Stereotype + scanning | Your own classes |
| `@Bean` method | Third-party classes or conditional, customised creation |
| `@Import` | Pulling in specific configuration classes explicitly |
| Auto-configuration | Library defaults that back off when you define your own |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Make the layering explicit:
1. Annotate OrderHub classes with the correct stereotype (`ProductController` `@RestController`, `ProductService` `@Service`, `InMemoryProductRepository` `@Repository`).
2. Make the repository throw `DuplicateKeyException` when a product with the same name (case-insensitive) already exists.
3. In `ProductService`, catch `DataAccessException` and rethrow a domain `DuplicateProductException`.
4. Map it to HTTP 409 in the controller.

Acceptance criteria: creating two products named "Keyboard" and "keyboard" returns 201 then 409; `ProductService` imports nothing from the repository implementation.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/InMemoryProductRepository.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.springframework.dao.DuplicateKeyException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

@Repository
public class InMemoryProductRepository implements ProductRepository {
    private final Map<Long, Product> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final AtomicLong ids = new AtomicLong();

    public synchronized Product save(Product p) {
        boolean duplicate = store.values().stream()
                .anyMatch(e -> e.name().equalsIgnoreCase(p.name()) && !Objects.equals(e.id(), p.id()));
        if (duplicate) throw new DuplicateKeyException("product name exists: " + p.name());
        Product saved = p.id() == null ? p.withId(ids.incrementAndGet()) : p;
        store.put(saved.id(), saved);
        return saved;
    }
    public List<Product> findAll() { return new ArrayList<>(store.values()); }
    public Optional<Product> findById(long id) { return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id)); }
    public void deleteById(long id) { store.remove(id); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/DuplicateProductException.java
package com.orderhub.product;

public class DuplicateProductException extends RuntimeException {
    public DuplicateProductException(String name) { super("Product already exists: " + name); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductService.java (create method)
package com.orderhub.product;

import org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository repository;
    public ProductService(ProductRepository repository) { this.repository = repository; }

    public Product create(Product product) {
        try {
            return repository.save(product);
        } catch (DataAccessException e) {
            throw new DuplicateProductException(product.name());
        }
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductController.java (handler)
package com.orderhub.product;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {
    private final ProductService service;
    public ProductController(ProductService service) { this.service = service; }

    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public Product create(@RequestBody Product p) { return service.create(p); }

    @ExceptionHandler(DuplicateProductException.class)
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
    public String duplicate(DuplicateProductException e) { return e.getMessage(); }
}
```
In Phase 11 the local `@ExceptionHandler` moves into a global `@RestControllerAdvice` returning `ProblemDetail`.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: Which Spring stereotype annotation adds real behaviour beyond marking a bean, and what is it?
A: `@Repository`, which enables translation of persistence exceptions into Spring's `DataAccessException` hierarchy.

Q: What is `@RestController` made of?
A: `@Controller` plus `@ResponseBody`.

Q: Why does component scanning find classes annotated with `@Service`?
A: Because `@Service` is meta-annotated with `@Component`.

Q: Where does Spring Boot's component scan start?
A: At the package of the `@SpringBootApplication` class, including sub-packages.

Q: Does `@Service` add any functionality over `@Component`?
A: No; it is semantic, documenting the business layer for readers and tools.

**Related:** [[@Configuration and @Bean]] · [[ApplicationContext and Bean Definitions]] · [[@RestController and Request Mapping Deep Dive]]

# @Configuration and @Bean

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: Spring 3.0 (JavaConfig); proxyBeanMethods Spring 5.2**

> **30-second interview answer:** A `@Configuration` class holds `@Bean` methods; each method's return value becomes a bean named after the method. I use `@Bean` for third-party classes I cannot annotate, or when creation needs logic. Spring subclasses full `@Configuration` classes with CGLIB, so when one `@Bean` method calls another, the call is intercepted and returns the existing singleton instead of creating a new object. With `proxyBeanMethods = false` (lite mode) there is no proxy, startup is faster, and inter-bean calls are plain Java calls, so you should pass dependencies as method parameters instead.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A restaurant's prep list versus staff who show up on their own.

| Restaurant | Spring |
|---|---|
| Staff who walk in wearing a badge | Stereotype-annotated classes found by scanning |
| The head chef's prep list: "make one big pot of stock, then use it for soup and sauce" | `@Configuration` class with `@Bean` methods |
| A sous-chef who checks "stock already made? reuse it" | CGLIB proxy intercepting `@Bean` method calls |
| A prep list without the sous-chef: every recipe makes fresh stock | Lite mode (`proxyBeanMethods = false`) with method calls |
| Ingredients bought from a supplier, not made in-house | Third-party classes (`Clock`, `RestClient`, `ObjectMapper`) |

### What it is
- **`@Configuration`:** marks a class as a source of bean definitions. It is itself a `@Component`, so scanning finds it.
- **`@Bean`:** on a method; the return value is registered as a bean. Bean name = method name (override with `@Bean("name")`). Supports `initMethod`, `destroyMethod`, and works with `@Scope`, `@Primary`, `@Lazy`, `@Profile`, `@Conditional...`.
- **Full mode:** `@Configuration` (default `proxyBeanMethods = true`); the class is subclassed with CGLIB.
- **Lite mode:** `@Bean` methods inside a `@Component` class, or in `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)`; no proxy.
- **Method parameter injection:** `@Bean` method parameters are resolved from the container like constructor parameters.
- **Inferred destroy method:** for `@Bean`, Spring automatically calls a public `close()` or `shutdown()` method on destruction unless you set `destroyMethod = ""`.

### Why it exists
Stereotypes only work on classes you own. For a `Clock`, a configured `RestClient`, a `DataSource` or a `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` you need to call constructors and builders yourself. Java config gives you type-safe, refactor-friendly, debuggable bean creation that replaced XML.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Ctx as "Container"
    participant Proxy as "AppConfig$$SpringCGLIB"
    participant Real as "AppConfig method body"
    Ctx->>Proxy: call executor() to create bean "executor"
    Proxy->>Real: not in container yet, invoke super.executor()
    Real-->>Proxy: new ThreadPool
    Proxy-->>Ctx: register singleton "executor"
    Ctx->>Proxy: call reportService() to create bean "reportService"
    Real->>Proxy: body calls executor()
    Proxy-->>Real: intercepted, return EXISTING "executor" from container
```

- `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor` parses `@Configuration` classes and creates a bean definition per `@Bean` method (factory bean = the config class, factory method = the method).
- `ConfigurationClassEnhancer` creates a CGLIB subclass for full-mode classes. Its interceptor checks the container before running the method body. That is why config classes and `@Bean` methods must not be `final` or `private` in full mode.
- Static `@Bean` methods are never intercepted; use them for `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` and `BeanPostProcessor` beans.
- Spring Boot's own auto-configurations use `proxyBeanMethods = false` everywhere for faster startup and GraalVM native compatibility.

```text
Full mode:  reportService() calls executor()  -> proxy -> same singleton executor
Lite mode:  reportService() calls executor()  -> plain call -> NEW executor (bug!)
Lite mode fix: reportService(ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor)  -> container injects the singleton
```

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/config/AppConfig.java
package com.orderhub.config;

import java.time.Clock;
import java.time.ZoneOffset;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationRunner;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClient;

@Configuration                       // full mode: CGLIB proxy
public class AppConfig {

    static final AtomicInteger CREATED = new AtomicInteger();

    public static class AuditSink {
        private final int id = CREATED.incrementAndGet();
        public int id() { return id; }
        public void close() { System.out.println("AuditSink " + id + " closed"); }  // inferred destroy method
    }

    public record OrderAuditor(AuditSink sink) { }
    public record PaymentAuditor(AuditSink sink) { }

    @Bean
    public Clock clock() {                       // third-party class: no stereotype possible
        return Clock.system(ZoneOffset.UTC);
    }

    @Bean
    public AuditSink auditSink() {
        return new AuditSink();
    }

    @Bean
    public OrderAuditor orderAuditor() {
        return new OrderAuditor(auditSink());    // intercepted: returns the singleton
    }

    @Bean
    public PaymentAuditor paymentAuditor() {
        return new PaymentAuditor(auditSink());  // intercepted again: same singleton
    }

    @Bean
    public RestClient inventoryClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {  // parameter injection
        return builder.baseUrl("http://localhost:8081/api/inventory").build();
    }

    @Bean
    ApplicationRunner configDemo(OrderAuditor o, PaymentAuditor p, AppConfig self) {
        return args -> {
            System.out.println("config class: " + self.getClass().getSimpleName());
            System.out.println("order sink " + o.sink().id() + ", payment sink " + p.sink().id()
                    + ", sinks created " + CREATED.get());
        };
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/config/LiteConfig.java
package com.orderhub.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)    // lite mode: no proxy, faster startup
public class LiteConfig {

    public record Greeting(String text) { }
    public record Greeter(Greeting greeting) { }

    @Bean
    public Greeting greeting() { return new Greeting("Welcome to OrderHub"); }

    @Bean
    public Greeter greeter(Greeting greeting) {   // correct lite style: take it as a parameter
        return new Greeter(greeting);
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `clock()` and `inventoryClient()` register third-party types as beans. `inventoryClient` receives Boot's auto-configured `RestClient.Builder` as a method parameter.
- `orderAuditor()` and `paymentAuditor()` both call `auditSink()`. Because `AppConfig` is a CGLIB subclass, the call is intercepted and returns the container's singleton, so only one `AuditSink` is ever created.
- Injecting `AppConfig` itself shows the proxy class name.
- `AuditSink.close()` is called automatically at shutdown because `@Bean` infers `close` and `shutdown` as destroy methods.
- `LiteConfig` uses `proxyBeanMethods = false` and passes `Greeting` as a parameter, which is correct in lite mode. Calling `greeting()` directly inside `greeter()` would create a second, unmanaged `Greeting`.

### Output
```text
config class: AppConfig$$SpringCGLIB$$0
order sink 1, payment sink 1, sinks created 1
```
On shutdown (Ctrl+C):
```text
AuditSink 1 closed
```
If you change `AppConfig` to `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)` without other changes:
```text
config class: AppConfig
order sink 2, payment sink 3, sinks created 3
```
Three sinks: one bean plus two unmanaged copies created by direct calls (and only the bean is closed at shutdown).

### Compare
| Aspect | Full `@Configuration` | Lite mode |
|---|---|---|
| CGLIB proxy | Yes | No |
| Inter-bean method calls | Return the singleton | Plain Java calls (new objects) |
| Startup cost | Slightly higher | Lower |
| Can be `final` | No | Yes |
| Native image friendliness | Needs proxy generation at build time | Simplest |
| Recommended style | Fine for apps | Used by Boot auto-config; pass dependencies as parameters |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What do `@Configuration` and `@Bean` do?**
   `@Configuration` marks a class as a source of bean definitions; each `@Bean` method's return value is registered as a bean, named after the method by default. Method parameters are injected by the container.
2. **When do you use `@Bean` instead of `@Component`?**
   For classes you do not own (library classes), when creation needs logic or builder configuration, when you need several differently configured instances of one class, or when creation is conditional.
3. **What is the default bean name for a `@Bean` method?**
   The method name. You can set names (and aliases) with `@Bean(name = {"a", "b"})`.

**Follow-up**
4. **Why does Spring proxy `@Configuration` classes?**
   To preserve singleton semantics when one `@Bean` method calls another. The CGLIB subclass intercepts the call and returns the bean from the container instead of running the method again.
5. **What is `proxyBeanMethods = false` and why does Boot use it?**
   It disables the CGLIB proxy (lite mode). Startup is faster and it suits native images. The trade-off is that inter-bean method calls are plain calls, so dependencies must be passed as method parameters.
6. **What happens if you put `@Bean` methods in a `@Component` class?**
   They work in lite mode: beans are registered, but calls between those methods are not intercepted.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **Can a `@Configuration` class or a `@Bean` method be `final` or `private`?**
   In full mode no: CGLIB must subclass the class and override the methods, so startup fails with an error for a final class, and private or final `@Bean` methods are not allowed. In lite mode final is fine.
8. **A `@Bean` returns an `ExecutorService`. Do you need to shut it down?**
   Usually not explicitly: Spring infers a public `close()` or `shutdown()` method as the destroy method for `@Bean` and calls it on context close. `ExecutorService` has `shutdown()` (and `close()` since Java 19), so it is shut down. Set `destroyMethod = ""` to disable that.
9. **Your `@Bean` method declares return type `Object` but returns a `StripeGateway`. Injection by `PaymentGateway` sometimes fails. Why?**
   Before the bean is created, Spring only knows the declared return type, so type matching for not-yet-created or lazy beans sees `Object`. Declare the most specific useful return type on `@Bean` methods.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you know the CGLIB interception trick, the lite-mode trade-off and when Java config beats scanning.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Calling another `@Bean` method in lite mode | Duplicate, unmanaged objects | Inject as a method parameter |
| `final` `@Configuration` class in full mode | Startup failure (CGLIB cannot subclass) | Remove `final` or use lite mode |
| Vague return type (`Object`, base class) | Type matching fails before creation | Declare a specific return type |
| Non-static `@Bean` for a `BeanPostProcessor` | Config class created early, warnings, lost processing | Make it `static` |
| Same bean declared via `@Component` and `@Bean` | `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` | Declare once |

### Best practices
- Group configuration by concern (`WebConfig`, `PaymentConfig`, `ClientsConfig`) instead of one giant class.
- Prefer `proxyBeanMethods = false` with parameter injection for new configuration classes; it is explicit and works in both modes.
- Use builders auto-configured by Boot (`RestClient.Builder`, `Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder`) so global settings apply.
- Keep business logic out of configuration classes.

### Compare with
| Approach | Strengths | Weaknesses |
|---|---|---|
| `@Bean` in `@Configuration` | Full control, third-party types, conditional | More code |
| Stereotype + scanning | Minimal code | Only for your own classes |
| XML config | Change wiring without recompiling | Verbose, not type-safe, legacy |
| Functional registration (`registerBean`) | Fast, no reflection | Less common, less tooling |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Create `com.orderhub.config.CoreConfig` with:
1. A `Clock` bean (UTC) and change `OrderService` to stamp new orders with `Instant.now(clock)`.
2. A `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` bean named `notificationExecutor` (core 2, max 4, queue 100, thread prefix `notify-`).
3. A `RestClient` bean named `inventoryClient` built from the auto-configured `RestClient.Builder` with base URL `http://localhost:8081`.
Use `proxyBeanMethods = false`.

Acceptance criteria: a unit test can pass `Clock.fixed(...)` to `OrderService` and assert the timestamp; threads named `notify-1` appear when the executor runs a task; no `@Bean` method calls another `@Bean` method.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/config/CoreConfig.java
package com.orderhub.config;

import java.time.Clock;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClient;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class CoreConfig {

    @Bean
    public Clock clock() {
        return Clock.systemUTC();
    }

    @Bean
    public ThreadPoolTaskExecutor notificationExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        ex.setCorePoolSize(2);
        ex.setMaxPoolSize(4);
        ex.setQueueCapacity(100);
        ex.setThreadNamePrefix("notify-");
        ex.setWaitForTasksToCompleteOnShutdown(true);
        return ex;                 // Spring calls initialize() (InitializingBean) and shutdown() on close
    }

    @Bean
    public RestClient inventoryClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        return builder.baseUrl("http://localhost:8081").build();
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java (excerpt)
package com.orderhub.order;

import java.time.Clock;
import java.time.Instant;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final Clock clock;
    public OrderService(Clock clock) { this.clock = clock; }

    public Order newOrder(long customerId) {
        return new Order(null, customerId, Instant.now(clock));
    }
}

record Order(Long id, long customerId, Instant createdAt) { }

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderServiceClockTest.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

import java.time.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class OrderServiceClockTest {
    @Test
    void stampsWithInjectedClock() {
        Instant fixed = Instant.parse("2026-01-01T10:00:00Z");
        OrderService service = new OrderService(Clock.fixed(fixed, ZoneOffset.UTC));
        assertEquals(fixed, service.newOrder(7).createdAt());
    }
}
```
Injecting a `Clock` is the standard way to make time-dependent code testable.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: Why does Spring create a CGLIB proxy for a `@Configuration` class?
A: So calls between `@Bean` methods return the existing singleton from the container instead of creating new objects.

Q: What does `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)` change?
A: It disables the CGLIB proxy (lite mode): faster startup, but inter-bean method calls become plain calls, so dependencies must be method parameters.

Q: What is the default name of a bean declared with `@Bean`?
A: The name of the method.

Q: When should you use `@Bean` instead of a stereotype annotation?
A: For third-party classes, creation that needs logic or builders, multiple configured instances, or conditional creation.

Q: Which destroy methods does Spring infer for `@Bean` beans?
A: A public `close()` or `shutdown()` method, unless `destroyMethod = ""` is set.

**Related:** [[Stereotype Annotations]] · [[Bean Lifecycle Callbacks]] · [[Conditional Beans]] · [[ApplicationContext and Bean Definitions]]

# Bean Scopes

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: singleton and prototype Spring 1.0; web scopes Spring 2.0; @RequestScope Spring 4.3**

> **30-second interview answer:** A scope decides how many instances of a bean exist and how long they live. Singleton (the default) is one instance per container; prototype is a new instance on every lookup; the web scopes request, session and application tie the instance to an HTTP request, an HTTP session or the servlet context. The classic trap is injecting a prototype or request-scoped bean into a singleton: injection happens once, so you get one instance forever unless you use a scoped proxy, `ObjectProvider` or `@Lookup`.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Things in an office.

| Office item | Scope |
|---|---|
| The one shared printer | Singleton: one per container, shared by all |
| Paper cups: take a new one every time | Prototype: new instance per request to the container |
| A visitor badge valid for one visit | Request scope: one per HTTP request |
| A locker assigned for your whole stay | Session scope: one per HTTP session |
| The building's notice board | Application scope: one per `ServletContext` |
| A receptionist who fetches "the current visitor's badge" each time you ask | Scoped proxy |

### What it is
- **singleton:** one shared instance per Spring container (not per JVM, not per class loader). Created eagerly by default. Must be thread-safe, because all requests share it.
- **prototype:** a new instance each time it is injected or requested with `getBean`. Spring creates and initialises it, then forgets it: **destroy callbacks are not called**.
- **request:** one instance per HTTP request (`@RequestScope`).
- **session:** one per HTTP session (`@SessionScope`).
- **application:** one per `ServletContext` (`@ApplicationScope`); differs from singleton only if several contexts share one servlet context.
- **websocket:** one per WebSocket session.
- **Scoped proxy:** a stand-in object injected into longer-lived beans; on every method call it looks up the real target in the current scope. `@RequestScope` and `@SessionScope` enable it by default (`proxyMode = TARGET_CLASS`).

### Why it exists
Most services are stateless, so one shared instance is efficient. But some objects hold per-use state: a builder, a per-request context (current tenant, correlation id), a shopping cart in a session. Scopes let the container manage those lifetimes instead of you passing objects around manually.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Need bean X"] --> B{"Scope of X"}
    B -- "singleton" --> C["Return from singleton cache, create once if missing"]
    B -- "prototype" --> D["Create new instance, inject, init, hand over, forget"]
    B -- "request or session" --> E["Ask Scope object: RequestScope looks in RequestAttributes of current thread"]
    E --> F{"Exists for this request?"}
    F -- "yes" --> G["Return it"]
    F -- "no" --> H["Create, store in request attributes, register destruction callback"]
```

The prototype-in-singleton trap and the scoped proxy fix:

```text
WITHOUT proxy                                   WITH scoped proxy
CheckoutController (singleton, created once)    CheckoutController (singleton)
   cart ------> Cart@1  (injected once)            cart ------> CartProxy (singleton stand-in)
                                                                  |  every call: "which cart for
request A uses Cart@1                                             |  the current request?"
request B uses Cart@1   <- shared state bug!                      +--> Cart@A for request A
                                                                  +--> Cart@B for request B
```

- Request and session scopes rely on `RequestContextHolder`, a thread-local set by `DispatcherServlet` (or `RequestContextFilter`). Accessing a request-scoped bean outside a request thread (for example in `@Async` or a scheduler) throws `ScopeNotActiveException` ("No thread-bound request found").
- Prototype beans that hold resources must be cleaned up by the caller, because Spring does not track them after handing them over.
- Custom scopes implement `org.springframework.beans.factory.config.Scope` (Spring Cloud's `@RefreshScope` is one).

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/scope/ScopeDemo.java
package com.orderhub.scope;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.ConfigurableBeanFactory;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.web.context.annotation.RequestScope;

// Prototype: a fresh builder every time
@Component
@Scope(ConfigurableBeanFactory.SCOPE_PROTOTYPE)
class InvoiceBuilder {
    private static final AtomicInteger COUNT = new AtomicInteger();
    private final int instance = COUNT.incrementAndGet();
    private final List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    InvoiceBuilder add(String line) { lines.add(line); return this; }
    String build() { return "invoice#" + instance + " " + lines; }
}

// Request scope: one per HTTP request, proxied by default
@Component
@RequestScope
class RequestContext {
    private final String requestId = UUID.randomUUID().toString().substring(0, 8);
    String requestId() { return requestId; }
}

// Singleton that WRONGLY holds a prototype directly
@Component
class BrokenInvoiceService {
    private final InvoiceBuilder builder;                 // injected ONCE
    BrokenInvoiceService(InvoiceBuilder builder) { this.builder = builder; }
    String invoice(String item) { return builder.add(item).build(); }
}

// Singleton that correctly asks for a new prototype each time
@Component
class InvoiceService {
    private final ObjectProvider<InvoiceBuilder> builders;
    InvoiceService(ObjectProvider<InvoiceBuilder> builders) { this.builders = builders; }
    String invoice(String item) { return builders.getObject().add(item).build(); }
}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/scopes")
class ScopeController {
    private final BrokenInvoiceService broken;
    private final InvoiceService fixed;
    private final RequestContext requestContext;          // actually a proxy

    ScopeController(BrokenInvoiceService broken, InvoiceService fixed, RequestContext requestContext) {
        this.broken = broken;
        this.fixed = fixed;
        this.requestContext = requestContext;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{item}")
    String demo(@PathVariable String item) {
        return "broken=" + broken.invoice(item)
                + " | fixed=" + fixed.invoice(item)
                + " | requestId same within request: "
                + requestContext.requestId().equals(requestContext.requestId())
                + " | proxy class: " + requestContext.getClass().getSimpleName();
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `InvoiceBuilder` is prototype. `BrokenInvoiceService` is a singleton that receives one builder at construction, so every call appends to the same list: state leaks across requests and threads.
- `InvoiceService` injects `ObjectProvider<InvoiceBuilder>` and calls `getObject()` per use, getting a fresh builder each time. Alternatives: a `@Lookup` method, or `@Scope(value = "prototype", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)`, which creates a new target on every method call (usually surprising, so avoid it for builders).
- `RequestContext` is request-scoped. The controller (a singleton) receives a CGLIB proxy; each call to `requestId()` inside one request resolves the same target, while a new request gets a new target.
### Output
```http
GET /api/scopes/book
```
```text
broken=invoice#1 [book] | fixed=invoice#2 [book] | requestId same within request: true | proxy class: RequestContext$$SpringCGLIB$$0
```
```http
GET /api/scopes/pen
```
```text
broken=invoice#1 [book, pen] | fixed=invoice#3 [pen] | requestId same within request: true | proxy class: RequestContext$$SpringCGLIB$$0
```
The broken service keeps growing the same invoice #1; the fixed one gets a new builder every call.

### Compare
| Scope | Instances | Destroy callback | Thread-safety concern | Typical use |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| singleton | One per container | Yes | High: shared by all threads | Stateless services, repositories |
| prototype | One per lookup | No | Low if not shared | Stateful builders, non-thread-safe helpers |
| request | One per HTTP request | Yes, at request end | Low | Per-request context, correlation id |
| session | One per HTTP session | Yes, at session end | Medium (parallel tabs) | Carts in server-rendered apps |
| application | One per servlet context | Yes | High | Rare; shared web-level state |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What bean scopes does Spring support?**
   Singleton (default) and prototype in any context; request, session, application and websocket in web-aware contexts. You can also register custom scopes.
2. **Is a Spring singleton the same as the GoF Singleton pattern?**
   No. A Spring singleton is one instance per container per bean definition. You can have two singleton beans of the same class with different names, and two contexts each with their own instance. GoF Singleton is one instance per class loader enforced by the class itself.
3. **Are singleton beans thread-safe?**
   Not automatically. Spring does nothing to make them thread-safe. Keep them stateless (only final, immutable or thread-safe fields) or protect mutable state with concurrency tools.

**Follow-up**
4. **Does Spring manage the full lifecycle of prototype beans?**
   No. It creates, injects and initialises them (including `@PostConstruct`), then hands them over and forgets them. Destroy callbacks such as `@PreDestroy` are never called; the client must release resources.
5. **What happens when you inject a prototype bean into a singleton?**
   The prototype is created once, when the singleton is created, and the singleton keeps that one instance forever. To get a new instance per use, inject `ObjectProvider<T>` or `Provider<T>`, use a `@Lookup` method, or use a scoped proxy.
6. **How does a request-scoped bean work when injected into a singleton controller?**
   The controller receives a scoped proxy. On every method call the proxy looks up the real instance in the current request's attributes (through a thread-local `RequestContextHolder`) and delegates to it.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **A request-scoped bean is used inside an `@Async` method and you get "No thread-bound request found". Why?**
   Request attributes live in a thread-local of the request thread. The async method runs on a pool thread with no request bound. Copy the values you need (for example the request id) into method parameters before going async, or use a `TaskDecorator` to propagate context.
8. **In production, order totals sometimes include items from other customers. The service is a singleton with a `List<Item> items` field. What happened?**
   A singleton is shared across all request threads, so the mutable field is shared state with race conditions. Make the service stateless (local variables, method parameters) or move per-request state into a request-scoped bean or a value object.
9. **What does `@Scope(value = "prototype", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)` do when injected into a singleton?**
   The singleton gets a proxy, and every method call on it fetches a new prototype instance. Two consecutive calls like `builder.add(x)` and `builder.build()` therefore hit two different objects, which surprises people. `ObjectProvider` is usually clearer.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you understand that injection happens once, and whether you design singletons to be stateless and thread-safe.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Mutable fields in a singleton service | Data leaks between concurrent requests | Stateless services, local variables |
| Prototype injected directly into a singleton | Same instance reused forever | `ObjectProvider`, `@Lookup` |
| Expecting `@PreDestroy` on prototypes | Resources never released | Clean up in client code |
| Request-scoped bean used in async or scheduled code | `ScopeNotActiveException` | Pass values explicitly or propagate context |
| Session scope for REST APIs behind a load balancer | Sticky sessions, memory per user, scaling pain | Stateless APIs with tokens; store state in DB or Redis |

### Best practices
- Default to singleton, and make singletons stateless and immutable after construction.
- Use prototype only for objects with per-use mutable state; obtain them via `ObjectProvider`.
- Keep request-scoped beans small (ids, tenant, user info) and never pass them to other threads.
- For REST APIs avoid session scope; prefer stateless design.

### Compare with
| Way to get a fresh object | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| `ObjectProvider<T>.getObject()` | Explicit, lazy, handles optional | Slight Spring API coupling |
| `jakarta.inject.Provider<T>` | Standard API | Needs `jakarta.inject` dependency |
| `@Lookup` method | No API in business code | Magic: method body overridden by CGLIB |
| Scoped proxy on prototype | Transparent | New target per call, surprising |
| Plain `new` | Simplest | No injection or container features |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Add a per-request correlation id:
1. Create a `@RequestScope` bean `RequestInfo` that generates a correlation id, or uses the incoming `X-Correlation-Id` header if present (inject `HttpServletRequest` into it).
2. Inject `RequestInfo` into `OrderService` (a singleton) and include the correlation id in a log line when an order is placed.
3. Return the id in an `X-Correlation-Id` response header from `OrderController`.

Acceptance criteria: two concurrent requests log different ids; a request sending `X-Correlation-Id: abc` gets `abc` back; `OrderService` stays a singleton.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/common/RequestInfo.java
package com.orderhub.common;

import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import java.util.UUID;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.context.annotation.RequestScope;

@Component
@RequestScope                    // proxyMode TARGET_CLASS by default
public class RequestInfo {
    private final String correlationId;

    public RequestInfo(HttpServletRequest request) {   // Spring injects a proxy to the current request
        String header = request.getHeader("X-Correlation-Id");
        this.correlationId = (header != null && !header.isBlank()) ? header : UUID.randomUUID().toString();
    }

    public String correlationId() { return correlationId; }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java (excerpt)
package com.orderhub.order;

import com.orderhub.common.RequestInfo;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class);
    private final RequestInfo requestInfo;      // proxy, safe in a singleton

    public OrderService(RequestInfo requestInfo) { this.requestInfo = requestInfo; }

    public long place(long customerId) {
        long orderId = System.nanoTime() % 100000;
        log.info("order {} placed for customer {} [correlationId={}]",
                orderId, customerId, requestInfo.correlationId());
        return orderId;
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderController.java (excerpt)
package com.orderhub.order;

import com.orderhub.common.RequestInfo;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {
    private final OrderService service;
    private final RequestInfo requestInfo;

    public OrderController(OrderService service, RequestInfo requestInfo) {
        this.service = service;
        this.requestInfo = requestInfo;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Long> place(@RequestParam long customerId) {
        long id = service.place(customerId);
        return ResponseEntity.ok()
                .header("X-Correlation-Id", requestInfo.correlationId())
                .body(id);
    }
}
```
Log:
```text
INFO  c.o.order.OrderService : order 48213 placed for customer 7 [correlationId=abc]
```
In Phase 21 this idea is replaced by MDC and Micrometer Tracing, which propagate ids automatically.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What is the default Spring bean scope?
A: Singleton: one instance per container per bean definition.

Q: Are Spring singleton beans thread-safe by default?
A: No; Spring does not synchronise them, so they must be stateless or protect their own mutable state.

Q: Does Spring call `@PreDestroy` on prototype-scoped beans?
A: No; Spring forgets prototypes after creating them, so the client must clean up.

Q: How do you get a new prototype instance per call inside a Spring singleton?
A: Inject `ObjectProvider<T>` (or `Provider<T>`) and call `getObject()`, or use a `@Lookup` method.

Q: How does a request-scoped bean work inside a singleton controller in Spring?
A: A scoped proxy is injected; each call looks up the current request's instance via a thread-local and delegates to it.

**Related:** [[Bean Lifecycle Callbacks]] · [[Race Conditions and synchronized]] · [[Constructor vs Setter vs Field Injection]]

# Bean Lifecycle Callbacks

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: InitializingBean Spring 1.0; @PostConstruct support Spring 2.5 (jakarta.annotation since Spring 6); SmartLifecycle Spring 3.0**

> **30-second interview answer:** A bean is instantiated, its dependencies are injected, `Aware` callbacks run, then `BeanPostProcessor`s run before initialisation, then init callbacks in this order: `@PostConstruct`, `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`, a custom `initMethod`. After that, post-processors run again, which is where AOP proxies are created. On shutdown, destroy callbacks run in order: `@PreDestroy`, `DisposableBean.destroy()`, custom `destroyMethod`. I use `@PostConstruct` and `@PreDestroy` in my code, and `SmartLifecycle` for things that must start after the whole context is ready, like consumers.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A new employee's first and last day.

| Employee | Bean |
|---|---|
| Signing the contract | Instantiation (constructor) |
| Receiving laptop and badge | Dependency injection |
| Learning their own employee number and manager's name | `Aware` callbacks (`BeanNameAware`, `ApplicationContextAware`) |
| HR checks and adds a uniform | `BeanPostProcessor` before and after init (proxy wrapping) |
| Orientation day: set up desk, log in | `@PostConstruct` and init methods |
| Whole office opens for customers | `SmartLifecycle.start()` and `ApplicationReadyEvent` |
| Exit interview, hand back the badge | `@PreDestroy` and destroy methods |

### What it is
- **Init callback:** code that runs once after all dependencies are injected: `@PostConstruct` (Jakarta annotation), `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`, or `@Bean(initMethod = "...")`.
- **Destroy callback:** code that runs when the container closes: `@PreDestroy`, `DisposableBean.destroy()`, `@Bean(destroyMethod = "...")` or the inferred `close` and `shutdown`.
- **Aware interfaces:** give a bean access to container objects (`BeanNameAware`, `BeanFactoryAware`, `ApplicationContextAware`, `EnvironmentAware`).
- **BeanPostProcessor:** a container-wide hook that sees every bean before and after initialisation and may return a different object (a proxy).
- **SmartLifecycle:** for beans that start and stop with the context as a whole (`start()` after all singletons are created, `stop()` before destruction), ordered by `getPhase()`.

### Why it exists
Constructors run before field and setter injection and before proxies exist. Some work must wait until a bean is fully wired (warm a cache, validate config, open a connection), and resources must be released cleanly on shutdown (close pools, flush buffers). Callbacks give you exact, well-defined moments for both.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["1. Instantiate (constructor injection)"] --> B["2. Populate properties (field and setter injection)"]
    B --> C["3. Aware callbacks: BeanNameAware, BeanFactoryAware, ApplicationContextAware"]
    C --> D["4. BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization (runs @PostConstruct)"]
    D --> E["5. InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet"]
    E --> F["6. Custom initMethod"]
    F --> G["7. BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization (AOP proxy created here)"]
    G --> H["8. Bean ready, in singleton cache"]
    H --> I["9. All singletons done: SmartInitializingSingleton, SmartLifecycle.start, ContextRefreshedEvent, ApplicationReadyEvent"]
    I --> J["10. Shutdown: SmartLifecycle.stop, @PreDestroy, DisposableBean.destroy, destroyMethod"]
```

Important consequences:
- `@PostConstruct` is executed by `CommonAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` in step 4, which is why it runs before `afterPropertiesSet`.
- The proxy is created in step 7, **after** `@PostConstruct`. Calling a `@Transactional` method of the same bean from `@PostConstruct` runs on the raw object, so no transaction. Use `ApplicationReadyEvent` or `SmartLifecycle` for work that needs proxies.
- Destruction order is the reverse of dependency order: a bean is destroyed before the beans it depends on.
- Spring Boot registers a JVM shutdown hook, so destroy callbacks run on SIGTERM and Ctrl+C, but not on `kill -9`.
- Graceful shutdown (`server.shutdown=graceful`, default in Boot 3.4+) stops accepting requests and lets in-flight ones finish, as a `SmartLifecycle` phase.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/lifecycle/LifecycleDemo.java
package com.orderhub.lifecycle;

import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import jakarta.annotation.PreDestroy;
import org.springframework.beans.BeansException;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.BeanNameAware;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.DisposableBean;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.InitializingBean;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.BeanPostProcessor;
import org.springframework.boot.context.event.ApplicationReadyEvent;
import org.springframework.context.SmartLifecycle;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class LifecycleDemo {

    public static class ProductCache implements BeanNameAware, InitializingBean, DisposableBean {
        private final String source;
        public ProductCache(String source) { this.source = source; System.out.println("1 constructor"); }
        public void setBeanName(String name) { System.out.println("3 BeanNameAware: " + name); }
        @PostConstruct void postConstruct() { System.out.println("4 @PostConstruct: warm cache from " + source); }
        public void afterPropertiesSet() { System.out.println("5 afterPropertiesSet"); }
        void customInit() { System.out.println("6 initMethod"); }
        @PreDestroy void preDestroy() { System.out.println("D1 @PreDestroy"); }
        public void destroy() { System.out.println("D2 DisposableBean.destroy"); }
        void customDestroy() { System.out.println("D3 destroyMethod"); }
    }

    public static class OrderConsumer implements SmartLifecycle {
        private volatile boolean running;
        public void start() { running = true; System.out.println("8 SmartLifecycle.start: consumer polling"); }
        public void stop() { running = false; System.out.println("S1 SmartLifecycle.stop: consumer stopped"); }
        public boolean isRunning() { return running; }
    }

    @Bean(initMethod = "customInit", destroyMethod = "customDestroy")
    public ProductCache productCache() {
        return new ProductCache("inventory-db");
    }

    @Bean
    public OrderConsumer orderConsumer() { return new OrderConsumer(); }

    // static: post-processors must be created before ordinary beans
    @Bean
    public static BeanPostProcessor tracingPostProcessor() {
        return new BeanPostProcessor() {
            public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String name) throws BeansException {
                if (bean instanceof ProductCache) System.out.println("4a BPP before init");
                return bean;
            }
            public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String name) throws BeansException {
                if (bean instanceof ProductCache) System.out.println("7 BPP after init (proxies are made here)");
                return bean;
            }
        };
    }

    @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)
    public void ready() { System.out.println("9 ApplicationReadyEvent: app is serving traffic"); }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- The constructor runs first. `BeanNameAware` gives the bean its name (`productCache`).
- Our post-processor's `postProcessBeforeInitialization` and Spring's `CommonAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` (which invokes `@PostConstruct`) both run in the "before init" step. Spring's internal annotation processor implements `PriorityOrdered`, so it runs before our unordered processor: that is why "4 @PostConstruct" prints before "4a BPP before init".
- Then `afterPropertiesSet` and the custom `initMethod`, then the after-init post-processing where AOP would wrap the bean.
- `OrderConsumer.start()` runs only after every singleton is ready, which is the right moment to begin consuming messages.
- `ApplicationReadyEvent` fires after the runners and once the app is ready to serve.
- On shutdown, `SmartLifecycle.stop()` runs first (stop consuming before resources close), then the three destroy callbacks in fixed order.

### Output
```text
1 constructor
3 BeanNameAware: productCache
4 @PostConstruct: warm cache from inventory-db
4a BPP before init
5 afterPropertiesSet
6 initMethod
7 BPP after init (proxies are made here)
8 SmartLifecycle.start: consumer polling
9 ApplicationReadyEvent: app is serving traffic
```
On Ctrl+C:
```text
S1 SmartLifecycle.stop: consumer stopped
D1 @PreDestroy
D2 DisposableBean.destroy
D3 destroyMethod
```

### Compare
| Init option | Coupling to Spring | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `@PostConstruct` | Jakarta annotation only | Your own classes (default choice) |
| `InitializingBean` | Spring interface | Framework or library code |
| `@Bean(initMethod)` | None in the class | Third-party classes you cannot annotate |
| `SmartLifecycle` | Spring interface | Start and stop with the whole context, phased |
| `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener | Spring Boot event | Work after startup that needs proxies and a running server |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **Describe the Spring bean lifecycle.**
   Instantiate, inject dependencies, call `Aware` methods, run `BeanPostProcessor` before-init, run init callbacks (`@PostConstruct`, `afterPropertiesSet`, `initMethod`), run `BeanPostProcessor` after-init (proxies), bean is ready. On shutdown: `@PreDestroy`, `destroy()`, `destroyMethod`.
2. **Why use `@PostConstruct` instead of the constructor?**
   Field and setter injection happen after the constructor, so injected fields are null inside it. `@PostConstruct` runs once all dependencies are set. With pure constructor injection, simple setup can live in the constructor, but `@PostConstruct` is still clearer for heavier initialisation.
3. **In which order do the three init mechanisms run?**
   `@PostConstruct`, then `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`, then the custom `initMethod`. Destroy follows the same pattern: `@PreDestroy`, `DisposableBean.destroy()`, `destroyMethod`.

**Follow-up**
4. **What is a `BeanPostProcessor` and give a real example?**
   A hook that receives every bean before and after initialisation and can modify or replace it. `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` performs field injection; `AnnotationAwareAspectJAutoProxyCreator` wraps beans in AOP proxies for `@Transactional`, `@Async` and `@Cacheable`.
5. **Why does a `@Transactional` call from `@PostConstruct` not start a transaction?**
   `@PostConstruct` runs on the raw bean before the after-init post-processor creates the proxy, and the call is a self-invocation anyway. Use an `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener or `SmartLifecycle` that calls the bean through its proxy.
6. **When are `@PreDestroy` methods not called?**
   For prototype beans, when the JVM is killed with `kill -9` or crashes, and when the context is never closed (no shutdown hook in a plain `AnnotationConfigApplicationContext` unless you call `close()` or `registerShutdownHook()`).

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **Your Kafka consumer starts processing messages before the database cache is warmed. How do you fix the order?**
   Start consumption in `SmartLifecycle.start()` (or rely on the listener container, which is itself a `SmartLifecycle`), and warm the cache in `@PostConstruct` of a singleton. All singletons, including their init callbacks, finish before any `start()` is called; `getPhase()` orders multiple lifecycle beans.
8. **Kubernetes sends SIGTERM and in-flight requests fail. What lifecycle features help?**
   Graceful shutdown (`server.shutdown=graceful`, `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase=30s`) stops accepting new requests and waits for active ones; `@PreDestroy` methods then release resources. Combine with a readiness probe that turns off first, so the load balancer stops routing.
9. **A `@PostConstruct` method throws an exception. What happens?**
   Bean creation fails with `BeanCreationException`, and since singletons are created at startup, the whole application fails to start. This is useful for validating configuration early.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you can order the callbacks, know where proxies appear, and pick the right hook for startup and shutdown work in production.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Heavy remote calls in `@PostConstruct` | Slow startup, app fails if the remote is down | Lazy loading, `ApplicationReadyEvent`, retries |
| Calling `@Transactional` or `@Async` methods from `@PostConstruct` | No proxy yet, annotations ignored | `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener |
| Expecting cleanup on prototypes | Leaked connections or threads | Client closes resources |
| Non-static `@Bean` for a `BeanPostProcessor` | Early creation warnings, some beans not post-processed | Make it static |
| Missing `jakarta.annotation-api` in a plain Spring app | `@PostConstruct` ignored silently | Add the dependency (Boot includes it) |

### Best practices
- Prefer `@PostConstruct` and `@PreDestroy` for your own beans; `initMethod` and `destroyMethod` for third-party classes.
- Keep init callbacks fast and local; do remote warm-up after readiness, ideally in the background.
- Use `SmartLifecycle` for components that consume external work (message listeners, schedulers) so they start last and stop first.
- Enable graceful shutdown and set a phase timeout in containerised deployments.

### Compare with
| Hook | Runs | Proxy available? |
|---|---|---|
| Constructor | Instantiation | No |
| `@PostConstruct` | After injection, per bean | No (raw bean) |
| `SmartInitializingSingleton` | After all singletons are created | Yes |
| `SmartLifecycle.start()` | After refresh, by phase | Yes |
| `ApplicationReadyEvent` | After runners, app ready | Yes |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Add a product price cache with a proper lifecycle:
1. `ProductPriceCache` (`@Component`) loads all product prices from `ProductRepository` into a `ConcurrentHashMap` in `@PostConstruct` and logs how many it loaded.
2. On `@PreDestroy` it logs "price cache cleared" and clears the map.
3. Add an `OrderEventsConsumer` implementing `SmartLifecycle` that logs "consuming order events" on start and "stopped consuming" on stop.
4. Enable graceful shutdown in `application.yml`.

Acceptance criteria: startup logs show the cache load before the consumer start; shutdown logs show the consumer stop before the cache clear.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/product/ProductPriceCache.java
package com.orderhub.product;

import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import jakarta.annotation.PreDestroy;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ProductPriceCache {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ProductPriceCache.class);
    private final ProductRepository repository;
    private final Map<Long, BigDecimal> prices = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public ProductPriceCache(ProductRepository repository) { this.repository = repository; }

    @PostConstruct
    void load() {
        repository.findAll().forEach(p -> prices.put(p.id(), p.price()));
        log.info("price cache loaded {} products", prices.size());
    }

    public Optional<BigDecimal> price(long productId) { return Optional.ofNullable(prices.get(productId)); }

    @PreDestroy
    void clear() {
        prices.clear();
        log.info("price cache cleared");
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderEventsConsumer.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.context.SmartLifecycle;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class OrderEventsConsumer implements SmartLifecycle {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderEventsConsumer.class);
    private volatile boolean running;

    @Override public void start() { running = true; log.info("consuming order events"); }
    @Override public void stop() { running = false; log.info("stopped consuming"); }
    @Override public boolean isRunning() { return running; }
    @Override public int getPhase() { return Integer.MAX_VALUE - 100; }   // start late, stop early
}
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml
server:
  shutdown: graceful
spring:
  lifecycle:
    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 20s
```
Startup:
```text
INFO  c.o.product.ProductPriceCache : price cache loaded 0 products
INFO  c.o.order.OrderEventsConsumer : consuming order events
```
Shutdown:
```text
INFO  c.o.order.OrderEventsConsumer : stopped consuming
INFO  c.o.product.ProductPriceCache : price cache cleared
```
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: In what order do Spring init callbacks run?
A: `@PostConstruct`, then `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`, then the custom `initMethod`.

Q: At which lifecycle step does Spring create AOP proxies for a bean?
A: In `BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization`, after all init callbacks.

Q: Why does calling a `@Transactional` method from `@PostConstruct` not start a transaction?
A: The proxy does not exist yet and the call is on the raw object, so no transactional advice runs.

Q: What is `SmartLifecycle` used for in Spring?
A: For components that start after all singletons are ready and stop before destruction, ordered by phase, such as message consumers.

Q: When are Spring `@PreDestroy` methods skipped?
A: For prototype beans, on `kill -9` or a crash, and when the context is never closed.

**Related:** [[Bean Scopes]] · [[ApplicationContext and Bean Definitions]] · [[@Configuration and @Bean]] · [[Spring Application Events]]

# Profiles and Environment-Specific Config

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: @Profile Spring 3.1; profile groups and spring.config.activate.on-profile Boot 2.4**

> **30-second interview answer:** A profile is a named set of configuration and beans, like `dev`, `test` or `prod`. Spring Boot loads `application.yml` first and then `application-{profile}.yml` for each active profile, with later values overriding earlier ones. Beans annotated `@Profile("dev")` exist only when that profile is active. I activate profiles with `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` in each environment, keep secrets out of the files, and remember that environment variables and command-line arguments override file values.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A phone with modes: Home, Work and Flight.

| Phone modes | Spring profiles |
|---|---|
| Base settings shared by all modes | `application.yml` |
| Work mode turns on email sync and silences games | `application-prod.yml` plus `@Profile("prod")` beans |
| Flight mode disables the radio entirely | A bean excluded by `@Profile("!prod")` |
| You switch mode from the quick menu | `--spring.profiles.active=prod` or an environment variable |
| A temporary override like "do not disturb for 1 hour" | Command-line argument that beats file values |

### What it is
- **Profile:** a label that activates profile-specific property files and beans.
- **Active profiles:** set with `spring.profiles.active` (property, `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` env var, `-Dspring.profiles.active`, `--spring.profiles.active`, or `@ActiveProfiles` in tests).
- **Default profile:** `default` is active when no profile is set.
- **`@Profile`:** on a `@Component` or `@Bean` method; supports expressions like `"!prod"`, `"dev & local"`, `"dev or test"` (the logical operator words or their symbol forms).
- **Profile groups (Boot 2.4+):** `spring.profiles.group.prod=prod-db,prod-mq` activates several at once.
- **Multi-document YAML:** one file with `---` separators and `spring.config.activate.on-profile` per document.
- **Environment:** the Spring abstraction that holds active profiles and an ordered list of property sources.
- **Property source precedence (high to low, simplified):** command-line arguments, `SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON`, Java system properties, OS environment variables, profile-specific files outside the jar, profile-specific files inside the jar, `application.yml` outside the jar, `application.yml` inside the jar, `@PropertySource`, defaults.

### Why it exists
The same jar must run on a laptop (H2 or local Postgres, verbose logs), in CI (Testcontainers), and in production (managed database, JSON logs, real payment provider). Without profiles you would rebuild for each environment or fill the code with if-statements on environment names. The Twelve-Factor App principle "build once, configure per environment" depends on this.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["SpringApplication.run"] --> B["Create Environment"]
    B --> C["Add property sources: command line, env vars, system props"]
    C --> D["ConfigDataEnvironmentPostProcessor loads application.yml"]
    D --> E["Determine active profiles (spring.profiles.active, groups)"]
    E --> F["Load application-dev.yml, application-local.yml (last active wins on conflicts)"]
    F --> G["Bean definition phase: @Profile condition checked per bean"]
    G --> H["Beans resolve properties from the Environment in precedence order"]
```

```text
Lookup of orderhub.payment.provider (first hit wins)
  1. --orderhub.payment.provider=stripe          (command line)
  2. ORDERHUB_PAYMENT_PROVIDER=stripe            (environment variable, relaxed binding)
  3. application-prod.yml                         (profile-specific)
  4. application.yml                              (base)
```

- `@Profile` is implemented as a `@Conditional(ProfileCondition.class)`, evaluated when bean definitions are registered. Excluded beans never get a definition.
- **Relaxed binding:** `orderhub.payment.provider` can be set by env var `ORDERHUB_PAYMENT_PROVIDER` (dots become underscores, upper case).
- `spring.profiles.active` cannot be set inside a profile-specific file (Boot 2.4+ rejects it), which prevents confusing chains.
- `spring.profiles.include` adds profiles on top of the active ones; groups are the recommended replacement.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml  (shared by all environments)
spring:
  application:
    name: orderhub
  profiles:
    group:
      prod: "prod-db,json-logs"
orderhub:
  payment:
    provider: fake
  free-shipping-threshold: 50.00
logging:
  level:
    com.orderhub: INFO
---
# Same file, second document: only when the dev profile is active
spring:
  config:
    activate:
      on-profile: dev
logging:
  level:
    com.orderhub: DEBUG
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application-prod.yml
orderhub:
  payment:
    provider: stripe
  free-shipping-threshold: 75.00
server:
  shutdown: graceful
```

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/NotificationConfig.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationRunner;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Profile;
import org.springframework.core.env.Environment;

interface EmailSender { void send(String to, String body); }

class SmtpEmailSender implements EmailSender {
    public void send(String to, String body) { System.out.println("SMTP to " + to + ": " + body); }
}

class ConsoleEmailSender implements EmailSender {
    public void send(String to, String body) { System.out.println("[console email] to " + to + ": " + body); }
}

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class NotificationConfig {

    @Bean
    @Profile("prod")                 // real emails only in prod
    EmailSender smtpEmailSender() { return new SmtpEmailSender(); }

    @Bean
    @Profile("!prod")                // everywhere else: print to console
    EmailSender consoleEmailSender() { return new ConsoleEmailSender(); }

    @Bean
    ApplicationRunner profileReport(Environment env, EmailSender sender,
                                    @Value("${orderhub.payment.provider}") String provider,
                                    @Value("${orderhub.free-shipping-threshold}") String threshold) {
        return args -> {
            System.out.println("active profiles: " + String.join(",", env.getActiveProfiles()));
            System.out.println("payment provider: " + provider + ", free shipping from " + threshold);
            System.out.println("email sender: " + sender.getClass().getSimpleName());
            System.out.println("is prod? " + env.acceptsProfiles(org.springframework.core.env.Profiles.of("prod")));
        };
    }
}
```

```java
// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/notification/NotificationProfileTest.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.ActiveProfiles;

@SpringBootTest
@ActiveProfiles("test")
class NotificationProfileTest {
    @Autowired EmailSender sender;

    @Test
    void nonProdUsesConsoleSender() {
        assertThat(sender).isInstanceOf(ConsoleEmailSender.class);
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `application.yml` holds defaults. The second YAML document applies only with `dev` active and raises the log level.
- `application-prod.yml` overrides the provider and threshold. The group `prod` also activates `prod-db` and `json-logs`, which could have their own files.
- Exactly one `EmailSender` bean exists in any environment thanks to complementary profile expressions `prod` and `!prod`.
- `Environment` exposes active profiles and `acceptsProfiles`. Prefer `@Profile` over checking profiles in code.
- The test activates `test`, so the console sender is chosen.

### Output
Run with `java -jar orderhub.jar --spring.profiles.active=dev`:
```text
active profiles: dev
payment provider: fake, free shipping from 50.00
email sender: ConsoleEmailSender
is prod? false
```
Run with `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod java -jar orderhub.jar`:
```text
active profiles: prod,prod-db,json-logs
payment provider: stripe, free shipping from 75.00
email sender: SmtpEmailSender
is prod? true
```
Run with `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod java -jar orderhub.jar --orderhub.payment.provider=adyen`:
```text
active profiles: prod,prod-db,json-logs
payment provider: adyen, free shipping from 75.00
email sender: SmtpEmailSender
is prod? true
```

### Compare
| Old way | Modern Boot way |
|---|---|
| Separate builds per environment | One jar, profile chosen at runtime |
| `if (env.equals("prod"))` in code | `@Profile("prod")` beans and profile-specific files |
| `spring.profiles` key in YAML documents | `spring.config.activate.on-profile` (Boot 2.4+) |
| `spring.profiles.include` chains | `spring.profiles.group` |
| Passwords in `application-prod.yml` | Env vars, Kubernetes secrets, Vault |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is a Spring profile?**
   A named configuration set. When active, Spring loads `application-{profile}.yml` on top of the base file and registers beans marked `@Profile` with that name. It lets one artifact behave differently per environment.
2. **How do you activate a profile?**
   `spring.profiles.active` via command line (`--spring.profiles.active=prod`), the `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` environment variable, a system property, or `@ActiveProfiles` in tests. If none is set, the `default` profile is active.
3. **What happens when the same property is in `application.yml` and `application-prod.yml`?**
   With `prod` active, the profile-specific value wins. With several active profiles, the last one listed wins on conflicts.

**Follow-up**
4. **What is the property source precedence in Spring Boot?**
   From highest: command-line arguments, `SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON`, system properties, OS environment variables, profile-specific config files (outside the jar before inside), then the base config files (outside before inside), `@PropertySource`, then defaults. So an env var overrides any value in a packaged YAML file.
5. **How can an environment variable set `orderhub.payment.provider`?**
   Relaxed binding: `ORDERHUB_PAYMENT_PROVIDER=stripe`. Dots become underscores, dashes are removed, everything upper case. List indexes use `_0_`, as in `ORDERHUB_ADMINS_0_`.
6. **What expressions does `@Profile` support?**
   Negation (`!prod`), and, or, and parentheses, written with the logical words or symbols: `@Profile("dev & !cloud")`. A list `@Profile({"dev", "test"})` means any of them.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **A teammate puts `spring.profiles.active: prod` inside `application-dev.yml`. What happens?**
   Since Boot 2.4, startup fails: `spring.profiles.active` is not allowed in profile-specific documents, because it would make activation order ambiguous. Use profile groups instead.
8. **The prod database password is in `application-prod.yml` in Git. How would you fix it?**
   Remove it and rotate the password. Reference it as `${DB_PASSWORD}` and inject the value from the platform: Kubernetes secrets as env vars, AWS Secrets Manager or Vault via Spring Cloud integrations, or `spring.config.import=configtree:/run/secrets/`.
9. **Is it a good idea to have profiles per customer or per feature?**
   Usually not. Profiles should describe environments or broad infrastructure choices. For features use properties with `@ConditionalOnProperty` or a feature-flag tool; for tenants use data, not profiles. Profile explosion makes it unclear which beans exist.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you can configure one artifact for many environments safely and know the precedence rules that cause "why is my value ignored?" bugs.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Secrets in profile YAML files | Leaked credentials in Git | Env vars, secret managers, `configtree` |
| Forgetting to set a profile in production | `default` profile runs with dev settings | Set `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` in deployment manifests; fail if missing |
| Two beans with overlapping profiles | `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` in one environment | Use complementary expressions (`prod` and `!prod`) |
| Profile checks in business code | Logic tied to environment names | `@Profile` beans or properties |
| Many tiny profiles | Unclear which beans exist | Few environment profiles plus groups |

### Best practices
- Base `application.yml` with safe defaults, one file per environment, secrets from the platform.
- Use `@ActiveProfiles("test")` in tests and a `test` profile with Testcontainers or fakes.
- Print active profiles at startup (Boot logs "The following profiles are active").
- Prefer feature properties (`orderhub.notifications.enabled`) over profiles for toggling single features.

### Compare with
| Mechanism | Granularity | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| Profiles | Whole environment | dev, test, staging, prod |
| `@ConditionalOnProperty` | One feature | Feature toggles |
| Spring Cloud Config or Kubernetes ConfigMaps | External, central | Many services, runtime refresh |
| Environment variables | Single values | Secrets and per-deployment overrides |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Configure OrderHub for three environments:
1. `application.yml`: `orderhub.payment.provider: fake`, `orderhub.order.max-items: 20`.
2. `application-dev.yml`: debug logging for `com.orderhub`, H2 console enabled placeholder setting `orderhub.dev-tools: true`.
3. `application-prod.yml`: `provider: stripe`, `max-items: 50`, Stripe key read from `${STRIPE_API_KEY}`.
4. Beans: `FakePaymentGateway` active for `!prod`, `StripePaymentGateway` for `prod`.
5. A `@SpringBootTest` with `@ActiveProfiles("prod")` and the property `STRIPE_API_KEY=test-key` that asserts the Stripe gateway is used.

Acceptance criteria: no secret value in any file; the app starts without a profile using the fake gateway; the prod test passes.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml
orderhub:
  payment:
    provider: fake
  order:
    max-items: 20
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application-dev.yml
orderhub:
  dev-tools: true
logging:
  level:
    com.orderhub: DEBUG
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application-prod.yml
orderhub:
  payment:
    provider: stripe
    stripe-api-key: ${STRIPE_API_KEY}
  order:
    max-items: 50
```

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/payment/PaymentGateways.java
package com.orderhub.payment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Profile;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@Profile("!prod")
class FakePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount) { return "fake-" + customer; }
}

@Component
@Profile("prod")
class StripePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    private final String apiKey;
    StripePaymentGateway(@Value("${orderhub.payment.stripe-api-key}") String apiKey) { this.apiKey = apiKey; }
    public String charge(String customer, BigDecimal amount) {
        return "stripe(" + apiKey.substring(0, 4) + "****)-" + customer;
    }
}

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/payment/ProdProfileTest.java
package com.orderhub.payment;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.ActiveProfiles;

@SpringBootTest(properties = "STRIPE_API_KEY=test-key")
@ActiveProfiles("prod")
class ProdProfileTest {
    @Autowired PaymentGateway gateway;

    @Test
    void prodUsesStripe() {
        assertThat(gateway).isInstanceOf(StripePaymentGateway.class);
    }
}
```
If `STRIPE_API_KEY` is missing in prod, startup fails with "Could not resolve placeholder", which is the fail-fast behaviour you want. The `****` masks the rest of the key so it never appears in logs.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What does a Spring profile control?
A: Which profile-specific property files are loaded and which `@Profile`-annotated beans are registered.

Q: How do you activate the prod profile through an environment variable?
A: Set `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod`.

Q: Which wins in Spring Boot: an environment variable or a value in `application.yml`?
A: The environment variable, because OS environment variables have higher precedence than packaged config files.

Q: What replaced `spring.profiles` inside multi-document YAML in Boot 2.4?
A: `spring.config.activate.on-profile`.

Q: What does `spring.profiles.group.prod=prod-db,json-logs` do?
A: Activating `prod` also activates `prod-db` and `json-logs`.

**Related:** [[@Value vs @ConfigurationProperties]] · [[Conditional Beans]] · [[@Configuration and @Bean]]

# @Value vs @ConfigurationProperties

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: MUST | Since: @Value Spring 3.0; @ConfigurationProperties Boot 1.0; constructor binding for records Boot 2.2 (implicit in Boot 3)**

> **30-second interview answer:** `@Value("${key:default}")` injects one property (or a SpEL expression) into a field or parameter. It is fine for one or two values. `@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "orderhub")` binds a whole tree of properties into a typed object, ideally an immutable record, with relaxed binding, type conversion for durations and sizes, nested objects, lists and maps, and `@Validated` JSR-380 validation at startup. For anything more than a couple of values, I use `@ConfigurationProperties`.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
Reading a settings form.

| Settings form | Spring |
|---|---|
| Copying one field from the form onto a sticky note | `@Value("${orderhub.currency}")` |
| Photocopying a whole section into a neat typed folder | `@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "orderhub")` |
| The folder has labelled tabs (payment, shipping) | Nested records |
| A clerk checks "max items must be at least 1" before filing | `@Validated` with `@Min` |
| The form accepts "30s" or "5MB" and converts them | `Duration` and `DataSize` conversion |

### What it is
- **`@Value`:** resolves `${...}` placeholders from the `Environment` and `#{...}` SpEL expressions. Supports defaults: `${orderhub.currency:EUR}`.
- **`@ConfigurationProperties`:** binds all properties under a prefix to a Java object using `Binder`. Works with JavaBeans (setters) or constructor binding (records, immutable classes).
- **Registration:** `@EnableConfigurationProperties(OrderHubProperties.class)`, `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` on the main class, or `@Component` on a mutable class.
- **Relaxed binding:** `free-shipping-threshold`, `freeShippingThreshold`, `FREE_SHIPPING_THRESHOLD` all bind to the field `freeShippingThreshold`.
- **Conversion:** `Duration` (`30s`, `5m`), `DataSize` (`10MB`), `Period`, enums, lists from comma-separated strings, maps.
- **Metadata:** adding `spring-boot-configuration-processor` generates `META-INF/spring-configuration-metadata.json`, giving IDE autocompletion and docs for your properties.

### Why it exists
Scattered `@Value` strings are typo-prone (a misspelled key only fails at runtime or silently uses the default), not type-safe, cannot be validated as a group, and make it hard to see all settings in one place. `@ConfigurationProperties` gives one typed, documented, validated, testable configuration object.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Environment property sources"] --> B["@Value: AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor resolves placeholder per injection point"]
    A --> C["@ConfigurationProperties: ConfigurationPropertiesBindingPostProcessor"]
    C --> D["Binder walks prefix orderhub.*"]
    D --> E["Relaxed name matching and ConversionService"]
    E --> F["Construct record (constructor binding) or call setters"]
    F --> G["@Validated: Bean Validation, fail startup on violation"]
```

- `@Value` placeholders are resolved by the `PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer` mechanism; an unresolvable placeholder without a default fails startup with `IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder`.
- `@Value` does **not** support relaxed binding fully (only the canonical kebab-case form is recommended) and has no validation.
- In Boot 3, a class with a single parameterised constructor (such as a record) uses constructor binding automatically; `@ConstructorBinding` is only needed to choose among several constructors.
- `@DefaultValue` sets defaults for constructor-bound parameters; otherwise missing values are null (or empty for collections only if you annotate them).
- Configuration properties objects are ordinary beans, so you can inject them anywhere.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml
orderhub:
  currency: EUR
  free-shipping-threshold: 50.00
  order:
    max-items: 20
    reservation-timeout: 15m
  payment:
    provider: stripe
    retry-attempts: 3
  admin-emails:
    - ops@orderhub.example
    - cto@orderhub.example
  upload:
    max-size: 5MB
```

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/config/OrderHubProperties.java
package com.orderhub.config;

import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.bind.DefaultValue;
import org.springframework.util.unit.DataSize;
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;

@Validated
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "orderhub")
public record OrderHubProperties(
        @NotBlank String currency,
        @NotNull @DecimalMin("0.00") BigDecimal freeShippingThreshold,
        @Valid Order order,
        @Valid Payment payment,
        @DefaultValue({}) List<@Email String> adminEmails,
        Upload upload) {

    public record Order(@Min(1) @Max(100) int maxItems,
                        @DefaultValue("10m") Duration reservationTimeout) { }

    public record Payment(@NotBlank String provider,
                          @DefaultValue("2") @Min(0) int retryAttempts) { }

    public record Upload(@DefaultValue("2MB") DataSize maxSize) { }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/OrderHubApplication.java
package com.orderhub;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationPropertiesScan;

@SpringBootApplication
@ConfigurationPropertiesScan          // finds @ConfigurationProperties classes under com.orderhub
public class OrderHubApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) { SpringApplication.run(OrderHubApplication.class, args); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/ShippingService.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import com.orderhub.config.OrderHubProperties;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ShippingService {
    private final OrderHubProperties props;
    private final String appName;
    private final int cpuCount;

    public ShippingService(OrderHubProperties props,
                           @Value("${spring.application.name:orderhub}") String appName,   // placeholder with default
                           @Value("#{T(java.lang.Runtime).getRuntime().availableProcessors()}") int cpuCount) { // SpEL
        this.props = props;
        this.appName = appName;
        this.cpuCount = cpuCount;
    }

    public BigDecimal shippingFee(BigDecimal orderTotal) {
        return orderTotal.compareTo(props.freeShippingThreshold()) >= 0
                ? BigDecimal.ZERO : new BigDecimal("4.99");
    }

    public String describe() {
        return appName + " ships in " + props.currency()
                + ", max items " + props.order().maxItems()
                + ", reservation " + props.order().reservationTimeout().toMinutes() + " min"
                + ", upload limit " + props.upload().maxSize().toMegabytes() + " MB"
                + ", admins " + props.adminEmails().size()
                + ", cpus>0 " + (cpuCount > 0);
    }
}
```

```xml
<!-- File: pom.xml (dependencies) -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-configuration-processor</artifactId>
    <optional>true</optional>
</dependency>
```

### Walkthrough
- The YAML uses kebab-case keys; relaxed binding maps `free-shipping-threshold` to `freeShippingThreshold` and `max-items` to `maxItems`.
- `OrderHubProperties` is an immutable record bound through its canonical constructor. Nested records map nested YAML blocks. `15m` becomes a `Duration`, `5MB` a `DataSize`, the YAML list a `List<String>`.
- `@Validated` plus constraints validate at startup. `@Valid` on nested records cascades validation into them.
- `@DefaultValue` provides defaults when a key is missing (for example `retry-attempts`).
- `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` registers the record as a bean; `ShippingService` injects it like any other bean.
- `@Value` is used for one framework property with a default and one SpEL expression, which is where `@Value` still shines.
- The configuration processor generates metadata so the IDE autocompletes `orderhub.*` keys.

### Output
`shippingService.describe()` and fees:
```text
orderhub ships in EUR, max items 20, reservation 15 min, upload limit 5 MB, admins 2, cpus>0 true
fee for 30.00 = 4.99
fee for 80.00 = 0
```
Starting with `--orderhub.order.max-items=0`:
```text
***************************
APPLICATION FAILED TO START
***************************

Description:

Binding to target com.orderhub.config.OrderHubProperties failed:

    Property: orderhub.order.max-items
    Value: "0"
    Origin: "orderhub.order.max-items" from property source "commandLineArgs"
    Reason: must be greater than or equal to 1
```

### Compare
| Feature | `@Value` | `@ConfigurationProperties` |
|---|---|---|
| Granularity | One value | A whole prefix tree |
| Type safety | String key, converted on injection | Typed object, IDE metadata |
| Relaxed binding | Limited | Full |
| Validation | No | `@Validated` with JSR-380 |
| SpEL | Yes | No |
| Immutability | Constructor parameter only | Records with constructor binding |
| Testability | Needs context or reflection | Construct the record directly in unit tests |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is the difference between `@Value` and `@ConfigurationProperties`?**
   `@Value` injects a single property or SpEL expression at one injection point. `@ConfigurationProperties` binds a group of properties under a prefix into a typed object with relaxed binding, nested structures, conversion and validation. Use the latter for structured configuration.
2. **How do you provide a default value with `@Value`?**
   After a colon: `@Value("${orderhub.currency:EUR}")`. Without a default, a missing property fails startup.
3. **How do you register a `@ConfigurationProperties` class?**
   With `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan`, `@EnableConfigurationProperties(MyProps.class)`, or by making a mutable class a `@Component`. Records need one of the first two.

**Follow-up**
4. **What is relaxed binding?**
   Boot matches property names regardless of format: `max-items`, `maxItems`, `max_items` and the env var `ORDERHUB_ORDER_MAXITEMS` all bind to `maxItems`. The canonical form in files is kebab-case.
5. **How do you validate configuration properties?**
   Add `spring-boot-starter-validation`, annotate the class with `@Validated`, and put constraints such as `@NotBlank`, `@Min`, `@Email` on fields or record components. Use `@Valid` on nested objects. Violations fail startup with a clear report.
6. **What is the difference between `${...}` and `#{...}` in `@Value`?**
   `${...}` is a property placeholder resolved from the Environment. `#{...}` is a SpEL expression evaluated at injection time, which can call methods, reference beans (`#{orderService.limit}`) or compute values. They can be combined: `#{'${orderhub.admins}'.split(',')}`.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **`@Value("${orderhub.max-items}")` on a field in a class you create with `new` is always 0. Why?**
   Only Spring beans are processed; `new` bypasses the container, so nothing injects the value. The same happens for a static field: `@Value` is not applied to static fields.
8. **You changed a property in Kubernetes ConfigMap. Does the running app see it?**
   No. Properties are bound at startup. To pick up changes you restart (the usual approach), or use Spring Cloud's `@RefreshScope` and the refresh endpoint, which re-creates refresh-scoped beans and rebinds `@ConfigurationProperties`.
9. **Why prefer records for configuration properties?**
   They are immutable, so nothing can change configuration at runtime by accident, they are thread-safe, concise and easy to construct in unit tests without Spring.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you write type-safe, validated configuration and know the limits of `@Value` (no validation, string keys, beans only).

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Typo in an `@Value` key with a default | Default silently used, wrong behaviour | `@ConfigurationProperties` with validation and metadata |
| `@Value` on a static field | Stays null or 0 | Instance field or constructor parameter |
| Forgetting `spring-boot-starter-validation` | `@Validated` constraints silently ignored | Add the starter |
| No `@Valid` on nested objects | Nested constraints not checked | Annotate nested fields with `@Valid` |
| Mutable properties class shared everywhere | Code changes config at runtime | Records with constructor binding |

### Best practices
- One properties record per feature or prefix (`orderhub.payment`, `orderhub.shipping`), validated, immutable.
- Add the configuration processor for IDE help and documentation.
- Use `Duration` and `DataSize` types instead of `long timeoutMillis`.
- Keep `@Value` for one-off values and SpEL; never for groups of settings.
- Fail fast: no defaults for values that must be set per environment (like API keys).

### Compare with
| Way to read config | When |
|---|---|
| `@Value` | One value, SpEL needed |
| `@ConfigurationProperties` | Groups of related settings (default choice) |
| `Environment.getProperty` | Dynamic keys at runtime, framework code |
| `@PropertySource` | Loading an extra `.properties` file (YAML not supported by default) |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Replace scattered `@Value` usages in OrderHub with one typed config:
1. Create `OrderProperties` record bound to `orderhub.order` with `maxItems` (1 to 100), `minTotal` (`BigDecimal`, at least 0), `reservationTimeout` (`Duration`, default 10 minutes) and `allowedCountries` (list, not empty).
2. Use it in `OrderService.place(...)` to reject orders with too many items, a total below the minimum or a country not in the list (throw `IllegalArgumentException` for now; Phase 11 maps it to 400).
3. Write a plain unit test constructing `OrderProperties` directly.

Acceptance criteria: invalid config (`max-items: 0`) fails startup; the unit test needs no Spring context.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderProperties.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.bind.DefaultValue;
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;

@Validated
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "orderhub.order")
public record OrderProperties(
        @Min(1) @Max(100) int maxItems,
        @NotNull @DecimalMin("0") BigDecimal minTotal,
        @DefaultValue("10m") Duration reservationTimeout,
        @NotEmpty List<String> allowedCountries) { }

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java (excerpt)
package com.orderhub.order;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderProperties props;

    public OrderService(OrderProperties props) { this.props = props; }

    public void validate(int itemCount, BigDecimal total, String country) {
        if (itemCount > props.maxItems())
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Too many items: " + itemCount + " (max " + props.maxItems() + ")");
        if (total.compareTo(props.minTotal()) < 0)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order total below minimum " + props.minTotal());
        if (!props.allowedCountries().contains(country))
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("We do not ship to " + country);
    }
}

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderServiceLimitsTest.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class OrderServiceLimitsTest {
    private final OrderService service = new OrderService(
            new OrderProperties(3, new BigDecimal("10"), Duration.ofMinutes(10), List.of("DE", "NL")));

    @Test
    void rejectsTooManyItems() {
        var ex = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
                () -> service.validate(4, new BigDecimal("50"), "DE"));
        assertEquals("Too many items: 4 (max 3)", ex.getMessage());
    }

    @Test
    void acceptsValidOrder() {
        assertDoesNotThrow(() -> service.validate(2, new BigDecimal("50"), "NL"));
    }
}
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml
orderhub:
  order:
    max-items: 20
    min-total: 5.00
    allowed-countries: DE,NL,BE
```
The comma-separated string binds to a `List<String>` automatically.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: When should you use `@ConfigurationProperties` instead of `@Value`?
A: For groups of related settings: it gives typed, validated, relaxed-bound, testable configuration objects.

Q: How do you give `@Value` a default in Spring?
A: Add it after a colon in the placeholder, like `${orderhub.currency:EUR}`.

Q: How do you validate `@ConfigurationProperties` at startup?
A: Add the validation starter, annotate the class with `@Validated`, add constraints, and use `@Valid` for nested objects.

Q: What is the difference between `${}` and `#{}` in Spring's `@Value`?
A: `${}` resolves a property placeholder; `#{}` evaluates a SpEL expression.

Q: Do `@ConfigurationProperties` records need `@ConstructorBinding` in Boot 3?
A: No; a single-constructor class such as a record uses constructor binding automatically.

**Related:** [[Profiles and Environment-Specific Config]] · [[Bean Validation]] · [[Conditional Beans]]

# Conditional Beans

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: SHOULD | Since: @Conditional Spring 4.0; @ConditionalOn... annotations Boot 1.0; @AutoConfiguration Boot 2.7**

> **30-second interview answer:** `@Conditional` lets Spring decide at startup whether to register a bean, based on a `Condition` that inspects the environment, classpath or existing beans. Spring Boot builds auto-configuration on it: `@ConditionalOnClass` (a library is on the classpath), `@ConditionalOnProperty` (a feature flag), and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` (only if the user has not defined their own). That last one is why defining your own `ObjectMapper` or `DataSource` makes Boot's default back off.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A hotel room prepared according to the booking.

| Hotel | Spring |
|---|---|
| Put a baby cot in the room only if the booking says "infant" | `@ConditionalOnProperty` |
| Provide a hair dryer only if the room has a socket for it | `@ConditionalOnClass` |
| Put a standard pillow only if the guest did not bring their own | `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` |
| Housekeeping checklist that runs these checks for every room | Auto-configuration classes |
| The checklist report you can ask for at the front desk | The conditions evaluation report (`--debug`) |

### What it is
- **`Condition`:** interface with `matches(ConditionContext, AnnotatedTypeMetadata)`. The context gives access to the `Environment`, bean factory, class loader and resource loader.
- **`@Conditional(MyCondition.class)`:** on a `@Configuration`, `@Component` or `@Bean` method.
- **Boot conditions (most used):**
  - `@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix, name, havingValue, matchIfMissing)`
  - `@ConditionalOnClass` and `@ConditionalOnMissingClass`
  - `@ConditionalOnBean` and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`
  - `@ConditionalOnWebApplication`, `@ConditionalOnExpression`, `@ConditionalOnResource`, `@ConditionalOnCloudPlatform`, `@ConditionalOnThreading` (virtual threads, Boot 3.2)
- **Auto-configuration:** classes annotated `@AutoConfiguration`, listed in `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`, processed after your own configuration.

### Why it exists
Libraries need sensible defaults that adapt to what you have: if Jackson is on the classpath, configure an `ObjectMapper`; if you set `spring.datasource.url`, create a `DataSource`; if you define your own, step aside. Applications also need feature toggles without code changes. `@Profile` is itself implemented as a condition.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["ConfigurationClassPostProcessor parses config classes"] --> B["User config and scanned components first"]
    B --> C["Then auto-configurations via DeferredImportSelector"]
    C --> D{"Class-level conditions match? (OnClass checked from bytecode, no class loading)"}
    D -- "no" --> E["Skip whole configuration class"]
    D -- "yes" --> F{"For each @Bean: conditions match?"}
    F -- "OnMissingBean finds user bean" --> G["Skip: user bean wins (back off)"]
    F -- "match" --> H["Register bean definition"]
```

Key points:
- Conditions are evaluated during the **bean definition** phase, not when beans are created. `@ConditionalOnBean` therefore only sees definitions registered so far, which is why it is reliable in auto-configuration (processed last) but fragile in your own configuration.
- `@ConditionalOnClass(name = "...")` is checked by reading annotation metadata with ASM, so a missing class does not cause `ClassNotFoundException`.
- Ordering among auto-configurations: `@AutoConfiguration(after = ..., before = ...)`.
- See every decision with `--debug` or `debug=true` (the "CONDITIONS EVALUATION REPORT"), or the Actuator `conditions` endpoint.

```text
CONDITIONS EVALUATION REPORT (excerpt)
Positive matches:
   JacksonAutoConfiguration matched:
      - @ConditionalOnClass found required class 'com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper'
Negative matches:
   MongoAutoConfiguration:
      Did not match:
         - @ConditionalOnClass did not find required class 'com.mongodb.client.MongoClient'
```

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/NotificationAutoConfig.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationRunner;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnClass;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnMissingBean;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Condition;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.ConditionContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Conditional;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.core.type.AnnotatedTypeMetadata;

interface Notifier { String name(); void notify(String msg); }

class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public String name() { return "sms"; }
    public void notify(String msg) { System.out.println("SMS: " + msg); }
}

class SlackNotifier implements Notifier {
    public String name() { return "slack"; }
    public void notify(String msg) { System.out.println("Slack: " + msg); }
}

class LogNotifier implements Notifier {
    public String name() { return "log"; }
    public void notify(String msg) { System.out.println("LOG: " + msg); }
}

// Custom condition: only on weekdays-style business rule, here "running in a container"
class RunningInContainerCondition implements Condition {
    @Override
    public boolean matches(ConditionContext ctx, AnnotatedTypeMetadata metadata) {
        return ctx.getEnvironment().containsProperty("KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST");
    }
}

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class NotificationAutoConfig {

    // Feature flag: create only if orderhub.notifications.sms.enabled=true
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = "orderhub.notifications.sms", name = "enabled", havingValue = "true")
    Notifier smsNotifier() { return new SmsNotifier(); }

    // Only if a Slack client library is on the classpath (string name: safe if absent)
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnClass(name = "com.slack.api.Slack")
    Notifier slackNotifier() { return new SlackNotifier(); }

    // Fallback: only if no other Notifier bean has been defined
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean(Notifier.class)
    Notifier logNotifier() { return new LogNotifier(); }

    // Custom condition
    @Bean
    @Conditional(RunningInContainerCondition.class)
    ApplicationRunner containerBanner() {
        return args -> System.out.println("running inside Kubernetes");
    }

    @Bean
    ApplicationRunner notifierReport(java.util.List<Notifier> notifiers) {
        return args -> System.out.println("notifiers: " + notifiers.stream().map(Notifier::name).toList());
    }
}
```

```yaml
# File: src/main/resources/application.yml
orderhub:
  notifications:
    sms:
      enabled: false
```

### Walkthrough
- `smsNotifier` exists only when `orderhub.notifications.sms.enabled=true`. With `matchIfMissing = true` it would also be created when the property is absent.
- `slackNotifier` checks the classpath by class name; without the Slack SDK the bean is skipped without errors.
- `logNotifier` is a fallback with `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`. Because these beans are in the same configuration class, evaluation order follows method order, so the fallback is declared last. In real auto-configuration, the fallback lives in an `@AutoConfiguration` class processed after user configuration, which makes it reliable.
- `RunningInContainerCondition` reads the environment: Kubernetes injects `KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST` into every pod.
- `notifierReport` injects all `Notifier` beans to show which ones exist.

### Output
Default (`sms.enabled=false`, no Slack SDK, not in Kubernetes):
```text
notifiers: [log]
```
With `--orderhub.notifications.sms.enabled=true`:
```text
notifiers: [sms]
```
With the SMS flag on and the Slack SDK dependency added, in a Kubernetes pod:
```text
running inside Kubernetes
notifiers: [sms, slack]
```

### Compare
| Mechanism | Decides by | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `@Profile` | Active profiles | Environment-wide differences |
| `@ConditionalOnProperty` | A property value | Feature flags |
| `@ConditionalOnClass` | Classpath content | Library integrations |
| `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` | Existing bean definitions | Overridable defaults |
| Custom `@Conditional` | Any logic | Platform detection, complex rules |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What does `@Conditional` do?**
   It registers a bean or configuration only if a `Condition` returns true at startup. The condition can check properties, the classpath, existing beans or anything in the environment.
2. **Name some Spring Boot conditional annotations.**
   `@ConditionalOnProperty`, `@ConditionalOnClass`, `@ConditionalOnMissingClass`, `@ConditionalOnBean`, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, `@ConditionalOnWebApplication`, `@ConditionalOnExpression` and `@ConditionalOnResource`.
3. **How does Spring Boot auto-configuration work?**
   Boot loads auto-configuration classes listed in `AutoConfiguration.imports` after your own configuration. Each is guarded by conditions: on the classpath, on properties and on missing beans. So it configures what you have on the classpath and backs off when you define your own beans.

**Follow-up**
4. **How do you override an auto-configured bean?**
   Define your own bean of the same type; the auto-configuration's `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` skips its default. Alternatively set properties it reads, use a customizer bean, or exclude the auto-configuration with `@SpringBootApplication(exclude = ...)` or `spring.autoconfigure.exclude`.
5. **How do you find out why a bean was or was not created?**
   Start with `--debug` to print the conditions evaluation report, or call the Actuator `/actuator/conditions` endpoint. It lists positive and negative matches with reasons.
6. **Why is `@ConditionalOnBean` risky in your own configuration classes?**
   Conditions are evaluated in the order definitions are registered. If the bean you are checking for is registered later (another config class or auto-configuration), the condition sees nothing and wrongly fails. It is designed for auto-configuration, whose ordering is controlled.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **You add `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` and the app no longer starts: "Failed to configure a DataSource". Why?**
   JPA brings `DataSource` classes onto the classpath, so `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` matches its `@ConditionalOnClass` and tries to build a `DataSource`, but no URL is configured and no embedded database is present. Configure `spring.datasource.url`, add an embedded database for tests, or exclude the auto-configuration.
8. **How does `@ConditionalOnClass` work when the class is missing? Would that not throw `ClassNotFoundException`?**
   Spring reads the annotation metadata from bytecode with ASM without loading the configuration class, so the referenced class is never resolved. That is why it is safe on the class level. On `@Bean` methods, use the `name` attribute with a string to be safe.
9. **What is the difference between `@Profile` and `@ConditionalOnProperty` for toggling a feature?**
   `@Profile` ties the feature to an environment name; `@ConditionalOnProperty` ties it to a specific setting that can be flipped per environment or per deployment. Properties are finer-grained and easier to reason about for single features.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you understand auto-configuration as conditional bean registration and can debug "why is this bean here or missing".

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `@ConditionalOnBean` in user config | Order-dependent, false negatives | Use it only in auto-configuration, or `ObjectProvider` |
| `@ConditionalOnClass(SomeClass.class)` on a `@Bean` method | `NoClassDefFoundError` if absent | Use the `name` string attribute or a nested config class |
| Expecting conditions to re-evaluate at runtime | Bean set fixed at startup | Restart or use refresh scope or a feature-flag service |
| Excluding auto-config instead of setting one property | Lose many sensible defaults | Prefer properties and customizers |

### Best practices
- Use `@ConditionalOnProperty` for feature toggles with an explicit `havingValue` and a deliberate `matchIfMissing`.
- Put overridable defaults in `@AutoConfiguration` classes with `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` (Phase 18 builds a custom starter this way).
- Run with `--debug` once when a bean is missing; read the report before guessing.
- Keep custom conditions simple and side-effect free.

### Compare with
| Toggle approach | Changes at runtime? | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| `@Profile` | No | Environment |
| `@ConditionalOnProperty` | No (startup) | Feature |
| Feature-flag service (Unleash, LaunchDarkly) | Yes | Per request or user |
| `@RefreshScope` (Spring Cloud) | Yes, bean re-created | Bean |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Make order notifications pluggable:
1. Interface `OrderNotifier` with `void orderPlaced(long orderId, String customerEmail)`.
2. `EmailOrderNotifier`, created only when `orderhub.notifications.email.enabled=true`.
3. `WebhookOrderNotifier`, created only when `orderhub.notifications.webhook.url` is set (use `@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "orderhub.notifications.webhook.url")`, which matches any value except `false`).
4. `NoopOrderNotifier` fallback, created only when no other `OrderNotifier` exists; put it in a separate configuration class annotated `@AutoConfiguration` and registered in `AutoConfiguration.imports` so it is evaluated last.
5. `OrderService` injects `List<OrderNotifier>` and calls all of them.

Acceptance criteria: with no properties only the no-op notifier exists; enabling email gives only email; enabling both gives both and no no-op.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/OrderNotifiers.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = "orderhub.notifications.email", name = "enabled", havingValue = "true")
class EmailOrderNotifier implements OrderNotifier {
    public void orderPlaced(long orderId, String email) {
        System.out.println("email to " + email + ": order " + orderId + " placed");
    }
}

@Component
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "orderhub.notifications.webhook.url")
class WebhookOrderNotifier implements OrderNotifier {
    private final String url;
    WebhookOrderNotifier(@Value("${orderhub.notifications.webhook.url}") String url) { this.url = url; }
    public void orderPlaced(long orderId, String email) {
        System.out.println("POST " + url + " {orderId:" + orderId + "}");
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/OrderNotifier.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

public interface OrderNotifier {
    void orderPlaced(long orderId, String customerEmail);
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/NotifierFallbackAutoConfiguration.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnMissingBean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;

@AutoConfiguration      // processed after user configuration
public class NotifierFallbackAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean(OrderNotifier.class)
    OrderNotifier noopOrderNotifier() {
        return (orderId, email) -> System.out.println("no notifier configured for order " + orderId);
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java (excerpt)
package com.orderhub.order;

import com.orderhub.notification.OrderNotifier;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final List<OrderNotifier> notifiers;
    public OrderService(List<OrderNotifier> notifiers) { this.notifiers = notifiers; }

    public void place(long orderId, String email) {
        notifiers.forEach(n -> n.orderPlaced(orderId, email));
    }
}
```

```text
# File: src/main/resources/META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports
com.orderhub.notification.NotifierFallbackAutoConfiguration
```
Make sure component scanning does not also pick up the auto-configuration class: `@AutoConfiguration` classes are excluded from scanning by `@SpringBootApplication`'s `AutoConfigurationExcludeFilter` as long as they are listed in the imports file.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: When are Spring `@Conditional` conditions evaluated?
A: At startup during bean definition registration, not at bean creation or runtime.

Q: Which Spring Boot condition makes auto-configuration back off when you define your own bean?
A: `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`.

Q: How do you see why Spring Boot auto-configured a bean or skipped it?
A: Run with `--debug` for the conditions evaluation report, or use the Actuator `conditions` endpoint.

Q: What does `@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "x.enabled", havingValue = "true", matchIfMissing = true)` mean?
A: Create the bean if `x.enabled` is true or not set at all.

Q: Why is `@ConditionalOnBean` unreliable in your own configuration classes?
A: It only sees bean definitions registered so far, so ordering can make it fail; it is meant for auto-configuration.

**Related:** [[Profiles and Environment-Specific Config]] · [[@Configuration and @Bean]] · [[@Qualifier, @Primary and Multiple Beans]]

# Spring Application Events

**Level: Intermediate | Phase 10 | Priority: SHOULD | Since: ApplicationEvent Spring 1.0; @EventListener and any-object events Spring 4.2; @TransactionalEventListener Spring 4.2**

> **30-second interview answer:** Spring has an in-process publish-subscribe bus. A bean publishes an event object with `ApplicationEventPublisher.publishEvent()`, and every `@EventListener` method whose parameter type matches is invoked. By default listeners run synchronously in the publisher's thread and transaction, so an exception propagates back. `@Async` makes a listener asynchronous, and `@TransactionalEventListener` runs it only after the surrounding transaction commits, which is how I send emails only for orders that were really saved.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A school public-address system.

| School PA system | Spring events |
|---|---|
| The office announces "Fire drill at 10:00" | `publisher.publishEvent(new FireDrillEvent())` |
| Teachers who care about drills react; others ignore it | `@EventListener` methods for that event type |
| The office does not phone each teacher | Publisher does not know its listeners (decoupling) |
| By default the office waits until every class confirms | Synchronous listeners in the same thread |
| "Announce the results only after the exam is officially graded" | `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` |
| Announcements are not recorded; a teacher out sick misses it | In-memory only, lost on crash (not a message broker) |

### What it is
- **Event:** any object (since Spring 4.2); historically a subclass of `ApplicationEvent`. Records make great events.
- **`ApplicationEventPublisher`:** inject it (the `ApplicationContext` implements it) and call `publishEvent(event)`.
- **`@EventListener`:** on a bean method; parameter type selects events. Supports `condition` (SpEL), ordering with `@Order`, and returning a new event to publish it.
- **`@TransactionalEventListener`:** bound to the current transaction; phases `BEFORE_COMMIT`, `AFTER_COMMIT` (default), `AFTER_ROLLBACK`, `AFTER_COMPLETION`.
- **`@Async` listener:** runs on a task executor, needs `@EnableAsync`.
- **Built-in events:** `ContextRefreshedEvent`, `ContextClosedEvent`, and Boot's `ApplicationStartingEvent`, `ApplicationEnvironmentPreparedEvent`, `ApplicationPreparedEvent`, `ApplicationStartedEvent`, `ApplicationReadyEvent`, `ApplicationFailedEvent`.

### Why it exists
When an order is placed, many things should happen: send a confirmation, update analytics, reserve stock, award loyalty points. If `OrderService` calls each service directly, it depends on all of them and grows with every new feature. With events, `OrderService` announces "order placed" and new reactions are added as listeners without touching it. This is the in-process version of the event-driven design you will use with Kafka in Phase 20.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant OS as "OrderService (in @Transactional)"
    participant M as "ApplicationEventMulticaster"
    participant L1 as "LoyaltyListener (@EventListener)"
    participant L2 as "EmailListener (@TransactionalEventListener)"
    OS->>M: publishEvent(OrderPlacedEvent)
    M->>L1: invoke now, same thread, same transaction
    M->>L2: register synchronization with the transaction
    OS-->>OS: method returns, transaction commits
    Note over L2: after commit, EmailListener runs
```

- `EventListenerMethodProcessor` (a `SmartInitializingSingleton`) scans beans for `@EventListener` methods at startup and registers an `ApplicationListenerMethodAdapter` for each.
- `SimpleApplicationEventMulticaster` invokes matching listeners one by one in the calling thread (unless an executor is configured on it, which affects all listeners).
- An exception in a synchronous listener propagates to the publisher: later listeners do not run and a surrounding transaction rolls back.
- `@TransactionalEventListener` registers a `TransactionSynchronization`. If there is no active transaction, the event is **dropped** unless `fallbackExecution = true`.
- In an `AFTER_COMMIT` listener the transaction is already committed; any database writes there need a new transaction (`@Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)`).
- Events are in memory only: if the JVM crashes after commit but before the listener runs, the reaction is lost. For guaranteed delivery use the transactional outbox pattern (Phase 20).

## 3. Code Example
### Code
```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderPlacedEvent.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record OrderPlacedEvent(long orderId, String customerEmail, BigDecimal total) { }

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationEventPublisher;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;
    private final AtomicLong ids = new AtomicLong(1000);

    public OrderService(ApplicationEventPublisher events) { this.events = events; }

    @Transactional
    public long place(String email, BigDecimal total) {
        long id = ids.incrementAndGet();
        System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] order " + id + " saved");
        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(id, email, total));
        System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] place() returning");
        return id;
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderListeners.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionPhase;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionalEventListener;
import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;

@Component
class OrderListeners {

    @EventListener
    @Order(1)
    void awardLoyaltyPoints(OrderPlacedEvent e) {      // sync, inside the transaction
        System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] loyalty points for order " + e.orderId());
    }

    @EventListener(condition = "#e.total.compareTo(new java.math.BigDecimal('100')) > 0")
    @Order(2)
    void flagBigOrder(OrderPlacedEvent e) {            // conditional listener
        System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] big order " + e.orderId() + " flagged for review");
    }

    @Async
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    void sendConfirmation(OrderPlacedEvent e) {        // only after commit, on another thread
        System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] email sent to " + e.customerEmail());
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/config/AsyncConfig.java
package com.orderhub.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableAsync;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig { }
```

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/StartupListener.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.springframework.boot.context.event.ApplicationReadyEvent;
import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
class StartupListener {
    @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)
    void ready() { System.out.println("OrderHub ready to take orders"); }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `OrderPlacedEvent` is a plain record; no need to extend `ApplicationEvent`.
- `OrderService` depends only on `ApplicationEventPublisher`, not on loyalty, review or email services.
- `awardLoyaltyPoints` runs synchronously in the request thread, inside the transaction, before `place()` returns. If it threw, the order transaction would roll back.
- `flagBigOrder` uses a SpEL `condition`, so it only runs for totals above 100. `@Order` controls the order of synchronous listeners.
- `sendConfirmation` is transactional and async: Spring holds the event until commit, then hands it to the async executor (`task-1` is Boot's default executor thread name). If the transaction rolled back, no email would be sent.
- `StartupListener` reacts to a built-in Boot event.
- This needs a transaction manager. Until Phase 12 adds JPA, add `spring-boot-starter-jdbc` with H2 so `@Transactional` has a `DataSourceTransactionManager`.

### Output
`POST /api/orders` that calls `place("ana@example.com", 150.00)`:
```text
[http-nio-8080-exec-1] order 1001 saved
[http-nio-8080-exec-1] loyalty points for order 1001
[http-nio-8080-exec-1] big order 1001 flagged for review
[http-nio-8080-exec-1] place() returning
[task-1] email sent to ana@example.com
```
With total 40.00, the "big order" line is absent.

### Compare
| Listener type | Thread | Transaction | Exception effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| `@EventListener` | Publisher's thread | Same as publisher | Propagates, may roll back |
| `@EventListener` + `@Async` | Executor thread | None | Logged, publisher unaffected |
| `@TransactionalEventListener` (AFTER_COMMIT) | Publisher's thread after commit | Committed already | Logged, does not undo the commit |
| `@TransactionalEventListener` + `@Async` | Executor thread after commit | None | Logged, publisher unaffected |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **How do you publish and listen to events in Spring?**
   Inject `ApplicationEventPublisher` and call `publishEvent(object)`. Annotate a method in any bean with `@EventListener`; its parameter type decides which events it receives.
2. **Are Spring events synchronous or asynchronous?**
   Synchronous by default: listeners run in the publisher's thread, one after another, before `publishEvent` returns. Add `@Async` (with `@EnableAsync`) for asynchronous listeners.
3. **Name some built-in Spring Boot events.**
   `ApplicationStartingEvent`, `ApplicationEnvironmentPreparedEvent`, `ApplicationPreparedEvent`, `ContextRefreshedEvent`, `ApplicationStartedEvent`, `ApplicationReadyEvent`, `ApplicationFailedEvent` and `ContextClosedEvent`.

**Follow-up**
4. **What is `@TransactionalEventListener` and why use it?**
   A listener bound to the publisher's transaction. With the default `AFTER_COMMIT` phase, it runs only if the transaction commits. It prevents side effects such as emails or messages for data that was rolled back.
5. **What happens if a synchronous listener throws an exception?**
   The exception propagates to the publisher, remaining listeners are skipped, and if the publisher is in a transaction that is rolled back. Async and after-commit listeners do not affect the publisher.
6. **What happens to a `@TransactionalEventListener` event published outside a transaction?**
   It is silently discarded, unless the listener sets `fallbackExecution = true`. This is a common "my listener never fires" bug.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **An `AFTER_COMMIT` listener saves an audit row with a repository, but the row never appears. Why?**
   The original transaction has already committed; the listener runs in its completion phase where the resources are still bound but no new commit will happen. Annotate the listener's work with `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)` or make the listener async so it starts its own transaction.
8. **Can Spring events replace Kafka?**
   Only inside one JVM and without durability. Events are lost on crash, are not shared between instances and have no replay. Use them for in-process decoupling; use a broker plus the outbox pattern for cross-service or guaranteed delivery.
9. **Your `@Async` event listener is not asynchronous. What do you check?**
   That `@EnableAsync` is present, that the listener bean is a Spring proxy (not created with `new`), and that the method is not private or final. Also check the thread name in logs to confirm.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you know the default synchronous, same-transaction behaviour and the after-commit pattern that avoids inconsistent side effects.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Sending emails from a plain `@EventListener` inside a transaction | Email sent even if the transaction rolls back | `@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)` |
| Publishing a transactional event outside a transaction | Listener never runs | Publish inside `@Transactional` or set `fallbackExecution = true` |
| Slow synchronous listeners | Request latency grows with each listener | `@Async` for slow, non-critical work |
| Relying on events for critical cross-service work | Lost on crash | Transactional outbox and a broker |
| Mutable event objects | Listeners change data others see | Immutable records |

### Best practices
- Model events as immutable records named in the past tense (`OrderPlacedEvent`).
- Keep events small: ids and key facts, not whole entities with lazy relations.
- Use after-commit listeners for external side effects; make them idempotent.
- Configure a dedicated executor for async listeners and monitor it.
- Spring Modulith (`@ApplicationModuleListener`, event publication registry) adds durable in-process events if you need them.

### Compare with
| Approach | Coupling | Durability | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| Direct method call | High | n/a | In process |
| Spring application events | Low | None (in memory) | One JVM |
| Spring Modulith event registry | Low | Stored in DB, retried | One application |
| Kafka or RabbitMQ | Low | Durable | Across services |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
**OrderHub task.** Decouple order side effects:
1. Publish `OrderPlacedEvent(orderId, customerEmail, total)` from `OrderService.place` inside `@Transactional`.
2. `InventoryListener` (`@EventListener`, synchronous) reserves stock and throws `OutOfStockException` if a product is unavailable, causing the order to roll back.
3. `ConfirmationEmailListener` (`@Async` + `@TransactionalEventListener`) sends the email only after commit.
4. Write a test that publishes an order that fails stock reservation and verifies no email was sent.

Acceptance criteria: a failed reservation leaves no order and sends no email; a successful order logs the email on an async thread.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/inventory/InventoryListener.java
package com.orderhub.inventory;

import com.orderhub.order.OrderPlacedEvent;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class InventoryListener {
    @EventListener
    public void reserve(OrderPlacedEvent e) {
        // demo rule: orders above 1000 cannot be reserved
        if (e.total().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000")) > 0) {
            throw new OutOfStockException(e.orderId());
        }
        System.out.println("stock reserved for order " + e.orderId());
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/inventory/OutOfStockException.java
package com.orderhub.inventory;

public class OutOfStockException extends RuntimeException {
    public OutOfStockException(long orderId) { super("Out of stock for order " + orderId); }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/ConfirmationEmailListener.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import com.orderhub.order.OrderPlacedEvent;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionalEventListener;

@Component
public class ConfirmationEmailListener {
    public final List<Long> sent = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();   // visible for the test

    @Async
    @TransactionalEventListener   // AFTER_COMMIT by default
    public void send(OrderPlacedEvent e) {
        sent.add(e.orderId());
        System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] confirmation to " + e.customerEmail());
    }
}

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderEventsTest.java
package com.orderhub.order;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.*;
import static org.awaitility.Awaitility.await;

import com.orderhub.inventory.OutOfStockException;
import com.orderhub.notification.ConfirmationEmailListener;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;

@SpringBootTest
class OrderEventsTest {
    @Autowired OrderService orders;
    @Autowired ConfirmationEmailListener emails;

    @Test
    void failedReservationSendsNoEmail() {
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> orders.place("bob@example.com", new BigDecimal("5000")))
                .isInstanceOf(OutOfStockException.class);
        assertThat(emails.sent).isEmpty();
    }

    @Test
    void successfulOrderSendsEmailAfterCommit() {
        long id = orders.place("ana@example.com", new BigDecimal("50"));
        await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(() -> emails.sent.contains(id));
    }
}
```
The synchronous inventory listener throws inside the transaction, so the transaction rolls back and the after-commit listener is never triggered. Awaitility (bundled with `spring-boot-starter-test` in Boot 3) waits for the async thread instead of a fragile `Thread.sleep`.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: Are Spring application event listeners synchronous by default?
A: Yes; they run in the publisher's thread and transaction before `publishEvent` returns.

Q: What does `@TransactionalEventListener` do by default?
A: Runs the listener only after the publisher's transaction commits (phase `AFTER_COMMIT`).

Q: What happens to a `@TransactionalEventListener` event published without a transaction?
A: It is discarded unless `fallbackExecution = true` is set.

Q: How do you make a Spring event listener asynchronous?
A: Add `@Async` to the listener method and `@EnableAsync` to a configuration class.

Q: Why are Spring application events not a replacement for Kafka?
A: They are in-memory and single-JVM: no durability, no replay, lost on crash, invisible to other instances.

**Related:** [[Bean Lifecycle Callbacks]] · [[IoC and Dependency Injection]] · [[Conditional Beans]]

# Coding drill: Spring Core

Ten tasks of the kind interviewers ask in a Spring round: "fix this wiring", "what does this print", "write the configuration for X". Try each on paper or in OrderHub before opening the solution. All tasks use Spring Boot 3.5 and Java 21.

## 1. Fix the field injection
Refactor this class to constructor injection and make it unit-testable without Spring.

```java
// Snippet: Spring imports (org.springframework.*) and surrounding classes omitted for brevity
@Service
public class InvoiceService {
    @Autowired private OrderRepository orders;
    @Autowired private TaxCalculator tax;

    public BigDecimal total(long orderId) {
        Order o = orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
        return o.subtotal().add(tax.taxFor(o));
    }
}
```
**Hint:** final fields, one constructor, no annotation.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/invoice/InvoiceService.java
package com.orderhub.invoice;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class InvoiceService {
    private final OrderRepository orders;
    private final TaxCalculator tax;

    public InvoiceService(OrderRepository orders, TaxCalculator tax) {   // implicit @Autowired
        this.orders = orders;
        this.tax = tax;
    }

    public BigDecimal total(long orderId) {
        Order o = orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
        return o.subtotal().add(tax.taxFor(o));
    }
}
```
A test can now write `new InvoiceService(fakeRepo, fakeTax)`. Fields are `final`, so they can never be null or reassigned after construction, and the dependencies are visible in the signature.
</details>

## 2. Two implementations, one injection point
`EmailSender` has `SmtpEmailSender` and `SesEmailSender`. Startup fails with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`. Make SES the default everywhere, but let `AdminAlertService` use SMTP.

**Hint:** `@Primary` on one bean, `@Qualifier` at one injection point.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/mail/MailBeans.java
package com.orderhub.mail;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

interface EmailSender { void send(String to, String text); }

@Component("smtp")
class SmtpEmailSender implements EmailSender {
    public void send(String to, String text) { System.out.println("SMTP " + to); }
}

@Component("ses")
@Primary
class SesEmailSender implements EmailSender {
    public void send(String to, String text) { System.out.println("SES " + to); }
}

@Service
class AdminAlertService {
    private final EmailSender sender;
    AdminAlertService(@Qualifier("smtp") EmailSender sender) { this.sender = sender; }
    void alert(String msg) { sender.send("ops@orderhub.example", msg); }
}
```
Every unqualified `EmailSender` gets SES; `AdminAlertService` gets SMTP because `@Qualifier` filters candidates before `@Primary` is considered.
</details>

## 3. Strategy map from a list
Write a `DiscountService` that receives every `DiscountRule` bean (each has `String code()` and `BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal total)`) and applies the rule chosen by a coupon code. Unknown codes return the total unchanged.

**Hint:** inject `List<DiscountRule>` and collect to a map.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/discount/DiscountService.java
package com.orderhub.discount;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class DiscountService {
    private final Map<String, DiscountRule> rules;

    public DiscountService(List<DiscountRule> all) {
        // toMap throws IllegalStateException on duplicate codes: fail fast at startup
        this.rules = all.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(DiscountRule::code, Function.identity()));
    }

    public BigDecimal apply(String coupon, BigDecimal total) {
        DiscountRule rule = rules.get(coupon);
        return rule == null ? total : rule.apply(total);
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/discount/Rules.java
package com.orderhub.discount;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

interface DiscountRule {
    String code();
    BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal total);
}

@Component
class TenPercentOff implements DiscountRule {
    public String code() { return "SAVE10"; }
    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal t) { return t.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.90")).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN); }
}

@Component
class FiveOff implements DiscountRule {
    public String code() { return "FIVEOFF"; }
    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal t) { return t.subtract(new BigDecimal("5")).max(BigDecimal.ZERO); }
}
```
`apply("SAVE10", 80.00)` returns `72.00`; `apply("NOPE", 80.00)` returns `80.00`. Adding a rule is one new class, no change to the service.
</details>

## 4. Predict the output: prototype inside a singleton
```java
@Component @Scope("prototype")
class Counter { private int n; int next() { return ++n; } }

@Component
class Ticketing {
    private final Counter counter;
    Ticketing(Counter counter) { this.counter = counter; }
    int ticket() { return counter.next(); }
}

// in a runner:
System.out.println(ticketing.ticket() + " " + ticketing.ticket() + " " + ticketing.ticket());
```

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```text
1 2 3
```
The prototype is created once, when the singleton `Ticketing` is constructed, and reused forever. To get a fresh `Counter` per call (output `1 1 1`), inject `ObjectProvider<Counter>` and call `getObject()` inside `ticket()`:

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/demo/Ticketing.java
package com.orderhub.demo;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
class Ticketing {
    private final ObjectProvider<Counter> counters;
    Ticketing(ObjectProvider<Counter> counters) { this.counters = counters; }
    int ticket() { return counters.getObject().next(); }   // new prototype every call
}
```
</details>

## 5. Predict the output: @Configuration proxying
```java
@Configuration
class Cfg {
    @Bean Pool pool() { System.out.println("pool created"); return new Pool(); }
    @Bean A a() { return new A(pool()); }
    @Bean B b() { return new B(pool()); }
}
```
How many times is "pool created" printed? What if the annotation becomes `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)`?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Full mode:
```text
pool created
```
Lite mode:
```text
pool created
pool created
pool created
```
In full mode the CGLIB proxy intercepts `pool()` calls and returns the singleton. In lite mode, `a()` and `b()` call `pool()` as plain Java methods, so two extra unmanaged pools are created. The fix for lite mode is parameter injection:

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/demo/Cfg.java
package com.orderhub.demo;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class Cfg {
    @Bean Pool pool() { System.out.println("pool created"); return new Pool(); }
    @Bean A a(Pool pool) { return new A(pool); }
    @Bean B b(Pool pool) { return new B(pool); }
}
```
</details>

## 6. Typed configuration with validation
Bind this YAML to an immutable, validated object and fail startup if `timeout` is missing or `retries` is negative.

```yaml
orderhub:
  inventory-client:
    base-url: http://inventory:8081
    timeout: 2s
    retries: 3
```
**Hint:** record, `@ConfigurationProperties`, `@Validated`, `Duration`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/inventory/InventoryClientProperties.java
package com.orderhub.inventory;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.Min;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotNull;
import java.net.URI;
import java.time.Duration;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;

@Validated
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "orderhub.inventory-client")
public record InventoryClientProperties(
        @NotNull URI baseUrl,
        @NotNull Duration timeout,
        @Min(0) int retries) { }
```
Register it with `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` on the main class. `2s` is converted to `Duration.ofSeconds(2)`, `base-url` binds to `baseUrl` through relaxed binding. A missing `timeout` or `retries: -1` stops startup with a "Binding to target ... failed" report naming the property and reason.
</details>

## 7. Feature flag with a fallback
Create a `FraudCheck` bean: `ExternalFraudCheck` when `orderhub.fraud.enabled=true`, otherwise `AllowAllFraudCheck`. Exactly one bean must exist in every case, including when the property is absent.

**Hint:** two complementary `@ConditionalOnProperty` with `matchIfMissing`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/fraud/FraudConfig.java
package com.orderhub.fraud;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

interface FraudCheck { boolean allowed(long customerId); }

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class FraudConfig {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "orderhub.fraud.enabled", havingValue = "true")
    FraudCheck externalFraudCheck() {
        return customerId -> customerId % 13 != 0;      // pretend remote scoring
    }

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "orderhub.fraud.enabled", havingValue = "false", matchIfMissing = true)
    FraudCheck allowAllFraudCheck() {
        return customerId -> true;
    }
}
```
`true` creates only the external check; `false` or absent creates only allow-all. Complementary conditions avoid both "no bean" and "two beans" failures, and do not depend on evaluation order like `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` would in user configuration.
</details>

## 8. Lifecycle: warm a cache safely
`CategoryCache` must load categories from `CategoryRepository` at startup and log the count. A teammate wrote the loading code in the constructor using a field-injected repository and gets an NPE. Fix it two ways.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/catalog/CategoryCache.java
package com.orderhub.catalog;

import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class CategoryCache {
    private final CategoryRepository repository;
    private volatile List<String> categories = List.of();

    // Way 1: constructor injection, the dependency is available immediately
    public CategoryCache(CategoryRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    // Way 2: keep the constructor trivial and load in @PostConstruct,
    // which runs after ALL injection (also works with field injection)
    @PostConstruct
    void load() {
        categories = List.copyOf(repository.findAllNames());
        System.out.println("loaded " + categories.size() + " categories");
    }

    public List<String> all() { return categories; }
}
```
The NPE happened because field injection runs after the constructor. Either receive the repository in the constructor, or move the loading into `@PostConstruct`. If loading needs a transaction or another proxy feature, use an `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener instead, because proxies do not exist yet during `@PostConstruct`.
</details>

## 9. Send the email only if the order commits
This code sometimes emails customers for orders that were rolled back. Fix it.

```java
@Transactional
public Order place(OrderRequest req) {
    Order o = repository.save(Order.from(req));
    emailService.sendConfirmation(o);      // remote call inside the transaction
    stock.reserve(o);                       // may throw OutOfStockException
    return o;
}
```

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/OrderService.java (excerpt)
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.springframework.context.ApplicationEventPublisher;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository;
    private final StockService stock;
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository, StockService stock, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {
        this.repository = repository;
        this.stock = stock;
        this.events = events;
    }

    @Transactional
    public Order place(OrderRequest req) {
        Order o = repository.save(Order.from(req));
        stock.reserve(o);                                  // may throw: rollback
        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(o.id(), o.customerEmail(), o.total()));
        return o;
    }
}

// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/notification/ConfirmationListener.java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import com.orderhub.order.OrderPlacedEvent;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionalEventListener;

@Component
class ConfirmationListener {
    private final EmailService emailService;
    ConfirmationListener(EmailService emailService) { this.emailService = emailService; }

    @Async
    @TransactionalEventListener      // AFTER_COMMIT
    void onPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent e) {
        emailService.sendConfirmation(e.orderId(), e.customerEmail());
    }
}
```
The email now runs only after commit, on another thread, so a slow mail server does not hold the database transaction open either. If the JVM crashes between commit and email, the email is lost; the outbox pattern in Phase 20 closes that gap.
</details>

## 10. Profile-specific beans and a test
Write a `StorageService` with `LocalDiskStorage` for every profile except `prod`, and `S3Storage` for `prod`. Write a test proving the `test` profile gets local disk.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: src/main/java/com/orderhub/storage/StorageBeans.java
package com.orderhub.storage;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Profile;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

interface StorageService { String store(String name, byte[] data); }

@Component
@Profile("!prod")
class LocalDiskStorage implements StorageService {
    public String store(String name, byte[] data) { return "file:/tmp/orderhub/" + name; }
}

@Component
@Profile("prod")
class S3Storage implements StorageService {
    public String store(String name, byte[] data) { return "s3://orderhub-prod/" + name; }
}

// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/storage/StorageProfileTest.java
package com.orderhub.storage;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.ActiveProfiles;

@SpringBootTest
@ActiveProfiles("test")
class StorageProfileTest {
    @Autowired StorageService storage;

    @Test
    void testProfileUsesLocalDisk() {
        assertThat(storage.store("a.pdf", new byte[0])).startsWith("file:");
    }
}
```
Complementary expressions (`prod` and `!prod`) guarantee exactly one bean in every environment.
</details>

# Phase 10 review: Spring Core

## Cheat sheet

| Idea | Remember |
|---|---|
| IoC | The container creates and wires objects; your classes do not call `new` on collaborators |
| DI | Dependencies passed in; constructor injection preferred |
| Bean definition | Recipe (class, scope, deps, lazy, init, destroy); created before any object |
| BeanFactory vs ApplicationContext | Lazy and minimal vs eager singletons, events, Environment, i18n |
| refresh() | Definitions, BeanFactoryPostProcessors, register BeanPostProcessors, web server, singletons, ContextRefreshedEvent |
| Injection styles | Constructor (required, final), setter (optional), field (avoid) |
| Field injection timing | After the constructor, so fields are null inside it |
| Circular deps | Prohibited by default since Boot 2.6; refactor or use `@Lazy` parameter |
| Resolution order | Type, then `@Qualifier`, then `@Primary`, then `@Fallback`, then parameter name |
| All implementations | `List<T>` ordered by `@Order`, `Map<String, T>` keyed by bean name |
| Stereotypes | `@Repository` adds exception translation; `@Service` is semantic; `@RestController` = `@Controller` + `@ResponseBody` |
| Scan root | Package of the `@SpringBootApplication` class |
| `@Configuration` | CGLIB proxy so inter-bean calls return singletons; `proxyBeanMethods = false` disables it |
| Scopes | singleton (default), prototype, request, session, application, websocket |
| Prototype | No destroy callbacks; inside a singleton use `ObjectProvider` |
| Lifecycle | Constructor, injection, Aware, BPP before (`@PostConstruct`), afterPropertiesSet, initMethod, BPP after (proxy) |
| Destroy order | `@PreDestroy`, `DisposableBean.destroy`, destroyMethod |
| Profiles | `application-{profile}.yml`, `@Profile`, `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` |
| Precedence | Command line over env vars over profile files over base file |
| `@Value` vs `@ConfigurationProperties` | One value or SpEL vs typed, validated, relaxed-bound tree |
| Conditions | Evaluated at definition time; OnClass, OnProperty, OnMissingBean drive auto-config |
| Events | Sync by default; `@Async` for async; `@TransactionalEventListener` runs after commit |

## Key code snippets

```java
// Snippet: Spring imports (org.springframework.*) and surrounding classes omitted for brevity
// Constructor injection with an optional dependency
@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository repository;
    private final ObjectProvider<ProductEventsPublisher> publisher;

    public ProductService(ProductRepository repository, ObjectProvider<ProductEventsPublisher> publisher) {
        this.repository = repository;
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }
}
```

```java
// Qualifier vs primary, and a strategy list
@Component @Primary class CardGateway implements PaymentGateway { }
@Component("paypal") class PaypalGateway implements PaymentGateway { }

CheckoutService(PaymentGateway defaultGateway,
