### Compare
| Default actuator setup | Production setup |
|---|---|
| Only `/health` exposed, no details | Health details for ops, probes, readiness group |
| Same port as the API | Separate management port |
| No build information | `/info` with git commit and version |
| Log levels need a redeploy | `/loggers` changes levels at runtime |
| Sensitive endpoints unprotected if exposed | Security chain with `EndpointRequest` and `access: none` for heapdump |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is Spring Boot Actuator?**
   A module that adds production endpoints for health, info, metrics, loggers, environment, thread dumps and more, plus Micrometer metrics integration. It lets platforms and operators monitor and manage the app without custom code.
2. **Which endpoints are exposed by default?**
   Over HTTP only `health` (and it hides details from anonymous callers). Over JMX more are available. You opt in with `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include`.
3. **How do you write a custom health check?**
   Implement `HealthIndicator` (or `ReactiveHealthIndicator`) as a bean and return `Health.up()` or `Health.down()` with details. The bean name (minus the `HealthIndicator` suffix) becomes the component name.

**Follow-up**
4. **Why would you run actuator on a separate port?**
   So sensitive endpoints are not reachable through the public ingress or load balancer, and network policies can restrict who reaches them (only Prometheus and the cluster). It also isolates monitoring traffic from application traffic.
5. **What is the difference between the liveness and readiness health groups?**
   Liveness answers "is the process healthy or should it be restarted"; it should reflect internal state only. Readiness answers "can it serve traffic now"; it can include critical dependencies. Kubernetes restarts on failed liveness and removes from load balancing on failed readiness.
6. **Which actuator endpoints are dangerous to expose and why?**
   `heapdump` (memory contents including secrets and personal data), `env` and `configprops` (configuration values; Boot masks them by default since 3.0 but `show-values` can reveal them), `threaddump`, `beans`, `mappings` (reconnaissance), and `shutdown` (stops the app). Expose them only on the management port with authentication.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **All pods restarted in a loop when the database had a short outage. What was misconfigured?**
   The database health check was part of liveness (for example a custom probe on `/actuator/health` instead of `/actuator/health/liveness`). Liveness failed, Kubernetes killed all pods, and the restarts added load when the database came back. Use the liveness group (internal state only) for liveness and put the database in readiness.
8. **Your custom health indicator calls inventory's full `/actuator/health`, which calls its database and Kafka. What can go wrong?**
   Cascading health failures: one slow downstream marks every upstream as DOWN, possibly taking the whole system out of load balancing at once. Health checks also become slow and can time out the probe. Check dependencies cheaply (their liveness, a connection check), and only in readiness when the app truly cannot work without them.
9. **Prometheus scrapes `/actuator/prometheus` but the security chain returns 401. Options?**
   Permit the prometheus endpoint on the management port and restrict that port with a network policy, or give Prometheus basic-auth or bearer credentials in its scrape config. Do not disable security for all actuator endpoints to fix it.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you can configure Actuator safely for production: right exposure, separate port, meaningful health groups, and no dependency checks in liveness.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `exposure.include: "*"` on the public port | Heap dumps and config leaked | Explicit list, management port, security |
| Database check in liveness | Restart storms during DB outages | DB only in readiness |
| Expensive custom health checks | Slow probes, cascading DOWN | Cheap checks with timeouts, cache results |
| Using `/actuator/health` as the Kubernetes liveness URL | Every component affects restarts | `/actuator/health/liveness` |
| Showing health details to everyone | Reveals infrastructure | `show-details: when-authorized` |

### Best practices
- Add `build-info` and git info so you can always answer "which version is running?".
- Use `/actuator/loggers` for temporary DEBUG in production instead of redeploying, and revert it.
- Put business-relevant gauges (pending orders) into metrics as well as custom endpoints.
- Review `/actuator/conditions` when auto-configuration does not do what you expect.
- Treat custom `@WriteOperation` endpoints as admin APIs: authenticated, audited.

### Compare with
| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Actuator endpoints | Per-instance health, info, management operations |
| Micrometer + Prometheus | Time-series metrics across instances |
| Spring Boot Admin | UI on top of many apps' actuator endpoints |
| JMX / JConsole | Local JVM management, older tooling |
| APM agents (Datadog, New Relic) | Commercial all-in-one observability |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
Add a Kafka-lag-aware readiness indicator to notification-service: it is `OUT_OF_SERVICE` when the consumer group `notification-service` has more than 10,000 records of lag on `order-events`, so the platform and dashboards show that notifications are badly delayed, while liveness stays UP. Add it to the readiness group and expose it with details for the `OPS` role.

Acceptance criteria: `/actuator/health/readiness` returns 503 with lag details when lag exceeds the threshold; liveness stays 200; the check uses a cached value refreshed at most every 15 seconds.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
package com.orderhub.notification.health;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.AdminClient;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.ListOffsetsResult;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.OffsetSpec;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.OffsetAndMetadata;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Status;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaAdmin;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component("kafkaLag")
class KafkaLagHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {
    private static final long MAX_LAG = 10_000;
    private static final Duration CACHE = Duration.ofSeconds(15);

    private final AdminClient admin;
    private volatile Health cached = Health.unknown().build();
    private volatile Instant cachedAt = Instant.EPOCH;

    KafkaLagHealthIndicator(KafkaAdmin kafkaAdmin) {
        this.admin = AdminClient.create(kafkaAdmin.getConfigurationProperties());
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        if (Duration.between(cachedAt, Instant.now()).compareTo(CACHE) < 0) return cached;
        cached = compute();
        cachedAt = Instant.now();
        return cached;
    }

    private Health compute() {
        try {
            Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> committed = admin
                    .listConsumerGroupOffsets("notification-service")
                    .partitionsToOffsetAndMetadata().get();
            Map<TopicPartition, OffsetSpec> latestSpec = committed.keySet().stream()
                    .collect(Collectors.toMap(tp -> tp, tp -> OffsetSpec.latest()));
            Map<TopicPartition, ListOffsetsResult.ListOffsetsResultInfo> latest =
                    admin.listOffsets(latestSpec).all().get();

            long lag = committed.entrySet().stream()
                    .mapToLong(e -> latest.get(e.getKey()).offset() - e.getValue().offset())
                    .sum();
            Health.Builder builder = lag > MAX_LAG ? Health.status(Status.OUT_OF_SERVICE) : Health.up();
            return builder.withDetail("lag", lag).withDetail("threshold", MAX_LAG).build();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return Health.unknown().withDetail("error", e.getMessage()).build();
        }
    }
}
```

```yaml
management:
  endpoint:
    health:
      show-details: when-authorized
      roles: OPS
      group:
        readiness:
          include: readinessState,kafkaLag
```
`OUT_OF_SERVICE` maps to HTTP 503. Because the indicator is only in the readiness group, a lagging consumer is never restarted by the liveness probe (a restart would only cause a rebalance and more lag). For a consumer-only service, readiness mostly drives dashboards and alerts; consider whether removing it from a Service matters at all.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: Which Spring Boot Actuator endpoint is exposed over HTTP by default?
A: Only `health`, and without details for anonymous users.

Q: Why should a Kubernetes liveness probe not check the database in a Spring Boot app?
A: A database outage would fail liveness on every pod, causing restart storms that do not fix the database.

Q: How do you add a custom component to Spring Boot Actuator health?
A: Register a bean implementing `HealthIndicator` that returns `Health.up()` or `Health.down()` with details; the bean name becomes the component name.

Q: Why run Spring Boot Actuator on a separate management port?
A: So sensitive endpoints are not reachable through the public ingress and can be restricted by network policy.

Q: Which Spring Boot Actuator endpoint changes log levels at runtime?
A: `/actuator/loggers/{logger}` with a POST body like `{"configuredLevel":"DEBUG"}`.

**Related:** [[Graceful Shutdown and Health Probes]] · [[Metrics with Micrometer and Prometheus]] · [[Logging with SLF4J, Logback and MDC]]

# Logging with SLF4J, Logback and MDC

**Level: Advanced | Phase 21 | Priority: MUST | Since: SLF4J 1.0 (2005), Logback default in Spring Boot since 1.0; structured logging (ECS, Logstash, GELF) built into Boot 3.4**

> **30-second interview answer:** Application code logs through SLF4J, a facade, so the backend can change; Spring Boot uses Logback by default. I use parameterized messages (`log.info("Order {} placed", id)`), the right levels, and pass exceptions as the last argument so stack traces are kept. In production I log structured JSON (Boot 3.4 has `logging.structured.format.console: ecs` built in) so a log platform can index fields. MDC, a per-thread map, adds context like `orderId` and the trace id to every line of a request; it must be cleared after the request and propagated explicitly to other threads.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A ship's logbook kept by many crew members.

| Ship's logbook | Logging |
|---|---|
| Crew write in a standard format whatever pen they use | SLF4J facade, any backend (Logback, Log4j2) |
| Entry types: routine, warning, emergency | Levels: TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR |
| The voyage number stamped on every entry of that voyage | MDC context (traceId, orderId) |
| A pre-printed form with boxes instead of free text | Structured JSON logs |
| Captain decides which entry types are worth writing today | Logger level configuration |
| Old logbooks archived after the voyage | Rolling files, retention, log shipping |

### What it is
- **SLF4J (Simple Logging Facade for Java):** an API (`Logger`, `LoggerFactory`) with pluggable backends. Spring Boot routes JUL, Commons Logging and Log4j API calls into SLF4J too, so all libraries end up in one place.
- **Logback:** Boot's default backend. Configured with `application.yml` (`logging.level.*`, `logging.file.name`, `logging.pattern.*`) or `logback-spring.xml` (supports `<springProfile>` and `<springProperty>`).
- **Logger hierarchy:** loggers named by package (`com.orderhub.order`) inherit levels from parents; `logging.level.com.orderhub=DEBUG` affects all below it.
- **Appender:** where events go (console, rolling file, socket). In containers, log to stdout and let the platform collect.
- **Parameterized logging:** `{}` placeholders are formatted only if the level is enabled, avoiding string building for disabled levels.
- **MDC (Mapped Diagnostic Context):** a `ThreadLocal` map of key-value pairs that appenders add to every event from that thread.
- **Structured logging:** each event is a JSON object with fields (`@timestamp`, `log.level`, `message`, `traceId`, MDC keys), formats ECS (Elastic), Logstash or GELF.

### Why it exists
`System.out.println` has no levels, no timestamps, no context and cannot be turned off per package. Free-text logs from 20 service instances are unsearchable: you cannot filter "all lines for order 42" without context fields. SLF4J decouples code from the backend, and MDC plus structured output turn logs into queryable data.

## 2. How It Works
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant F as "MdcFilter"
    participant C as "OrderController"
    participant L as "Logger (SLF4J)"
    participant B as "Logback"
    participant A as "Console appender (JSON encoder)"
    F->>F: "MDC.put(orderhub.customerId, 7)"
    F->>C: "chain.doFilter"
    C->>L: "log.info(Order {} placed, 42)"
    L->>B: "level INFO enabled for com.orderhub.order?"
    B->>B: "Create ILoggingEvent: message, args, thread, MDC copy"
    B->>A: "Encode as ECS JSON with MDC fields and traceId"
    A-->>A: "Write line to stdout"
    F->>F: "finally: MDC.remove(keys)"
```

Key internals:
- The level check is the first thing Logback does; with a disabled level, `log.debug("x {}", expensive())` still evaluates `expensive()` (arguments are evaluated by Java before the call), so use `log.atDebug().addArgument(() -> expensive()).log("x {}")` (SLF4J 2 fluent API) or an `isDebugEnabled()` guard for truly expensive arguments.
- MDC is thread-local. Thread pools reuse threads, so a value not removed leaks into the next request handled by that thread. Work handed to another thread (`@Async`, `CompletableFuture`, executors) does not see the MDC unless you copy it (a `TaskDecorator`, or Boot's `ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator` with Micrometer context propagation).
- With Micrometer Tracing on the classpath, the current `traceId` and `spanId` are put into the MDC automatically and Boot's default pattern prints them (Boot 3.2+ correlation id).
- Structured logging (Boot 3.4+) replaces the text encoder with a JSON formatter; MDC entries and key-value pairs (`log.atInfo().addKeyValue("orderId", 42)`) become JSON fields.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
order-service logging setup: JSON in production, readable text locally, MDC filter, async propagation.

```yaml
# order-service application.yml
spring:
  application:
    name: order-service
logging:
  level:
    root: INFO
    com.orderhub: INFO
    org.hibernate.SQL: WARN               # set to DEBUG temporarily via /actuator/loggers
  structured:
    format:
      console: ecs                         # JSON on stdout (Boot 3.4+); remove for plain text
---
spring:
  config:
    activate:
      on-profile: local
logging:
  structured:
    format:
      console: ""                          # local dev: human-readable text
  level:
    com.orderhub: DEBUG
```

```java
// order-service: put request context into the MDC and always clean it up
package com.orderhub.order.logging;

import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import java.io.IOException;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.core.Ordered;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.filter.OncePerRequestFilter;

@Component
@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE + 10)
class MdcFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,
                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String correlationId = request.getHeader("X-Correlation-Id");
        String customerId = request.getHeader("X-Customer-Id");   // in real code: from the validated JWT
        try {
            if (correlationId != null) MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);
            if (customerId != null) MDC.put("customerId", customerId);
            MDC.put("httpMethod", request.getMethod());
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            MDC.remove("correlationId");                          // pooled threads: never leak context
            MDC.remove("customerId");
            MDC.remove("httpMethod");
        }
    }
}
```

```java
// order-service: business logging done right
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderLoggingExample {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderLoggingExample.class);
    private final AsyncReceiptService receipts;

    public OrderLoggingExample(AsyncReceiptService receipts) { this.receipts = receipts; }

    public void place(long orderId, int lines, String cardNumber) {
        try (MDC.MDCCloseable ignored = MDC.putCloseable("orderId", String.valueOf(orderId))) {
            log.info("Placing order with {} lines", lines);                      // parameterized
            log.atInfo()                                                       // SLF4J 2 fluent API
               .addKeyValue("lineCount", lines)
               .log("Order validated");
            log.debug("Card ending {}", mask(cardNumber));                     // never log full PAN
            try {
                receipts.sendReceipt(orderId);
            } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
                log.error("Receipt failed for order {}", orderId, e);          // exception LAST: stack trace kept
            }
        }                                                                      // orderId removed here
    }

    private static String mask(String card) {
        return card.substring(card.length() - 4);
    }
}
```

```java
// order-service: MDC and trace context follow work onto @Async threads
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.boot.task.ThreadPoolTaskExecutorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.core.task.support.ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableAsync;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
class AsyncConfig {
    @Bean
    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor applicationTaskExecutor(ThreadPoolTaskExecutorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.threadNamePrefix("async-")
                .taskDecorator(new ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator())   // copies MDC + observation context
                .build();
    }
}

@Service
class AsyncReceiptService {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(AsyncReceiptService.class);

    @Async
    public void sendReceipt(long orderId) {
        log.info("Sending receipt");                  // still has orderId, customerId, traceId in MDC
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- `logging.structured.format.console: ecs` switches console output to Elastic Common Schema JSON, one object per line, ready for Loki, Elasticsearch or Cloud Logging. The `local` profile keeps readable text for developers.
- `MdcFilter` adds request-scoped keys and removes them in `finally`. Without the cleanup, Tomcat's next request on the same thread would log the previous customer's id: a correctness and privacy bug.
- `MDC.putCloseable` with try-with-resources scopes `orderId` to the block, which is neat for service-level context.
- `log.atInfo().addKeyValue("lineCount", lines)` adds a structured field without putting it in the message text; in JSON output it becomes `"lineCount": 3`.
- The card number is masked before logging. Personal and payment data in logs is a compliance problem (PCI DSS, GDPR).
- `log.error("Receipt failed for order {}", orderId, e)`: SLF4J treats a trailing `Throwable` without a matching placeholder as the exception, so the stack trace is printed.
- `ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator` (Spring 6.1) uses Micrometer context propagation to copy MDC values and the current trace into the `@Async` thread and restore the thread afterwards.

### Output
Production (ECS JSON, one object per line):

```json
{"@timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:15:02.318Z","log.level":"INFO","process.pid":1,"process.thread.name":"http-nio-8081-exec-3","service.name":"order-service","log.logger":"com.orderhub.order.OrderLoggingExample","message":"Placing order with 3 lines","traceId":"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","spanId":"00f067aa0ba902b7","correlationId":"3f2b8c1e","customerId":"7","httpMethod":"POST","orderId":"42","ecs":{"version":"8.11"}}
{"@timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:15:02.320Z","log.level":"INFO","process.pid":1,"process.thread.name":"http-nio-8081-exec-3","service.name":"order-service","log.logger":"com.orderhub.order.OrderLoggingExample","message":"Order validated","lineCount":3,"traceId":"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","spanId":"00f067aa0ba902b7","correlationId":"3f2b8c1e","customerId":"7","httpMethod":"POST","orderId":"42","ecs":{"version":"8.11"}}
{"@timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:15:02.325Z","log.level":"INFO","process.pid":1,"process.thread.name":"async-1","service.name":"order-service","log.logger":"com.orderhub.order.AsyncReceiptService","message":"Sending receipt","traceId":"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","spanId":"00f067aa0ba902b7","correlationId":"3f2b8c1e","customerId":"7","httpMethod":"POST","orderId":"42","ecs":{"version":"8.11"}}
```

Local profile (Boot default text pattern with correlation):

```text
2026-11-24T09:15:02.318Z  INFO 18244 --- [order-service] [nio-8081-exec-3] [4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7] c.o.order.OrderLoggingExample : Placing order with 3 lines
2026-11-24T09:15:02.319Z DEBUG 18244 --- [order-service] [nio-8081-exec-3] [4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7] c.o.order.OrderLoggingExample : Card ending 4242
```

A query in the log platform such as `orderId:42` (Elasticsearch) now returns every line about that order, on every thread and, with the trace id, in every service.

### Compare
| Bad | Good | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `log.info("Order " + id + " placed")` | `log.info("Order {} placed", id)` | No string building when the level is off |
| `log.error(e.getMessage())` | `log.error("Placing order {} failed", id, e)` | Keeps the stack trace and context |
| `e.printStackTrace()` | `log.error("Receipt failed", e)` | Goes through levels, format and appenders |
| Log and rethrow at every layer | Log once where handled | Avoids duplicate stack traces |
| Free text "user 7 order 42" | JSON fields `customerId`, `orderId` | Searchable and aggregatable |
| Log to files inside containers | Log to stdout | Platform collects, no disk filling |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **Why use SLF4J instead of Logback or Log4j directly?**
   SLF4J is a facade: code depends on a stable API while the backend is chosen at deployment. Libraries can log without forcing a backend, and Boot bridges other logging APIs into it so everything ends up in one configuration.
2. **What is MDC?**
   Mapped Diagnostic Context: a per-thread map of key-value pairs that the logging backend attaches to every event from that thread. It is used for request ids, trace ids, user ids and order ids, so related lines can be found together.
3. **How do you change log levels in Spring Boot?**
   With `logging.level.<package>=LEVEL` in configuration, per profile, or at runtime through `POST /actuator/loggers/<logger>` without restarting.

**Follow-up**
4. **Why are parameterized messages better than string concatenation?**
   The message is formatted only if the level is enabled, saving CPU and garbage for disabled DEBUG logs. The arguments themselves are still evaluated, so for expensive computations use a level guard or the fluent API with a supplier.
5. **What happens to MDC with thread pools and `@Async`?**
   MDC is thread-local. Values are not copied to the worker thread, so async logs lose context, and values not removed stay on pooled threads and leak into unrelated work. Copy context with a `TaskDecorator` (`ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator`) and always clean up in `finally`.
6. **Why structured logs?**
   JSON fields can be indexed, filtered and aggregated reliably (`level:ERROR AND service:order-service AND orderId:42`), without fragile regex parsing of text. Boot 3.4 produces ECS, Logstash or GELF JSON natively.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **Production logs show customer A's id on requests made by customer B. Explain.**
   A filter put the customer id in the MDC but did not remove it in `finally`, or an exception skipped the cleanup. The pooled Tomcat thread kept the value and the next request on that thread logged it. Always remove keys in `finally` or use `MDC.putCloseable`.
8. **After enabling DEBUG for Hibernate in production, latency doubled and the disk filled. What do you do and how do you avoid it?**
   Revert the level immediately via `/actuator/loggers`. Log volume costs I/O, CPU and storage; DEBUG for SQL with bind parameters can also leak personal data. Use targeted loggers for a short time, sampling, or tracing spans instead, and log to stdout with platform retention limits.
9. **A Logback async appender is configured and some logs are missing after a crash. Why?**
   `AsyncAppender` buffers events in a queue and by default discards TRACE, DEBUG and INFO events when the queue is 80% full; on a hard crash the queue is lost. Accept it for throughput, set `neverBlock` and `discardingThreshold` consciously, and make sure the shutdown hook flushes (Boot registers a logging shutdown hook).

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether your logs are useful during an incident (context, structure, levels) and safe (no leaks between requests, no secrets, no performance damage).

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Not clearing MDC | Context leaks into other requests | Remove in `finally` or `putCloseable` |
| Logging passwords, tokens, card numbers | Compliance breach, credential theft | Mask, never log secrets |
| Losing the stack trace | Cannot debug | Exception as the last argument |
| Logging the same exception at every layer | Noise, duplicate alerts | Log once at the handling boundary |
| Many DEBUG lines left on in production | Cost and latency | INFO by default, temporary DEBUG via actuator |
| Mixing SLF4J and a second logging config | Duplicate or missing logs | One backend, Boot bridges the rest |

### Best practices
- Log events that matter for operations: state changes, external calls failing, decisions (rejected order and why).
- Use consistent field names across services (`orderId`, `customerId`, `traceId`) so queries work everywhere.
- Log to stdout in JSON in containers; let Fluent Bit or the OTel collector ship logs.
- Include the trace id (automatic with Micrometer Tracing) and link logs from traces in Grafana.
- Set retention and sampling for noisy logs; alert on ERROR rate, not on individual lines.

### Compare with
| Backend | Notes |
|---|---|
| Logback | Boot default, mature, `logback-spring.xml` profile support |
| Log4j2 | Async loggers with LMAX Disruptor, very fast; use `spring-boot-starter-log4j2` and exclude Logback |
| java.util.logging | JDK built-in, limited; bridged into SLF4J by Boot |
| OpenTelemetry logs | Export logs via OTLP alongside traces and metrics |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
Add Kafka consumer context to notification-service logs: every log line produced while handling a record must contain `kafkaTopic`, `kafkaPartition`, `kafkaOffset` and `orderId` (the record key) in the MDC, and the keys must be removed afterwards even when the listener throws. Use a `RecordInterceptor` so listeners stay clean.

Acceptance criteria: JSON logs from the listener include the four fields; a log line from the next record never shows the previous record's offset.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
package com.orderhub.notification.logging;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.Consumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.RecordInterceptor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
class KafkaMdcInterceptor implements RecordInterceptor<String, String> {

    @Override
    public ConsumerRecord<String, String> intercept(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record,
                                                    Consumer<String, String> consumer) {
        MDC.put("kafkaTopic", record.topic());
        MDC.put("kafkaPartition", String.valueOf(record.partition()));
        MDC.put("kafkaOffset", String.valueOf(record.offset()));
        if (record.key() != null) MDC.put("orderId", record.key());
        return record;                                    // returning null would skip the record
    }

    @Override
    public void afterRecord(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record, Consumer<String, String> consumer) {
        MDC.remove("kafkaTopic");                         // called after success AND failure
        MDC.remove("kafkaPartition");
        MDC.remove("kafkaOffset");
        MDC.remove("orderId");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.orderhub.notification.logging;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.kafka.ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.kafka.config.ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.ConsumerFactory;

@Configuration
class KafkaLoggingConfig {
    @Bean
    ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<Object, Object> kafkaListenerContainerFactory(
            ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer configurer,
            ConsumerFactory<Object, Object> consumerFactory,
            KafkaMdcInterceptor interceptor) {
        var factory = new ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<Object, Object>();
        configurer.configure(factory, consumerFactory);      // keep Boot's settings (error handler, observation)
        @SuppressWarnings({"unchecked", "rawtypes"})
        var raw = (org.springframework.kafka.listener.RecordInterceptor) interceptor;
        factory.setRecordInterceptor(raw);
        return factory;
    }
}
```
`afterRecord` runs whether the listener succeeded or threw, so no offset leaks into the next record's lines. With Micrometer observation enabled on the listener, `traceId` is already in the MDC as well, continuing the producer's trace.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What is the role of SLF4J in Spring Boot logging?
A: It is the logging facade the code uses; Logback is the default backend, and Boot bridges other logging APIs into SLF4J.

Q: What is MDC in Java logging?
A: Mapped Diagnostic Context: a thread-local map whose key-value pairs are added to every log event from that thread.

Q: Why must MDC values be removed in a `finally` block in web filters?
A: Server threads are pooled, so leftover values leak into later requests handled by the same thread.

Q: How do you enable structured JSON logging in Spring Boot 3.4+?
A: Set `logging.structured.format.console` (or `.file`) to `ecs`, `logstash` or `gelf`.

Q: How do you keep the stack trace when logging an exception with SLF4J?
A: Pass the exception as the last argument, without a placeholder: `log.error("Failed for {}", id, e)`.

**Related:** [[Distributed Tracing with OpenTelemetry]] · [[Spring Boot Actuator]] · [[Metrics with Micrometer and Prometheus]]

# Coding drill: Production incidents

Ten incidents of the kind senior interviews love ("it is 3 a.m., what do you do?"). For each: read the symptoms, decide which signal you would check first and what you expect to see, then write the fix. The method is always the same: **detect** (alert on a symptom), **locate** (metrics, then traces to find the service and operation), **explain** (logs, dumps), **mitigate** (rollback, scale, feature flag), **fix** (code or config), **prevent** (test, alert, guardrail).

## 1. Pods restart with exit code 137, no OutOfMemoryError in the logs
order-service pods on Kubernetes restart every few hours. `kubectl describe pod` shows `Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137`. Container limit 1 GiB; JVM started with `-Xmx1g`.

Hint: the kernel killed the container, not the JVM; heap is only part of process memory.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: `-Xmx1g` lets the heap alone use the whole limit; metaspace, thread stacks (200 Tomcat threads), code cache, direct buffers (Kafka, Netty) and GC structures come on top, so total RSS crosses 1 GiB and the OOM killer sends SIGKILL. Check `container_memory_working_set_bytes` against the limit and `jvm_memory_used_bytes{area="nonheap"}`; enable `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary` and run `jcmd 1 VM.native_memory summary` in a pod.

```yaml
# k8s container fix
          env:
            - name: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS
              value: >-
                -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70
                -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=192m
                -XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize=128m
                -Xss512k
                -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError
          resources:
            requests: { memory: "1Gi", cpu: "500m" }
            limits:   { memory: "1Gi" }
```

```yaml
# application.yml: fewer threads if the service is I/O-bound
server:
  tomcat:
    threads:
      max: 100
spring:
  threads:
    virtual:
      enabled: true          # Java 21: request handling on virtual threads, tiny stacks
```
Heap is now about 700 MiB with explicit caps for the rest. Prevention: alert when working set exceeds 90% of the limit, and load-test with production limits.
</details>

## 2. Requests hang, then 500 "Connection is not available, request timed out after 30000ms"
Under a traffic peak, order-service returns 500s after exactly 30 seconds. Metrics: `hikaricp_connections_active` flat at 10 (pool max), `hikaricp_connections_pending` 150. Traces show long spans for `http post inventory-service` inside `orderhub.checkout`.

Hint: what holds a connection while waiting for the network?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: `@Transactional` on the checkout method opens a connection at the start and holds it during the 2-second inventory HTTP call. With 10 connections, throughput is capped at about 5 checkouts per second; everything else waits `connectionTimeout` (30 s) and fails. Hikari leak detection confirms long holds.

```yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    hikari:
      maximum-pool-size: 20
      connection-timeout: 3000          # fail fast instead of 30 s queues
      leak-detection-threshold: 2000    # logs a stack trace when a connection is held > 2 s
```

```java
package com.orderhub.order;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;

@Service
public class CheckoutFix {
    private final OrderRepository orders;
    private final ResilientInventory inventory;
    private final TransactionTemplate tx;

    public CheckoutFix(OrderRepository orders, ResilientInventory inventory, TransactionTemplate tx) {
        this.orders = orders;
        this.inventory = inventory;
        this.tx = tx;
    }

    // No @Transactional here: the network call happens with no connection held
    public Order checkout(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order order = tx.execute(s -> orders.save(Order.pending(cmd)));       // short tx 1
        inventory.reserve(order.getId(), cmd.lines());                         // network, no connection
        return tx.execute(s -> {                                               // short tx 2
            Order o = orders.findById(order.getId()).orElseThrow();
            o.confirm();
            return o;
        });
    }
}
```
Also check `spring.jpa.open-in-view`: if true (the default), the session and its connection stay open for the whole request when lazy loading occurs; set it to false. Prevention: alert on `hikaricp_connections_pending > 0` for 2 minutes.
</details>

## 3. Everything slows down when inventory is slow
Inventory's p99 went from 100 ms to 8 s during its database maintenance. Within minutes order-service and the gateway time out on all endpoints, including `/api/products` which never calls inventory.

Hint: look at Tomcat thread metrics and a thread dump.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: `tomcat_threads_busy_threads` hits the maximum; `GET /actuator/threaddump` shows 200 threads in `SocketInputStream.read` inside the inventory client. No read timeout and no bulkhead, so the slow dependency consumed every request thread and unrelated endpoints starved (cascading failure).

```java
package com.orderhub.order.client;

import java.time.Duration;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.web.client.RestClientBuilderConfigurer;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.loadbalancer.LoadBalanced;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.client.SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClient;

@Configuration
class TimeoutFixConfig {
    @Bean
    @LoadBalanced
    RestClient.Builder lbBuilder(RestClientBuilderConfigurer configurer) {
        var factory = new SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory();
        factory.setConnectTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(500));
        factory.setReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1));       // inventory p99 is 100 ms normally
        return configurer.configure(RestClient.builder()).requestFactory(factory);
    }
}
```

```yaml
resilience4j:
  bulkhead:
    instances:
      inventory:
        max-concurrent-calls: 25       # at most 25 of 200 threads may wait on inventory
        max-wait-duration: 0
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      inventory:
        slow-call-duration-threshold: 800ms
        slow-call-rate-threshold: 50
        minimum-number-of-calls: 20
        wait-duration-in-open-state: 15s
```
With `@Bulkhead` and `@CircuitBreaker` on the inventory gateway (Phase 20), slow inventory now affects only the checkout path, which fails fast with 503, while products keep working.
</details>

## 4. Heap grows until the pod dies every two days
`jvm_memory_used_bytes{area="heap"}` after each full GC climbs steadily (a sawtooth whose minimum rises). GC time increases; finally the JVM exits with `OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space` thanks to `ExitOnOutOfMemoryError`.

Hint: take a heap dump and look at the dominator tree.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: a heap dump (`jcmd 1 GC.heap_dump /tmp/heap.hprof` in the pod, or `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/tmp`) opened in Eclipse MAT shows a `ConcurrentHashMap` in `ProductPriceCache` retaining 1.4 GB: an unbounded "cache" keyed by product id plus currency plus customer group, never evicted.

```java
package com.orderhub.order.pricing;

import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.binder.cache.CaffeineCacheMetrics;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.function.Function;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ProductPriceCache {
    record Key(long productId, String currency, String customerGroup) { }

    private final Cache<Key, BigDecimal> cache;

    public ProductPriceCache(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(50_000)                       // bounded
                .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(10))  // stale prices age out
                .recordStats()
                .build();
        CaffeineCacheMetrics.monitor(registry, cache, "productPrices");   // size, hits, evictions
    }

    public BigDecimal price(long productId, String currency, String group, Function<Key, BigDecimal> loader) {
        return cache.get(new Key(productId, currency, group), loader);
    }
}
```
Never expose `/actuator/heapdump` publicly (it contains personal data); take dumps through `kubectl exec` with care and delete them afterwards. Prevention: every cache must be bounded and have metrics.
</details>

## 5. Async logs lose the trace id
In Grafana, the logs of `@Async` receipt sending and of `CompletableFuture` price lookups have empty `traceId` fields, so support cannot follow requests.

Hint: thread locals do not follow work onto other threads.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
package com.orderhub.order.async;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.Executor;
import org.springframework.boot.task.ThreadPoolTaskExecutorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.core.task.support.ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Configuration
class PropagationConfig {
    // Name applicationTaskExecutor: used by @Async and replaces Boot's default executor
    @Bean
    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor applicationTaskExecutor(ThreadPoolTaskExecutorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.threadNamePrefix("app-")
                .taskDecorator(new ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator())
                .build();
    }
}

@Service
class PriceLookups {
    private final Executor executor;

    PriceLookups(ThreadPoolTaskExecutor applicationTaskExecutor) { this.executor = applicationTaskExecutor; }

    CompletableFuture<Long> priceAsync(long productId) {
        // pass the decorated executor; the common ForkJoinPool would lose the context
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 1250L, executor);
    }
}
```
`ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator` captures MDC and the current observation (span) when the task is submitted and restores them on the worker thread. Also check Kafka (`observation-enabled`) and clients built without Boot's builders.
</details>

## 6. Prometheus runs out of memory after a release
Right after release 1.4.30, Prometheus memory doubles and dashboards time out. The release added a custom metric for order lookups.

Hint: count series per metric name.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

PromQL `topk(5, count by (__name__) ({__name__=~".+"}))` shows `orders_lookup_seconds_bucket` with 2.3 million series. The code tagged the timer with the raw path:

```java
// the bug
Timer.builder("orders.lookup").tag("path", request.getRequestURI()).register(registry);  // /api/orders/918273
```

Fix in code plus a safety net:

```java
package com.orderhub.order.metrics;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.config.MeterFilter;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
class CardinalityGuards {
    // Drop any "path" tag and cap distinct "uri" values as a last line of defence
    @Bean
    MeterFilter dropPathTag() {
        return MeterFilter.ignoreTags("path");
    }

    @Bean
    MeterFilter capUriTag() {
        return MeterFilter.maximumAllowableTags("http.server.requests", "uri", 200, MeterFilter.deny());
    }
}
```
Use the route template (`/api/orders/{id}`), which Spring's own `http.server.requests` already does. Put order ids in traces and logs, not metrics.
</details>

## 7. A burst of 502s during every deployment
Each rollout of order-service produces 200 to 400 `502 Bad Gateway` at the ingress over about 5 seconds, although `server.shutdown=graceful` is set.

Hint: compare when the pod stops accepting connections with when the ingress stops sending them.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: endpoint removal and SIGTERM happen in parallel. Tomcat stops accepting immediately on SIGTERM, while ingress-nginx and kube-proxy still route to the pod for a few seconds.

```yaml
    spec:
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 60
      containers:
        - name: order-service
          lifecycle:
            preStop:
              sleep:
                seconds: 10          # keep serving while routing converges
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 9081 }
            periodSeconds: 5
  strategy:
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 0
      maxSurge: 1
```

```yaml
# application.yml
server:
  shutdown: graceful
spring:
  lifecycle:
    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 30s   # 10 + 30 + cleanup < 60 grace period
```
Verify with a load test (`k6` or `hey`) running during `kubectl rollout restart`: zero non-2xx responses expected.
</details>

## 8. All pods restart together during a database blip
A 40-second PostgreSQL failover caused every order-service pod to restart, and the service was down for 4 minutes instead of 40 seconds.

Hint: what does the liveness probe check?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: the liveness probe pointed at `/actuator/health`, which includes the `db` indicator. The database outage failed liveness, Kubernetes restarted all pods, and cold starts plus startup probes extended the outage.

```yaml
# application.yml
management:
  endpoint:
    health:
      probes:
        enabled: true
      group:
        liveness:
          include: livenessState              # internal state only
        readiness:
          include: readinessState,db          # DB affects traffic, never restarts
```

```yaml
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 9081 }
            periodSeconds: 10
            failureThreshold: 3
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 9081 }
            periodSeconds: 5
            failureThreshold: 2
```
Also tune Hikari so the app recovers by itself after failover (`spring.datasource.hikari.max-lifetime` below the database's idle cutoff, short `connection-timeout`) and keep retries with backoff in the Kafka consumers.
</details>

## 9. Kafka consumer lag grows and records are processed several times
notification-service lag on `order-events` climbs to 500,000. Logs show repeated "Revoking previously assigned partitions" and the same order ids being emailed several times.

Hint: compare batch processing time with `max.poll.interval.ms`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Diagnosis: each poll returns 500 records; each email takes about 800 ms (slow SMTP), so a batch takes 400 s, longer than `max.poll.interval.ms` (300 s). The broker evicts the consumer, rebalances, and the uncommitted batch is redelivered, forever.

```yaml
spring:
  kafka:
    consumer:
      properties:
        max.poll.records: 50              # 50 x 0.8 s = 40 s per poll
        max.poll.interval.ms: 300000
    listener:
      ack-mode: record                    # commit progress after each record
      concurrency: 6                      # one thread per partition
```

```java
package com.orderhub.notification;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.springframework.dao.DuplicateKeyException;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.simple.JdbcClient;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
class IdempotentEmailListener {
    private final JdbcClient jdbc;
    private final EmailSender email;

    IdempotentEmailListener(JdbcClient jdbc, EmailSender email) {
        this.jdbc = jdbc;
        this.email = email;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "order-events", groupId = "notification-service")
    void on(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        String eventId = new String(record.headers().lastHeader("eventId").value());
        try {
            jdbc.sql("insert into processed_message(consumer, message_id) values ('email', ?)")
                .param(eventId).update();
        } catch (DuplicateKeyException duplicate) {
            return;                                   // redelivered after a rebalance
        }
        email.send(Long.parseLong(record.key()), "Order update");
    }
}
```
Longer term, send emails asynchronously from a bounded pool or batch them with the SMTP provider's API, and alert on consumer lag growth rate.
</details>

## 10. Need DEBUG logs from one pod without a redeploy
Customers report intermittent wrong totals for orders with discounts. You suspect `com.orderhub.order.pricing` but INFO logs show nothing. You need DEBUG for 10 minutes, only for that package, with minimal risk.

Hint: Actuator can change levels at runtime; find a failing request by its trace id.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```bash
# 1. Find a failing request: the customer gave the X-Trace-Id from the response
#    Grafana Tempo or Jaeger: search trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 -> pod order-service-6c8d7f9b5d-tx5lm

# 2. Port-forward the management port of that pod (not exposed publicly)
kubectl port-forward pod/order-service-6c8d7f9b5d-tx5lm 9081:9081 -n orderhub

# 3. Raise the level for one package only
curl -u ops:"$OPS_PASSWORD" -X POST localhost:9081/actuator/loggers/com.orderhub.order.pricing \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"configuredLevel":"DEBUG"}'

# 4. Reproduce, then query logs: traceId plus logger
#    {app="order-service"} | json | log_logger=~"com.orderhub.order.pricing.*" | log_level="DEBUG"

# 5. Reset to the configured default
curl -u ops:"$OPS_PASSWORD" -X POST localhost:9081/actuator/loggers/com.orderhub.order.pricing \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"configuredLevel":null}'
```

```java
package com.orderhub.order.pricing;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
class DiscountCalculator {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(DiscountCalculator.class);

    BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal total, BigDecimal percent) {
        BigDecimal discount = total.multiply(percent).divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(100), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
        log.atDebug()
           .addKeyValue("total", total)
           .addKeyValue("percent", percent)
           .addKeyValue("discount", discount)
           .log("Discount applied");                  // structured fields, cheap when DEBUG is off
        return total.subtract(discount);
    }
}
```
The DEBUG output revealed a `double`-based percentage parsed upstream (`0.1 + 0.2` style rounding), fixed by using `BigDecimal` end to end. Level changes via `/actuator/loggers` affect only that pod and vanish on restart, which limits risk; reset explicitly anyway.
</details>

# Spring WebFlux vs Spring MVC

**Level: Advanced | Phase 22 | Priority: SHOULD | Since: Spring MVC 2.5 annotations (2007), WebFlux in Spring Framework 5.0 (2017)**

> **30-second interview answer:** Spring MVC is built on the Servlet API and uses one thread per request, which blocks while waiting for I/O. Spring WebFlux is a non-blocking stack, usually on Netty, where a few event-loop threads handle many connections and controllers return `Mono` or `Flux`. WebFlux shines for high-concurrency, I/O-heavy work such as gateways, streaming and fan-out to many services, but only if the whole chain is non-blocking. For typical CRUD with JDBC and JPA, Spring MVC (now with virtual threads) is simpler and just as fast.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A bank with tellers versus a bank with a ticket machine.

| Bank | Spring |
|---|---|
| One teller per customer who waits while the vault is opened | Spring MVC: one servlet thread per request, blocked on I/O |
| Bank runs out of tellers, customers queue outside | Tomcat thread pool exhausted (200 threads by default) |
| Ticket machine: a few clerks take requests and are notified when the vault is ready | WebFlux event loop on Netty |
| A clerk who personally waits at the vault | A blocking call on an event loop: it freezes everyone behind it |
| Same counter forms in both banks | Same `@RestController` annotations in both stacks |

### What it is
- **Spring MVC:** the web framework on the Servlet API (`spring-boot-starter-web`, Tomcat by default). Request handling runs on a container thread from start to finish.
- **Spring WebFlux:** the reactive web framework (`spring-boot-starter-webflux`, Reactor Netty by default). It can also run on Tomcat or Jetty in non-blocking mode through the Servlet 3.1+ async API.
- **Event loop:** a thread that runs a loop selecting ready network events (using `epoll` or NIO selectors) and dispatching callbacks. Netty creates about one event-loop thread per CPU core (`reactor-http-nio-N`).
- **Two programming models in WebFlux:** annotated controllers (`@RestController` returning `Mono` or `Flux`) and functional endpoints (`RouterFunction` plus `HandlerFunction`).
- **`WebClient`:** the non-blocking HTTP client from WebFlux. It is also usable from Spring MVC apps; `RestClient` is the blocking modern client.

### Why it exists
Around 2015 microservices made backends call many other services per request. With thread-per-request, threads spend most of their life waiting on sockets. Netty-style servers showed that a few threads can handle tens of thousands of connections. Spring added WebFlux so Spring developers could get that model with familiar annotations, plus first-class streaming (server-sent events, WebSocket, NDJSON).

## 2. How It Works

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph MVC["Spring MVC on Tomcat"]
        R1["Request 1"] --> T1["http-nio-8080-exec-1 (blocked on DB 50 ms)"]
        R2["Request 2"] --> T2["http-nio-8080-exec-2 (blocked on HTTP 300 ms)"]
        R3["Request 201"] --> Q["Queued: all 200 threads busy"]
    end
    subgraph WF["Spring WebFlux on Netty"]
        S1["Request 1"] --> EL["reactor-http-nio-1 (event loop)"]
        S2["Request 2"] --> EL
        S3["Request 10000"] --> EL
        EL -->|"register callback, return immediately"| IO["Non-blocking I/O: WebClient, R2DBC"]
        IO -->|"data ready signal"| EL
    end
```

**Request path in WebFlux**

```text
Netty channel read
  -> HttpHandler (adapter from Reactor Netty to Spring)
  -> WebHttpHandlerBuilder chain: WebFilter(s) (security, CORS, tracing)
  -> DispatcherHandler                (the reactive DispatcherServlet)
       HandlerMapping   -> finds @GetMapping method or RouterFunction
       HandlerAdapter   -> invokes it, gets Mono<ResponseEntity<...>> or Flux<T>
       HandlerResultHandler -> subscribes and encodes with Jackson, writes to the channel
  <- response bytes written as each element arrives (streaming)
```

What Spring does behind the annotations is the same idea as MVC (mapping, argument resolution, message conversion), but every component returns a publisher instead of a value, and the framework does the subscribing.

**Side by side**

| Aspect | Spring MVC | Spring WebFlux |
|---|---|---|
| API base | Servlet API (blocking) | Reactive Streams |
| Default server | Tomcat | Reactor Netty |
| Threads | Pool of ~200 request threads | ~1 event loop per core |
| Return types | `T`, `ResponseEntity<T>` | `Mono<T>`, `Flux<T>`, also plain `T` |
| Data access | JDBC, JPA, `JdbcClient` | R2DBC, reactive MongoDB, reactive Redis |
| Security | `SecurityFilterChain`, `SecurityContextHolder` (ThreadLocal) | `SecurityWebFilterChain`, `ReactiveSecurityContextHolder` (Reactor Context) |
| Testing | `MockMvc` | `WebTestClient` |
| Filters | `Filter`, `HandlerInterceptor` | `WebFilter` |
| Streaming | `SseEmitter`, `StreamingResponseBody` | `Flux` with `text/event-stream` or NDJSON |

**You cannot have both as the active web stack.** If both starters are on the classpath, Spring Boot picks MVC. Force WebFlux with `spring.main.web-application-type=reactive`. Using `WebClient` inside an MVC app is fine and common.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
The same OrderHub "order summary" endpoint in both stacks. It loads an order and calls a slow shipping service.

```xml
<!-- pom.xml (reactive version) -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webflux</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.projectreactor</groupId>
    <artifactId>reactor-test</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

```yaml
# application.yml
server:
  port: 8080
orderhub:
  shipping-base-url: http://localhost:9090
logging:
  level:
    reactor.netty.http.client: info
```

```java
// File: com/orderhub/summary/OrderSummary.java
package com.orderhub.summary;

public record OrderSummary(long orderId, String customer, String shippingStatus) { }
```

```java
// File: com/orderhub/summary/ReactiveOrderSummaryController.java  (WebFlux, annotated)
package com.orderhub.summary;

import java.time.Duration;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@RestController
public class ReactiveOrderSummaryController {

    private final WebClient shipping;
    private final ReactiveOrderRepository orders;   // R2DBC repository, see Backpressure and R2DBC

    public ReactiveOrderSummaryController(WebClient.Builder builder,
                                          @Value("${orderhub.shipping-base-url}") String baseUrl,
                                          ReactiveOrderRepository orders) {
        this.shipping = builder.baseUrl(baseUrl).build();
        this.orders = orders;
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/orders/{id}/summary")
    public Mono<ResponseEntity<OrderSummary>> summary(@PathVariable long id) {
        Mono<String> status = shipping.get().uri("/shipments/{id}/status", id)
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
                .onErrorReturn("UNKNOWN");               // degrade, do not fail

        return orders.findById(id)                        // Mono<OrderRow>, empty if missing
                .zipWith(status, (o, s) -> new OrderSummary(o.id(), o.customerName(), s))
                .map(ResponseEntity::ok)
                .defaultIfEmpty(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
    }

    // Streaming: each order is written to the socket as soon as it is read
    @GetMapping(value = "/api/orders/stream", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_NDJSON_VALUE)
    public Flux<OrderRow> stream() {
        return orders.findAll();
    }
}
```

```java
// File: com/orderhub/summary/OrderSummaryRouter.java  (WebFlux, functional style)
package com.orderhub.summary;

import static org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RequestPredicates.GET;
import static org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RouterFunctions.route;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RouterFunction;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.ServerResponse;

@Configuration
public class OrderSummaryRouter {

    @Bean
    RouterFunction<ServerResponse> healthRoutes() {
        return route(GET("/api/ping"), request -> ServerResponse.ok().bodyValue("pong"));
    }
}
```

```java
// File: com/orderhub/summary/MvcOrderSummaryController.java  (Spring MVC equivalent, blocking)
package com.orderhub.summary;

import java.util.Optional;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClient;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestClientException;

@RestController
public class MvcOrderSummaryController {

    private final RestClient shipping;
    private final OrderJpaRepository orders;

    public MvcOrderSummaryController(RestClient.Builder builder, OrderJpaRepository orders) {
        this.shipping = builder.baseUrl("http://localhost:9090").build();
        this.orders = orders;
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/orders/{id}/summary")
    public ResponseEntity<OrderSummary> summary(@PathVariable long id) {
        Optional<OrderEntity> order = orders.findById(id);          // thread waits for the DB
        if (order.isEmpty()) return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        String status;
        try {
            status = shipping.get().uri("/shipments/{id}/status", id)
                    .retrieve().body(String.class);                // thread waits for HTTP
        } catch (RestClientException e) {
            status = "UNKNOWN";
        }
        OrderEntity o = order.get();
        return ResponseEntity.ok(new OrderSummary(o.getId(), o.getCustomerName(), status));
    }
}
```

```java
// File: src/test/java/com/orderhub/summary/OrderSummaryRouterTest.java
package com.orderhub.summary;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.test.web.reactive.server.WebTestClient;

class OrderSummaryRouterTest {

    @Test
    void ping() {
        WebTestClient client = WebTestClient
                .bindToRouterFunction(new OrderSummaryRouter().healthRoutes())
                .build();
        client.get().uri("/api/ping").exchange()
              .expectStatus().isOk()
              .expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("pong");
    }
}
```

### Walkthrough
- **pom and yml:** only `spring-boot-starter-webflux` is present, so Boot starts Reactor Netty. `reactor-test` gives `StepVerifier`.
- **Reactive controller:** the method returns immediately with a `Mono` describing the work. The order lookup and the shipping call run concurrently because `zipWith` subscribes to both. `timeout` and `onErrorReturn` make the slow dependency non-fatal. `defaultIfEmpty` turns "order not found" (an empty `Mono`) into 404.
- **NDJSON streaming:** with `application/x-ndjson`, WebFlux writes each `OrderRow` as one JSON line as soon as R2DBC emits it, so the client starts receiving before the query finishes and memory stays flat.
- **Functional router:** the same stack without annotations; useful for small, explicit route tables and fully programmatic setups.
- **MVC controller:** the same logic written top-down. It is easier to read and debug, and the DB and HTTP calls run one after another (you would need `CompletableFuture` or structured concurrency to run them concurrently).
- **WebTestClient:** the reactive equivalent of MockMvc; it can bind to a router, a controller or a running server.

### Output
```http
GET /api/orders/42/summary HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8080

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"orderId":42,"customer":"Asha Rahman","shippingStatus":"IN_TRANSIT"}
```

```http
GET /api/orders/stream HTTP/1.1
Accept: application/x-ndjson

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/x-ndjson
transfer-encoding: chunked

{"id":41,"customerName":"Lee Chen","status":"PAID"}
{"id":42,"customerName":"Asha Rahman","status":"SHIPPED"}
```

When the shipping service is down, the first request still returns 200 with `"shippingStatus":"UNKNOWN"` after at most 2 seconds.

### Compare
| Situation | Pick | Why |
|---|---|---|
| CRUD service on PostgreSQL with JPA | MVC (plus virtual threads) | JPA is blocking; WebFlux would add complexity for no gain |
| API gateway proxying thousands of slow connections | WebFlux | Spring Cloud Gateway is built on it; connections are cheap |
| Server-sent events to 50,000 browsers | WebFlux | Long-lived connections without a thread each |
| Team new to reactive, tight deadline | MVC | Debuggability and hiring |
| Fan-out to 10 services per request | Either: WebFlux, or MVC with virtual threads and structured concurrency | Both avoid wasting platform threads |

## 4. Interview Questions
**Basic**
1. **What is the main difference between Spring MVC and Spring WebFlux?**
   MVC uses the blocking Servlet API with a thread per request; WebFlux is non-blocking, built on Reactive Streams, and uses a small number of event-loop threads. WebFlux controllers return `Mono` or `Flux`, and the framework subscribes and writes the response asynchronously. The annotations look the same, but the execution model is completely different.
2. **Which server does WebFlux use by default?**
   Reactor Netty. It can also run on Tomcat, Jetty or Undertow (Boot 3) using the Servlet non-blocking I/O API, but Netty is the natural fit and the default in `spring-boot-starter-webflux`.
3. **Can you use `WebClient` in a Spring MVC application?**
   Yes. You add `spring-boot-starter-webflux` for the client, and Boot still runs MVC because MVC wins when both are present. In MVC, though, `RestClient` (Spring 6.1) is usually simpler unless you need streaming or many concurrent calls.

**Follow-up**
4. **Is WebFlux faster than MVC?**
   Not per request: a single request is often slightly slower because of the reactive overhead. WebFlux gives better scalability under high concurrency with I/O waits, meaning stable latency and lower memory with tens of thousands of concurrent connections. For CPU-bound work it gives no advantage at all.
5. **What happens if you call a JDBC repository from a WebFlux controller?**
   The JDBC call blocks the Netty event-loop thread, so every other connection on that loop waits too. With 8 event loops, 8 slow queries can freeze the whole service. Either use R2DBC, or wrap the call with `Mono.fromCallable(...).subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())` and accept that you are back to a thread pool.
6. **How does security differ in WebFlux?**
   You configure a `SecurityWebFilterChain` with `ServerHttpSecurity`, and the security context lives in the Reactor `Context` (`ReactiveSecurityContextHolder`) instead of a `ThreadLocal`. Method security uses `@EnableReactiveMethodSecurity`, and the secured methods must return `Mono` or `Flux`.

**Tricky / scenario**
7. **Your team migrated a JPA-based service to WebFlux and throughput dropped. Why?**
   JPA is blocking, so they either blocked event loops (catastrophic) or wrapped every call in `boundedElastic` (a thread pool with extra overhead). They paid the reactive complexity cost without getting non-blocking I/O. The fix is to go back to MVC with virtual threads, or to move fully to R2DBC.
8. **When would you still choose WebFlux in 2026, now that virtual threads exist?**
   For streaming (SSE, WebSocket, NDJSON), for backpressure between producer and consumer, for gateways and proxies (Spring Cloud Gateway), and when the team already uses a reactive data stack. For request-response CRUD, virtual threads give similar scalability with simpler code.
9. **How do you test a WebFlux controller?**
   With `@WebFluxTest(Controller.class)` plus `WebTestClient`, mocking collaborators with `@MockitoBean`. For pipelines below the controller, use `StepVerifier` and virtual time instead of `block()`.

**What the interviewer is really testing:** whether you choose a stack from requirements (I/O pattern, data access, team skills), not from hype, and whether you know the blocking-call trap.

## 5. Pitfalls & Best Practices
### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What goes wrong | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Both starters added "just in case" | App silently runs MVC; reactive code still works but blocking assumptions differ | Pick one; set `spring.main.web-application-type` explicitly |
| Blocking call on the event loop | Whole service stalls under load | Non-blocking client or `boundedElastic` |
| `ThreadLocal` used for tenant or user id | Value missing after a thread hop | Reactor `Context` and context propagation |
| Returning `Flux<T>` to a client expecting a JSON array, then switching to NDJSON | Client breaks | Choose the media type deliberately and document it |
| No timeouts on `WebClient` | Hung connections pile up invisibly | Response timeout on the `HttpClient` plus `timeout()` on the chain |

### Best practices
- Default to MVC for business services; choose WebFlux for gateways, streaming and fully reactive stacks.
- Configure `WebClient` once as a bean with connection pool, connect timeout and response timeout on the Reactor Netty `HttpClient`.
- Run BlockHound in integration tests of WebFlux services.
- Keep reactive types at the edges you need them; do not leak `Mono` into domain objects.

### Compare with
| Feature | Spring MVC | Spring MVC + virtual threads | Spring WebFlux |
|---|---|---|---|
| Code style | Imperative | Imperative | Functional, declarative |
| Concurrency limit | Thread pool size | Memory and downstream limits | Memory and downstream limits |
| JDBC and JPA | Yes | Yes | No (blocking) |
| Streaming and backpressure | Limited | Limited | Native |
| Stack traces | Clear | Clear | Fragmented |

## 6. Hands-on Exercise
Add a live order-status feed to OrderHub's order service. Create `GET /api/orders/{id}/events` producing `text/event-stream` that emits the current status, then a new event every time the status changes, and finishes when the status becomes `DELIVERED`. Acceptance criteria: uses `Flux` and `ServerSentEvent`; each event has `id`, `event` name `status` and the status as data; a `WebTestClient` test reads the first two events. Files: `OrderEventsController.java`, `OrderStatusPublisher.java`, `OrderEventsControllerTest.java`.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```java
// File: order-service/src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/events/OrderStatusPublisher.java
package com.orderhub.order.events;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Sinks;

@Component
public class OrderStatusPublisher {

    // One replaying sink per order: late subscribers get the latest status first
    private final Map<Long, Sinks.Many<String>> sinks = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void publish(long orderId, String status) {
        Sinks.Many<String> sink = sinks.computeIfAbsent(orderId, id -> Sinks.many().replay().latest());
        sink.tryEmitNext(status);
        if ("DELIVERED".equals(status)) {
            sink.tryEmitComplete();
            sinks.remove(orderId);
        }
    }

    public Flux<String> statuses(long orderId) {
        return sinks.computeIfAbsent(orderId, id -> Sinks.many().replay().latest()).asFlux();
    }
}
```

```java
// File: order-service/src/main/java/com/orderhub/order/events/OrderEventsController.java
package com.orderhub.order.events;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.http.codec.ServerSentEvent;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;

@RestController
public class OrderEventsController {

    private final OrderStatusPublisher publisher;

    public OrderEventsController(OrderStatusPublisher publisher) {
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }

    @GetMapping(value = "/api/orders/{id}/events", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
    public Flux<ServerSentEvent<String>> events(@PathVariable long id) {
        AtomicLong seq = new AtomicLong();
        return publisher.statuses(id)
                .map(status -> ServerSentEvent.<String>builder()
                        .id(String.valueOf(seq.incrementAndGet()))
                        .event("status")
                        .data(status)
                        .build());
    }
}
```

```java
// File: order-service/src/test/java/com/orderhub/order/events/OrderEventsControllerTest.java
package com.orderhub.order.events;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.core.ParameterizedTypeReference;
import org.springframework.http.codec.ServerSentEvent;
import org.springframework.test.web.reactive.server.WebTestClient;
import reactor.test.StepVerifier;

class OrderEventsControllerTest {

    @Test
    void streamsStatusChanges() {
        OrderStatusPublisher publisher = new OrderStatusPublisher();
        publisher.publish(7L, "PAID");
        WebTestClient client = WebTestClient.bindToController(new OrderEventsController(publisher)).build();

        var body = client.get().uri("/api/orders/7/events").exchange()
                .expectStatus().isOk()
                .returnResult(new ParameterizedTypeReference<ServerSentEvent<String>>() { })
                .getResponseBody();

        StepVerifier.create(body.map(ServerSentEvent::data))
                .expectNext("PAID")
                .then(() -> publisher.publish(7L, "SHIPPED"))
                .expectNext("SHIPPED")
                .then(() -> publisher.publish(7L, "DELIVERED"))
                .expectNext("DELIVERED")
                .verifyComplete();
    }
}
```
`Sinks.many().replay().latest()` is a hot publisher that remembers the last value, so a browser that connects late immediately sees the current status. Completing the sink ends the HTTP stream cleanly.
</details>

## 7. Recall
Q: What is the core difference between Spring MVC and Spring WebFlux?
A: MVC is blocking with a thread per request on the Servlet API; WebFlux is non-blocking on Reactive Streams with a few event-loop threads.

Q: Which server and test client does Spring WebFlux use by default?
A: Reactor Netty as the server and `WebTestClient` for tests.

Q: What happens if both spring-boot-starter-web and spring-boot-starter-webflux are on the classpath?
A: Spring Boot runs Spring MVC; WebFlux classes such as `WebClient` are still usable.

Q: Why is calling JPA from a Spring WebFlux controller a problem?
A: JPA blocks the event-loop thread, which stalls every connection served by that loop.

Q: When is Spring WebFlux a better choice than Spring MVC?
A: For gateways, streaming (SSE, WebSocket, NDJSON), backpressure and fully non-blocking stacks with many concurrent slow connections.

**Related:** [[Reactive Programming and Project Reactor]] · [[Virtual Threads vs Reactive]] · [[DispatcherServlet Request Lifecycle]] · [[API Gateway with Spring Cloud Gateway]]

# Backpressure and R2DBC

**Level: Advanced | Phase 22 | Priority: NICE | Since: Reactive Streams 1.0 (2015), R2DBC 1.0 (2022), Spring Data R2DBC 1.0 (2019)**

> **30-second interview answer:** Backpressure is a consumer telling a producer how much it can handle: in Reactive Streams the subscriber calls `request(n)` and the publisher must never send more. When a source cannot slow down (like user clicks or a hot Kafka feed), you pick an overflow strategy: buffer, drop, keep latest or error. R2DBC is the reactive replacement for JDBC, so database rows flow as a `Flux` without blocking threads, and backpressure reaches all the way to the database cursor. It has no lazy loading or JPA-style relationships, so you model aggregates explicitly.

## 1. Concept in Simple Words
### Analogy
A water tap filling buckets carried by a person.

| Water and buckets | Backpressure |
|---|---|
| The tap | Publisher |
| The person carrying buckets | Subscriber |
| "Give me two buckets' worth now" | `request(2)` |
| A tap you can turn down | A cold source that respects demand (database cursor, `Flux.range`) |
| Rain you cannot turn off | A hot source (UI events, a sensor) |
| A big barrel to catch extra rain | `onBackpressureBuffer` |
| Letting extra rain run off | `onBackpressureDrop` |
| Only keeping the latest bucket | `onBackpressureLatest` |
| Shouting "too much water!" | `onBackpressureError` |

R2DBC is a well that only pumps water when the person asks for a bucket, instead of a pump that fills the whole yard (JDBC loading a full result set while a thread waits).

### What it is
- **Demand:** the number of items a subscriber has requested and not yet received. `request(Long.MAX_VALUE)` means unbounded (no backpressure); the simple `subscribe(lambda)` does exactly that.
- **Prefetch:** many operators (`flatMap`, `publishOn`, `concatMap`) request a batch ahead (often 256 or 32) and replenish at 75 percent consumption. `limitRate(n)` sets this explicitly.
- **Overflow strategy:** what to do when a source emits faster than demand and cannot be paused.
- **R2DBC (Reactive Relational Database Connectivity):** an SPI specification for non-blocking SQL drivers (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, Oracle, H2). Rows are emitted as a `Publisher`.
- **Spring Data R2DBC:** repositories (`ReactiveCrudRepository`), `R2dbcEntityTemplate` and `DatabaseClient` on top of R2DBC. It is a simple object mapper, not an ORM: no lazy loading, no dirty checking, no `@OneToMany`.

### Why it exists
Without backpressure, a fast producer either overwhelms memory (unbounded queues and `OutOfMemoryError`) or forces blocking. Reactive Streams made flow control part of the protocol. But a reactive web layer over JDBC still blocks at the database, which breaks the model; R2DBC closes that gap so a WebFlux service can be non-blocking end to end.

## 2. How It Works

**Demand flows up, data flows down**

```text
Subscriber            limitRate(4)                     Source (DB cursor / Flux.range)
    | request(MAX) ->     | request(4) ----------------->   |
    |                     |   <- onNext 1,2,3 ------------- |
    |   <- onNext 1,2,3 - |  (75% of 4 consumed)            |
    |                     | request(3) ----------------->   |  replenish
    |                     |   <- onNext 4,5,6 ------------- |
```

**Overflow strategies for hot sources**

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    H["Hot source emits 1000 per second"] --> D{"Consumer demand 10 per second"}
    D -->|"onBackpressureBuffer(500)"| B["Queue up to 500, then error"]
    D -->|"onBackpressureDrop"| X["Discard extras"]
    D -->|"onBackpressureLatest"| L["Keep only the newest"]
    D -->|"onBackpressureError"| E["Fail with OverflowException"]
```

**How R2DBC executes a query**

```text
repository.findByStatus("PAID")                 assembly: nothing sent
  WebFlux subscribes, requests N
    -> ConnectionFactory (r2dbc-pool) hands out a connection, non-blocking
    -> driver writes the query bytes on a Netty channel, returns immediately
    -> rows arrive from PostgreSQL in chunks; driver decodes each into a Row
    -> Spring Data maps Row -> OrderRow (record) and calls onNext
    -> when demand is exhausted the driver stops reading from the socket;
       TCP flow control slows the database (true end-to-end backpressure,
       driver-dependent: the PostgreSQL driver uses cursors/fetch size for this)
    -> onComplete, connection returned to the pool
```

**Transactions in R2DBC.** There is no `ThreadLocal`, so the transaction is bound to the Reactor `Context`. Spring Boot auto-configures an `R2dbcTransactionManager` (a `ReactiveTransactionManager`), and `@Transactional` works on methods that return `Mono` or `Flux`: the transaction starts on subscription and commits on completion. `TransactionalOperator` is the programmatic version.

**What you give up compared with JPA.** No entity relationships or lazy loading (load children with a second query and combine with `zip` or `collectList`), no persistence context or dirty checking (call `save` explicitly), and Flyway and Liquibase still need a JDBC driver for migrations at startup.

## 3. Code Example
### Code
Part A: backpressure mechanics with plain Reactor.

```java
// Dependency: org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-webflux (brings io.projectreactor:reactor-core)
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import org.reactivestreams.Subscription;
import reactor.core.publisher.BaseSubscriber;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;

public class BackpressureDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. A subscriber that asks for 2 items at a time
        Flux.range(1, 5)
            .doOnRequest(n -> System.out.println("request(" + n + ")"))
            .subscribe(new BaseSubscriber<Integer>() {
                private int received;

                @Override
                protected void hookOnSubscribe(Subscription subscription) {
                    request(2);                       // initial demand
                }

                @Override
                protected void hookOnNext(Integer value) {
                    System.out.println("onNext " + value);
                    received++;
                    if (received % 2 == 0) {
                        request(2);                   // ask for the next batch
                    }
                }

                @Override
                protected void hookOnComplete() {
                    System.out.println("complete");
                }
            });

        // 2. limitRate: the operator re-requests in batches for you
        Flux.range(1, 10)
            .doOnRequest(n -> System.out.println("upstream request(" + n + ")"))
            .limitRate(4)
            .subscribe();

        // 3. Overflow strategy: drop items the slow consumer did not ask for
        List<Integer> kept = new ArrayList<>();
        Flux.range(1, 10)
            .onBackpressureDrop(dropped -> System.out.println("dropped " + dropped))
            .subscribe(new BaseSubscriber<Integer>() {
                @Override
                protected void hookOnSubscribe(Subscription subscription) {
                    request(3);                       // consumer only ever wants 3
                }

                @Override
                protected void hookOnNext(Integer value) {
                    kept.add(value);
                }
            });
        System.out.println("kept " + kept);
    }
}
```

